A Republican Strategy to Constrain Obama's re-election
The Republican leaders justified their policy because of the need to combat electoral fraud.
However, the Brennan Center sees the latter as a myth and states that voter fraud is less frequent in the United States than the number of people who are killed by lightning.
In fact, Republican lawyers have found only 300 cases of voter fraud in the U.S. in 10 years.
One thing is certain: these new provisions will adversely affect the rate of participation.
In that regard, these measures will partly undermine the American democratic system.
Contrary to what's going on in Canada, American states are responsible for organizing federal elections in the U.S.
And along the same lines a majority of the American governments enacted, starting in 2009, new laws that made it harder for the enrollment or voting process.
This phenomenon has widened following the November 2010 elections, which saw an increase of 675 new Republican representatives in 26 states.
As a result, 180 bills restricting the exercise of the right to vote were introduced in 41 states during 2011.
The new electoral laws require voters to present an identity document with a photograph, in addition to an American citizenship test.
On the other hand, these laws reduce periods of early voting, invalidate the right to register as an elector on the day of the ballot and withdraw their right to vote for citizens with a criminal record.
Prior to the 2006 election, no U.S. state required electors to submit a photo ID.
The State of Indiana was the first to demand it.
The U.S. Supreme Court upheld the constitutionality of the Indiana law in 2008.
Therefore, the Republican authorities rushed to extend this practice to other states.
In recent years these authorities have supported bills in 34 states to force voters to submit a photo ID.
It is important to bear in mind that, contrary to what is happening in Quebec, American citizens do not have a universal identity document, such as the Social Security card.
In fact, 11 per cent of U.S. citizens, or 21 million people of voter age, do not have any photographic identity documents issued by their state's government agency.
On the other hand, five million new electors in 2012 do not have such an identity document.
Often garnishing the required identity card costs more than a hundred dollars.
Thus, the new restrictions disproportionately affect young people, minorities and low-income people.
Indeed, 25 per cent African-American, 15 per cent of people earning less than $35,000 a year, 18 per cent of citizens over the age of 65 and 20 per cent of voters between the ages of 18 and 29 don't have a photo ID.
There's even more.
In many states the students, voters who most vote for Democratic nominees, can't use the photo ID issued by their institution.
On the other hand, those same states authorize the members of the fishing or hunting clubs who usually vote for Republican parties to use the documents issued by such clubs.
Prior to 2004, neither state required proof of citizenship to vote.
Arizona was the first state to present such a requirement.
Since 2011, a dozen states have adopted laws that require electors to demonstrate that they are American citizens.
These measures clearly seek to limit the Hispanic vote.
It seems that two out of every three Hispanic voters support the Democratic party.
On the other hand, Republican legislators in 2011 supported certain laws abolishing voter enrollment on the same day of polling in eight states.
In addition, they limited the right of individuals and groups to set up an aid service for electors who wanted to enrol.
These restrictions have consequences.
For example, in the 2004 general elections, voter registration campaigns contributed to the registration of about 10 million citizens.
The measures taken since 2009 resulted in the registration rate of new electors falling in 2010 compared to 2006.
In addition, Republican lawmakers adopted legislation in five other states that favored reducing the voting period in advance.
For example, in the 2008 Florida election, 33 per cent of voters who voted in advance were African-American, while the latter accounted for only 13 per cent of the state's voters.
Exactly the same thing happened in the case of Hispanics.
These represent only 11 per cent of voters, equivalent to 24 per cent of citizens who vote in advance.
However, whites, who make up 76 per cent of the electorate, represent only 46 per cent of citizens who vote in advance.
Of course, Democratic lawmakers and their supporters have fervently opposed the adoption of legislation restricting voter registration.
In this sense, the Democratic governors have blocked several bills.
The U.S. Attorney's Office intervened to derogate the most controversial laws.
In part it was possible to limit the damage.
For example, only 16 out of 34 states passed the law that required the presentation of a photo ID.
Undeniable, however, it is undeniable that the newly adopted rules will complicate the exercise of the right to vote in 2012.
Democratic critics denounce the partisan nature of the laws that have been vetoed, as it is clear that they aim to influence the results of 2012 in the key states.
A 2011 Brennan Center report shows that the states that have adopted such laws represent 171 of the 270 votes needed in the electoral college to win the presidency.
It's too too early to say with certainty whether these electoral system legislative changes will have a significant impact on the results of the 2012 presidential elections.
One thing is certain: these new provisions will adversely affect the rate of participation.
In this sense, the measures will partly undermine the American democratic system.
