Prevent the disease
Unfortunately, there's no miraculous recipe to prevent cancer.
Despite the progress of research, adopting healthy living habits remains the best way to reduce the risks of developing the disease.
We're estimating that if everyone ate well and moved enough, 30 per per cent of cancers could be prevented.
"If no one smoked, that rate would rise to at least 50 per cent," says André Beaulieu, spokesman for the Canadian Cancer Society.
On the other hand, we assess that about 10 per cent of cancers are inherited.
plenty of others remain totally inexplicable.
According to the Cancer Canadian Cancer Society, anti-smoking is still a priority despite a decline in the number of smokers.
Smoking is linked to 85 per cent of lung cancer cases.
It also constitutes a risk factor for other types of cancers.
It greatly harms people's health.
"Today, there are still 1.5 million smokers in Quebec," spokesman André Beaulieu said.
A hopeful fact: 10 years after quitting smoking, the risk of dying from cancer is halved.
Weight
Thickness and obesity also favor the onset of the disease, according to the SCC.
They increase the risks of breast, colon and rectum, esophagus, pancreatic and uterine cancer.
"Researchs show that regular lifelong practice of physical activity protects against breast cancer," they add.
Feeding
The organism also recommends limiting the consumption of red flesh.
In large numbers, they scale the risks of colorectal cancer.
charcuterie products should also be avoided.
Preservation of the flesh for its smoking, drying or salting can result in the formation of carcinogenic substances.
"They can damage the cells of the organism and lead to the development of the cancer," they explain.
Vitamins
In recent years, several scientists have studied the links between vitamin complexes and cancer.
At the moment, however, their investigations are inconclusive at the moment.
According to SCC, studies about vitamin E are contradictory.
While in one of them there was a decrease in the risks of prostate cancer, in another what was observed was, rather, an increase.
The effect of Vitamin D on the cancer has also not been clearly determined either.
On the other hand, M. Beaulieu insists on the importance of talking about everyone's concerns and family history with his or her doctor.
"Making the screening test doesn't cause cancer".
The Higgs bosom in detail
Announcement of the Higgs bosbon's likely discovery sparked great shock last summer, and rightly so.
It is indeed believed that this boson is part of the mechanism responsible for the mass of everything that exists in the Universe, nothing else and nothing else.
What's more, it's the particle whose existence predated the standard model: our best or "least bad" explanation of the nature and behavior of matter, which has not yet been empirically observed.
For physicists, it's not entirely clear it's about Higgs.
Undoubtedly we know that there is a new particle and that it looks a lot like the Higgs boson predicted by the standard model.
In addition, new data unveiled this week at a major physic physics congress in Kyoto seems to confirm that it is, although more data are still needed to be completely secure.
Either way, we suppose it's about Higgs, because the chances of making a mistake seem smile, we'll see what it's about.
There is, in this great world, a widespread and inescapable law that implies that two things cannot be found in the same place at the same time at the same time.
There's no way to violate that rule and I don't try too hard, because it's going to end up hurting each other.
Although particle physics is a very complex world, it has been shown that there is also such a law in it: Pauli's principle of exclusion, which stipulates that two particles cannot occupy the same space at the same time if they are in the same "quantum state"; a "state" that consists, grossly, of some of its characteristics.
physicists classify particles into two categories.
On the one the one hand we find the good citizens called Fermions, who obey wisely at the beginning of Pauli.
And on the other there are the bosses, a dirty panda of anarchists who have no respect for anything, or at least not for this principle, which means that they can be found in the same place and at the same time.
According to the website The Particle Adventure of the Berkeley Laboratories which, by the way, it is extraordinary, the bosses are divided into two groups: the inns, of which we won't talk here, and the "particles of strength," by which the great forces of nature propagate and with which the Higgs boson would somehow be related.
These bosses, it might be emphasized here, aren't the exotic bugs we might believe.
In fact, if he manages to read this chronic, it is thanks to an extraordinarily banal boson: the photon or the "light particle" that acts as a "messenger" of electromagnetic force.
In fact, when a particle with an electrical charge accelerates or changes direction, it "disguises" the electromagnetic field in that precise environment; a little as when we threw a stone into a pond.
An electromagnetic wave is born in this "disorder" (of the light, of the infrared, of the ultraviolets, etc.), and this wave is nothing other than a photon; that is, one of the bosses "strengtheners."
Sparker field
It's the same with the Higgs bosom, with the difference that it's about another field, the Higgs pitch, which needs to be "disguised" for this bosom to appear.
However, the Higgs field is much more stable than the electromagnetic field. To activate it you must reach extremely high energies, a little as if it were an icy pond in which only a thick rock could slide through the surface.
That's why you need an immense particle accelerator such as CERN (the great hadron collider is a ring of 27 km of circumference) to reach such energy levels.
The analogy with the electromagnetic field is a new tool that explains the relationship between Higgs and mass.
In fact, not all particles or materials interact with the electromagnetic field.
Some, such as magnets, do so, but others do not: a piece of paper, for example, will never keep itself glued to a fridge's door.
And in the same way, not all particles interact with the Higgs field: those who do have a dough, while the rest (like the photon, by the way), don't have it.
"Certain all these investigations?" asks Mrs Plamondon.
For science, this serves to verify the validity of the standard model (MS), and also allows physicists to scrutinize the differences between MS observations and predictions.
On the other hand, many fervently want them to be found, as the slightest difference could open a door to a "new physics" that cover certain holes of the model.
This, it must be said, still has enormous shortages, such as that it does not propose any explanation relating to gravity (ups) or dark matter, which makes up about 80 per cent of the material of the Universe.
But we haven't found such differences in CERN so far.
Repercussions
Expulsions of such research in our day-to-day lives are difficult to predict, but we don't think we're wrong to say it won't have any.
To be seen: in the early 1960s, laser pioneers in lab labs didn't suspect at all the revolution their jobs would provoke.
The scientific applications existed, but nothing else.
In fact, as physic physicist Willard Boyle, who worked at the Bell Labs, where the laser was invented in the 1960s and who produced the first continuous laser (the first were pulsed) in 1962 said, at first the laser was rather considered a "lab box."
Imagine...
In addition, the applications can also come from all the instrumentation that surrounds the research.
For example, Willard Boyle himself developed a light catcher in 1969 in the course of his op-optics research.
Even though it wasn't his original intention at all, this collector now serves as an "eye" for every digital gadget in the world, which earned him the physic physics laureate in 2009.
That doesn't mean, of course, that LHC activities necessarily want to transform our lives, but you don't ever know...
