Fifty-three per cent of patients who are admitted to the Victor-Gadboisarrivent centre come from their homes and 47 per cent from the hospital.
Conveniability of palliative care
Seventy-seven per cent of Canadians simply do not have access to palliative care, i.e. care designed to alleviate suffering when a sick person arrives at the terminal stage of his or her life, either at home, in the hospital or in a care facility.
And several organizations, such as the Victor-Gadbois Center and the Home Palliative Care Society of the Grand Montréal, specialize almost exclusively in the care of cancer patients.
It's precisely this great relative lack in Quebec health care that causes many doctors specializing in palliative care the adoption of a law regulating euthanasia and assisted suicide.
Since the month of October, a manifesto signed by palliative care eminences, such as Dr. Balfour Mount and Dr. Bernard Lapointe, has been circulating to demonstrate their opposition to such an initiative.
According to Dr. Christiane Martel, Québec's health system is not competent enough to ensure that anyone is entitled to quality palliative care before euthanasia is agreed.
"Rescientally," he says, "I've watched a patient spend 14 days in the ER suffering great pain without what's been necessary to relieve it.
I'm afraid that patients want to die just because they don't get the right care.'
At the same time, many oncologists stubborn with their patients until the last day, despite the worst prognoses.
The survival hopes of Helene Richard were already low when he left a grueling chemotherapy treatment.
"When I told my oncologist that I wanted to stop the treatment, she told me I was very sorry that she wanted to stop fighting," she says.
"However, she had told me that there was nothing else to do".
There's no almighty caring.
Dr. Martel believes that 90 per cent of patients who claim to die are thanking their caregivers for failing to meet them in their demands once they have been relieved of their grief thanks to a palliative care team.
But it has to be said that palliative care isn't absolutely almighty when it comes to the treatment of pain.
According to Elsie Monereau, director of palliative Home Care at Grand Montréal, patients don't respond to pain treatments in 8 per cent of cases.
At the end of their lives, doctors usually resort to palliative sedation, which is used to sleep the patient until the time of his death, either sporadically or on a permanent basis.
We don't pretend we don't understand that part of suffering.
Increasingly, unrelieved disease will have the possibility of having that palliative sedation.
Sickers unrelieved say the same thing: "I want to die."
But that doesn't necessarily mean "I love euthanasia," but "I want to be relieved."
