A Permanent Anti-Corruption Unit Created in 2011
The Permanent Anti-Corruption Unit, created in 2011, is also being implemented with its team of interveners, Investigators and government analysts.
This unit also works from the "Marteau Squad Police," who, since 2009, have caused Montreal's "sewerage cartel" to be moderated when it comes to inflating contracts.
In recent recent weeks, the unit has carried out a serial search of fraud and corruption charges against municipal charges such as Frank Zampino and Richard Marcotte, the mayor of a city on the outskirts.
Next to the list would be Gilles Vaillancourt, who has just resigned from his position as mayor of Laval, Quebec's 3rd city.
Repeated bribes are suspected in exchange for public contracts.
Montreal road network engineers have been formally charged and Italian-born entrepreneurs such as Tony Accurso and Lino Zambito.
The latter has caused a commotion by explaining to the commission the mechanics of the "system" of obtaining public contracts.
He himself paid for three years 3% of the value of the contracts obtained in Montreal to an intermediary linked to the mafia that transferred the money to Union Montréal, Mayor Gérald Tremblay's party.
M. Zambito distributed money right-footed during the 2000s, awarding more than 88,000 Canadian dollars (about 68,000 euros) to provincial parties, especially the liberals in power.
He also admitted that he had organized a collection of illegal funds for former Liberal prime minister Nathalie Normandeau.
Inconpensation over the cost of sewerage contracts
In Montreal, the "system of corruption" functioned effectively.
Gilles Surprenant, a former public works engineer, explained it perfectly before the commission: in ten years he received gifts, travel invitations, golf tournaments, restaurants, hockey games and bribes from construction companies totalling $736,000, all in exchange in exchange for sewerage contracts whose costs floated.
Other road network officials have admitted that they received money for inflating bills between 30% and 40 per cent for fake extras.
In addition, an organizer of the mayor's party, Martin Dumont, accused M. Tremblay of deliberately turning a blind eye to a parallel budget and filled his coffers with dirty money.
In the wake of these revelations, Mr. Tremblay resigned in early November and led Montreal to a huge crisis.
Chantal Rouleau was one of Montreal's first elected policies to sound the alarm.
As mayor of the district of Riviare-des-Prairies, in the east of the island, since 2010, she has opposed the sale of municipal land purchased for 5 million dollars and resold for $1.6 million to developers in the real estate boom.
