With Bitcoin, pay and sell without banks
As opposed to the current, central bank-based monetary exchanges, identified transactions and processing charges between the parties.
In addition, as is often the case in technologies, the political vision is palpable: the conviction that the current monetary system, made up of banking monopolies, leads to financial crises.
In fact, Bitcoin, invented by Satoshi Nakamoto (a pseudonym), is at the same time a virtual currency convertible to dollars, euros, etc. and a secure exchange protocol in the manner of BitTorrent, which allows the exchange of files of the same.
Nearly 200,000 transactions have been recorded through 15,000 network computers.
And about a thousand websites accept bitcoins as donations or as a form of payment.
bitcoin's listing, having peaked at $US30 (23 euros) in June 2011, fell to $2 five months later, before returning today to around $10 (contions are dined on the page bitcoincharts.com).
It's not awesome if we compare it to global changes in real currency or in financial products.
The European Central Bank (ECB), however, became interested in it in a report on virtual currencies published in October.
He described bitcoin as "the most successful "virtuate virtual currency," "which competes with the dollar or the euro" and "similar to conventional currencies."
Bitcoin differs from other systems with types of virtual coins such as "credits," used to advance in a video game and that can be gained by playing or can be bought (and sometimes even redeem).
The social network Facebook also developed this type of system.
But in every exchange a central authority controls and discusses the changes.
With bitcoin, all network nodes are at the same time depositories of accounting books, verifiers, currency issuers and buyers and sellers.
How does that network work?
Each transaction between two users is actually done between two e-mail addresses in the form of an e-mail.
A user can choose a different address for each payment, thus ensuring anonymity.
A set of information linked to this transaction is signed electronically through a dual-key encryption system.
The network can thus verify the authenticity of the transaction.
Thanks to the contents of the file, it is also possible to ensure that bitcoin changes are contained in the public accounts book, which is disseminated throughout the network.
The key milestone is to write that new transaction in that book.
This goes through the resolution of a mathematical challenge thrown at the computers and the winner, a kind of temporary central banker, will have the privilege of adding that complementary line.
It's a file-cutting phase, that's the transformation of a large file into a shorter and more unique numerical footprint.
The computers "cock" the new transaction and add a number before "twisting" the set.
The objective is to find the number that offers a particular footprint (more zero at the beginning).
Once that number is found, the other nodes easily verify that it's good.
The transaction is then indestructiblely linked to the chain of assemblies of the other transactions; any modification would change the footprint.
If, in order to commit a fraud, a user wanted to pay twice with the same money very quickly (within less than ten minutes), the network would only accept as valid one of the two transactions; the other would become orphan, since both have different prints.
The computer that solves the defiance takes 50 bitcoins.
In order to avoid inflation, that reward is divided into two regularly, probably before the end of 2012.
The number of bitcoins in circulation is therefore limited to 21 million, but are divisible up to a hundred billionth, which leaves a wide margin.
The difficulty of this challenge is also revealed in every increase in calculus power.
The life of the network already has its ups and downs.
Websites offering Bitcoin services have been attacked and bitcoins have been stealed in deposience.
"The judgment used does not affect the same protocol," says Pierre Noizat, who has just launched Paymium, a real-currency payment company that uses the Bitcoin network.
The ECB also takes into account the possibilities of money laundering thanks to this anonymous service.
But the cash presents the same flaw.
Weight actors such as Wikipedia reject payments of this kind.
Others, such as the Wordpress platform, accept them.
Recently, Adi Shamir and Dorit Ron, of Insitito Weizmann in Israel, analyzed the ledger and showed that about 80 per cent of bitcoins don't circulate.
"Greague rebates" were launched in November.
"$30,000 was changed," rejoins Jon Holmquist, who works for Coinabul, who turns bitcoins into gold.
Pierre Noizat, the author of a pedagogical book on that currency, believes a lot about the potential of this technology in addition to the transaction network.
Its system, Paytunia, equates to a credit card (in real currency) or non-contact mobile payment, but it uses Bitcoin to validate the transactions, thus becoming cheaplier.
The bearer also manages his identity and can therefore remain anonymous.
The system is easy to launch in shops, which don't have to install new terminals or software.
It is enough for them to provide an address that a phone can "photograph and recognize," says Pierre Noizat, who claims to have thousands of users.
There's an overall movement to review hierarchical systems by more horizontal systems.
"It'll take a while for Bitcoin to impose itself, but in 2013 there could be a change," he predicts.
On the other hand, in its report the ECB plans to reassess the various risks, now considered high, in the event that this currency succeeds.
We're out of Afghanistan.
What about after?
The French troops abandoned their area of responsibility in Afghanistan (Kapisa and Surobi).
NATO and U.S. must leave at the end of 2014.
It's time for the Afghan army to resettle its territory and for the Afghans to choose their future, without expecting anything from us.
We've mostly chased Afghan peasants, whom we've seen as terrorists.
And to ourselves, with our 88 soldiers assassinated, plus the wounded and mutilated.
The Taliban have foreign extremists and former refugee bosses in Pakistan, but often also with peasants rejecting the presence of the foreign army, such as in Soviet times.
They want to defend their ancient and archaic traditions, even if the jihadists, the Pakistanis, the Arabs, uzbecos or the Tajiks come to their causes.
Tolerated, sometimes even aided by the local insurgents, they will no longer be when Westerners become more discreet.
The departure of the French troops from the Nijrab base, which I observed from the top of the hills planted with almond trees thanks to the French credits, was made in order.
Truck and tank convoys returned to Kabul without any attack, guarded by the helicopters.
There will be no Taliban influx in Kabul starting at the end of 2014.
Circumstances have changed since its irresistible progress between 1994 and 1996.
Kabul was then emptied and the country embroiled in fire and blood because of struggles between different factions.
His takeover of the country had been perceived as a sort of liberation, a return to security.
Afghans have paid the price for the obscurantism of their peasants surpassed by the Al Qaeda organization, but their situation is no better today.
The old Mujahideen, the current government and the current Taliban share a desire to keep women in a lower position.
The main personalities of anti-Soviet warfare returned to power in 2001.
They've become speculators, clutching government land to resell them as plots for refugees who have returned from Iran and Pakistan, bragging alongside huge U.S. subcontracting contracts.
They are discredited; most of them haven't struggled.
"People, as I've heard in the field, aspire to a government that's not made up of thieves.
Many young people want to leave, as well as those who have been able to take advantage of American generosity will leave: the capital flight is considerable.
Young people are tired of war and ideologies.
They have lived the modern world during their exiles in Iran or Pakistan and have appreciated its advantages.
With about 65 per cent of its population under the age of 25, Kabul now accounts for 5 million, a fifth of the total population.
In the cities, state schools are full of both girls and boys.
It will be necessary to give work to those young people who do not want to return to obscurantism of the old parties or the corruption of certain leaders.
Everyone, including armed opponents, is eager for mobile phones. Television and its turkish folletines featuring a modern world has great admirers throughout the country.
The army's present now.
Will the authorities who want to run the country be considered legitimate?
Former anti-Soviet wrestling commanders are already worried about reconstituting provincial militias, which will flee central power.
Afghanistan, a mountain country of strong local identities, should be able to benefit from some decentralization in the image of the Western nations. However, the United States has wanted to transform it into a centralized state, with strong presidential power, suppressing the position of the Prime Minister that existed since the 1964 Constitution.
President Karzai does not want foreign control, in particular at the elections scheduled in April 2014.
However, his country depended on foreign aid since the 1950s and even earlier.
Neither industry has re-established its services, no reservoirs have been fixed and important irrigation systems have not been repaired.
Everything's imported and you don't produce anything but fruits and vegetables.
Principle is left to private initiative.
In a country ruined after 30 years of war, there would have been a need for government control.
Rumor has spread that Afghanistan possessed immense mining riches.
And that only increased the feeling that Westerners were there to seize them.
Without the energy to extract iron or copper minerals from the ground and without means of transport to export them through the mountains, there is no mining operation.
The Chinese have almost abandoned the copper mines of Mes Aynak, letting international archaeologists (funded by the World Bank) excavate the immense Buddha site and remain there as the province's most important employers.
There will also be a need for Afghanistan and Pakistan, the country on which imports and exports are broadly dependent, to re-establish their normal relations.
French troops departed on November 20.
The new cooperation agreement envisages the continuation of traditional support: girls &apos; schools, children &apos; shoes, a French department at the university, the French Institute, cooperation in the military, legal, health and agricultural fields and support for the archaeological delegation.
Since 2009, in order to try to "get on hearts and spirits" and to carry out the impossible task of making aid and offensive actions coincide a service of "civil and military actions" of the Ministry of Defense (Cimic), suppressed in 2012, brought and brought through a small French NGO numerous works of collective interest and agricultural rehabilitation actions in dozens of mountain localities.
These works, greedy to obtain local labor, have been able to help contain the insurrection: irrigation, wells, drinking water, reforestation, fruit trees, sun protection and increase of growing areas.
What will we leave as a souvenir after two million euros of military expenses?
A rather modest budget will contribute to the improvement of local living conditions, very tough in those valleys often located more than 2,000 meters high.
The embassy received dozens of written lawsuits containing small agricultural proposals made by local communities in Kapisa province.
To be freed from the insurrection carried out by foreign groups, something that the farmers have told me that they want, it would be necessary to maintain small, well-required civilian aid that reaches them directly.
A Constitution with Forceps in Egypt
It's a new Double or Nothing by President Mohammed Morsi.
While Egypt is more divided than ever in terms of the constitutional declaration, which provisionally grants him full powers, he decided to play the whole.
He took everyone by surprise on Wednesday when he announced that the Constituent Assembly would vote its final text the next day.
Just a week ago, the head of State had given the Assembly two more months to complete its work.
And it's been almost two years since Egypt used a multi-modified provisional text, which weakens institutional stability and leads to judicial mess.
This new initiative has only increased the fraternity in the country.
For his opponents, the president perseveres in his "autocratic delirium" and continues to "trengite his word" and "floating the law."
On the part of its supporters, it is assured that this is a step towards a faster-to-embarrassing the institutional and political crisis and accelerating the transitional process.
A referendum should be organised over the next 15 days.
A very short period of time that forces the Brothers to relinquish their project to explain the text, article by article, to the Egyptians.
For the president, it is also a way of recovering a popular and democratic legitimization while the polemical causes havoc throughout the country.
Mohammed Morsi seems persuaded that the Egyptians will vote in his favor, as he stated in an interview with the American weekly Time.
Considering that this appeal has been adopted very quickly, it contains an ultimatum for the Egyptian people: "either they vote my text or I maintain the full powers," powers that will supposedly come to an end with the adoption of the Constitution.
It is in this strange environment in which 85 members of this Constituent Assembly, Islamist for the vast majority, voted the text yesterday.
Most liberals didn't come to the call.
By mid-November, shortly before the constitutional declaration, they had locked the gate because they felt they couldn't assert their views.
Representatives of human rights, religious minorities and civil society did the same.
In order to achieve the necessary quorum, yesterday morning 11 people, surrogate members, were added in a hurry.
Some of them are very close to the Muslim Brothers.
As might be expected, the compromised articles had been voted on unanimously.
On the other hand, commentators are grazed by the fact that one of the few discrepancies of the day has been practically expressed at the time of the sentence, so much so that several members of the committee came to think that the Constituent Clock was wrong.
The text, which was still being voted on yesterday afternoon, consists of 234 articles.
Article 2, object of all eyes, remains finally identical to that of the 1971 Constitution, which stipulated that "Sharia principles constitute the main source of the law."
The Salafist parties, for whom the establishment of Islamic law is a fundamental vindication, hoped that "principles" could be replaced by "the rules," which would have allowed for stricter application.
For the Islamists, that this article has not been modified is a guarantee of their goodwill and respect for the other components of Egyptian society.
The Liberals call it hypocrisy and consider that it's nothing more than an advertising trickery.
According to them, the Islamization of the Constitution is done through other articles.
They refer, in particular, to article 220, which gives Al-Azhar University an advisory role, in particular for anyone who is to confirm its conformity with Sharia laws.
According to Egypt specialist Sophie Pommier, it is troubling because "those called upon to speak out are not elected and have no democratic legitimacy.
Here we glimpse the premises of a theocracy."
Fears of the Liberals are also heightened by the fact that the university's next rector would be much less moderate than today.
"At the moment, there's no specific religious implication.
With this Constitution we are within the framework of civil status.
The majority of the jurists who have been working on this text are not ulemas, some of them trained in the French system," explains Alexis Blouet, who prepares a thesis on the Egyptian constitutional transition.
But he recognizes that there may be some ambiguity around article 220, since the terms used mimic the religious vocabulary.
"We specifically refer to the term iqh [Islorious Jurisprudence, ndlr].
And the question that could arise in the future would be to what extent the competent civilian judges are competent to rule over all this."
Regardless of its religious character, the text voted yesterday has received many criticisms concerning the extended powers conferred on the President of the Republic.
The Muslim Brother-Muslims will argue that there are few compared to those they were during the previous regime.
Another conflicting point: the powers conferred on the army.
According to the military &apos; s wishes, the review of the defence budget will not be submitted to Parliament, but to a national defence council.
There will also be no prohibition of prosecutions of civilians in military courts, as requested by human rights associations.
They have also expressed concerns about the text, which they consider libertarian.
The crime of blasphemmy is maintained and, henceforth, the insults are prohibited, which could have serious consequences for freedom of expression, especially that of the press.
In addition, no article emphasizes the protection of women, Heba Morayef of Human Rights Watch stresses.
According to her, the only positive has been the prohibition of torture in accordance with article 36.
The word wasn't contained in the previous Constitution.
Although the Egyptian president was due to leave last night on television, demonstrations are scheduled for this noon.
Supporters of the head of state demonstrated on Saturday.
In Israel, Ukrainian tourists await the holy land, the belly belly of the world and a sea of brine
Promised Earth combines the majesty of biblical truths with modern comfort and pristine nature.
"AIF" identified the five most important Reasons why Israel should be visited.
Reverence in the Holy Land
It's worth visiting the Jordan River where Jesus was baptized.
It iscreated that whoever enters this "baptismal stack" will receive divine grace.
And Galilee, the place of the miracles of Jesus: the conversion of water into wine in the wedding, walking upon the waters, the appeasement of the storm and the nets full.
In this same place, Jesus appeared before his disciples after the resurrection.
But as many holy places are found in Jerusalem.
Believers travel here the Viacrucis or the Camino de la Cruz de Jesús (in Latin "Worst Avenue").
It begins in the Strength of Antonio, the Pretoire, where the trial took place, and continues through the streets of the Old City, goes to the Holy Sepulchre on Mount Gólgotha, the place of the crucifixion, to the Stone of the Anointing and to the burial place of Jesus.
In this same place there is also the symbolic Christian navel of the world, which symbolizes the salvation of humanity.
And the monastery of the Holy Cross in Jerusalem, built in the place where, according to a Christian legend, there was a tree from which the cross was made for the crucifixion of Jesus.
In Jerusalem there is also the holiest place for the Jews, the Wailing Wall; the wreckage of a temple destroyed by the Romans in 70 AD.
As is tradition, people of different religions leave notes here with their desires, which will then come true.
Perform a vertical tour
The wrecks of the Masada Fortress was what was left of a secret refuge against the enemies, which Herod the Great commanded to build in the year 25 BC for his family.
They are located on mountain cliffs at an altitude of 450 meters above sea level.
And get there by their own means, they can only those who dedicate themselves to climbing.
Everyone else can get to this historic summit in the funicular.
And in the north of the country at an altitude of 1,600-2040 m is the famous Hermon ski resort, where in the winter months there is a large influx of tourists.
Bus services are available from the foot of the hill.
The total length of the ski slopes is 45 km.
According to ancient legend, on the top of the mountain they supposedly dwelved pagan gods.
To Visit Unique Museums
There's about 300 museums in this country.
You won't all be able to visit on a single trip.
But it's worth visiting for at least five of them, the most interesting.
Among them is the Museum of Israel, located near the Knesset (Anti-Italian Parliament).
The ancient Manuscripts of the Dead Sea or Qumran Smiles, undiscovered in the caves of Judea Desert, and about 500 thousand archaeological and anthropological pieces are preserved.
It's worth visiting the Museum of Art in Tel Aviv.
In his painting exhibition he has canvases of impressionists and expressionists such as Monet, Pissarro, Renoir, Cézanne, Sisley, Matisse, Modigliani, Chagall or Picasso.
In Acre you can visit the Al Basha Bathroom Museum, which are some rooms of ancient Turkish bathrooms with model figures of visitors and swimming swimmers of that time.
In Caesarea it's worth going through the exclusive private Ralli museum, where you can admire the sculptures of Rodin and Dalí.
There's no totour guides or shops there.
Entrance is free, and donations are categorically not accepted.
The fifth, the Museum of the Holocaust or Yad Vashem in Tel Aviv, shows one of the most dramatic pages in history.
The Most Tragic Section is the Children's Memorial, built in remembrance of the 1.5 million children killed in concentration camps and gas chambers.
You go into it and you're in absolute darkness.
The stars shine brilliant.
And the names of the Jewish children and the names of the countries in which they lost their lives sound.
Ukraine is also mentioned.
Cheers
Israel &apos; s three main tourist areas are on the shore shores of the Mediterranean Sea, the Red Sea and the Dead Sea.
In each of them there are swimming swimming pools, water parks, dolphins and oceanographic museums.
It is curious that in the Red Sea they bathe even in the winter months, since the water temperature does not drop from 21 degrees and the air heats up to 23 degrees.
The Dead Sea is even warmer and there they bathe all year round.
By the way, this sea, the most extraordinary in the world, is at the lowest point on the planet: at 417 m below sea level.
The bluish waters there are a brine that they easily sustain even those who don't know swimming. They don't know how to swim.
Surrounding landscapes are truly beautiful.
People arrive here to spend treatment courses with salt water, brine and healing mud and fortify their health in cases of dermatitis, allergies, asthma, eczema, arthritis, bronchitis, diabetes and to put their broken nerves in order.
Touch the mysteries of antiquity
It preserves the ancient part of Tel Aviv, the citadel of Jaffa, on the Mediterranean coast.
It passed the famous maritime route linking Egypt, Syria, Anatolia and Mesopotamia.
The city is mentioned in ancient Greek and Egyptian legends.
According to legend, it was here that Noah built his ark and Perseus rescued the Beautiful Andromed with whom, in this same place, he brought a long and happy life.
Tourists love to stroll through the narrow streets that bear the names of Zodiac signs.
They say that if you knock the walls in the street of your "same" symbol, fortune will be favorable to you.
In Jaffa you can find newlywed couples who come from all parts of Israel, and even from other countries to be photographed.
And in Caesarea, the city of King Herod you can stroll through the Roman theatheater, "take" the fortress of the Crusaders.
In Roman times, Caesarea was the main city of Judea and the residence of the Roman prosecutors, including Pontius Pilato.
Pretty-restrained theater is now used for evening concerts and opera shows.
Tourists Remember
If you're traveling to Israel, don't worry if your English leaves a lot to be desired: in this country about 30 per cent of the population speak Russian.
The money for this trip isn't advisable to wear it in euros, but in dollars, which can be easily changed to shéquels (currently 1-3$8, shéquels).
The urban transport consists mainly of the buses, but in Jerusalem there is fast tram and in Haifa there is the only metro line in the country, which consists of six stations and connects the Lower City with the Upper City.
Actually, it's from an underground funicular.
Roasting on any type of public transport costs 6 shéquels and for this price you can travel by trans transhipment for 1.5 hours.
The Sabbath is being celebrated in Israel according to the Jewish tradition on the Sabbath.
From Friday's Friday night to sunset on Saturday, markets and shops are closing and public transport interrupts their jobs.
But a new working week kicks off on Sunday morning.
In many cafes, restaurants and hotels only kosher food is served, which excludes pork, seafood, unspread fish and dishes combining milk and meat.
There a wide selection of dishes based on beef and lamb, soups and desserts prepared with coconut milk, the traditional Hebrew cream called hummus, a wide variety of sauces, the phaláfel, which are ground chickpeas balls, fruits and veggies.
You don't see stray dogs on the streets of Israeli cities.
On the other hand, there are plenty well-fed cats who "walk to their liking" in an imposing way.
At night you'll see them asleep even on the roofs of the parked cars.
Wrongs love busy places and don't reject treats.
Rental of a car, depending on the brand, will cost from $37 (Hyundai Getz) to $188 (Audi A6, Volvo S80) per day.
In addition to the insurance, it comes out for $15 a day.
And a bike's rent will come out for 15 shéquels a day.
Entries to the museums cost an average of 30 shéquels.
By Israel in Figures
More than three million tourists from around the world visited Israel in 2012.
The majority of those who came to touring the country and rest were citizens of the United States, Russia, France, Germany, Italy, the United Kingdom and Ukraine.
From January to October 2012, was visited by 118.8 thousand Ukrainian tourists, which exceeds the same rate in 2010, prior to the cancellation of the visa regime on February 9, 2011.
"The Great and Powerful One" will take you to Moscow: immigrants save for the tongue
While MPs and human rights defenders discuss the consequences of the law on compulsory examination, scammers have already appeared in the country who trade "false" certificates
Every year, 13 million immigrant workers arrive in Moscow, St. Petersburg and other cities in Russia.
For the most part are citizens of Central Asian countries: Uzbekistan, Tajikistan and Turkmenistan.
Their sole goal is to earn money to help their families who have stayed at home.
From 1 December a law enters into force, according to which each working immigrant will have to carry out an examination to verify his/her knowledge of the Russian language.
For the time being, this rule is applicable only to those who intend to work in the service delivery sector, community service institutions, domestic services and retail.
However, over time, as promised in the Federal Immigration Service, examinations will be made mandatory for all immigrants.
In addition, it will not be enough to take the examination of the language, but also that of Russian history and that of the foundations of the legislation.
Knowledge of the language must be confirmed even in order to receive and renovate the work permit.
The citizens of the countries where the Russian is the official language is an exception.
Persons who managed to obtain their certificates of education and diplomas prior to the dissolution of the Soviet Union in 1991 shall not be subject to this Act.
Utility, fatality and defense of rights
The network of surveying centres will be supported by the Pushkin Russian Language Institute, People &apos; s Friendship University, Moscow State University, St.Petersburg State &apos; s State University and other Russian higher education institutions.
Immigrants will be able to present themselves for the tests in all the cities, right now in Russia more than 160 such centers are open.
The initiative on the introduction of the review was supported by the deputies of the State Duma and the Federal Immigration Service.
But defenders of rights, long before the law came into force, often managed to ask this question to the press: And what will this actually contribute?
What changes will lead to the obligation of knowledge of the language for both the Russians and the immigrants themselves?
In the first place, representatives of the Immigration Service respond, this will make it possible to reduce the number of people suffering from "labour slavery."
A lot of people today talk about the defense of the rights of labor immigrants, explains Victor Sebelev, head of Russia's Federal Immigration Service representation in Tajikistan.
The defense of their rights must be initiated prior to their departure.
Only an organized selection system will make it possible to solve the problem of immigrant workers in 90 per cent of cases.
Trouations are faced by immigrants without a profession, education, those who don't know the language and don't pass medical control.
"Without an understanding of the language," Sebelev says, the immigrant future will be doomed to find unscathed people who, under the pretext of helping, put a "paid joice" on a creepy and narrow covacha, where he and dozens of others like him are consumed for months without food or documents, working from 12 to 14 hours a day.
We received countless complaints from our immigrants.
"In their homeland they promise them one thing, but when they arrive they are deceived, they are deprived of their passports, they do not be paid the promised wages," confirms the Head of the Labour Immigration Directorate of the Tajik Immigration Service, Tolib Sharipov.
- Don't get angry, Chief.
However, in the meantime many citizens of the Central Asian republics who want to work in Russia recognize that not only do they not know the language of the country they intend to go to, but also in their own language they write with difficulty.
Of course, this isn't so their fault but their own disgrace: there's a few Turkmenos, uzbecos, tayikos who can afford to receive normal primary education.
That's because their families don't even have anything to feed the children, let alone dress them, fit them and buy them what's necessary for the school.
Upon arriving in adolescence, these kids venture to work at the first opportunity.
It's difficult, if you don't know the language, they recognize them.
"Employments feel humiliated and inferior".
However, as far as the language law is concerned, rights advocates point to an important point.
Excesses shall be conducted only for those immigrants whose status is legal.
If you don't have that status, there won't be an exam and, consequently, no official work.
In the meantime, most immigrant workers continue to live in Russia illegally.
"Welcome or forbidden the passage to strangers".
Many visitors say receiving official status in our country isn't that easy.
Cause lies in bureaucratic hurdles and language-seeking difficulties.
In addition, legalization costs the money of twelve thousand to sixteen roubles.
However, a fake inscription can be done quickly and by only a thousand five hundred rubles.
Employees of the Russian police know that we usually carry false documents, we are not registered and cases of extortion appear.
"They're being asked for a hundred or two-hundred roubles for cigarettes or tea," Tajikistan citizen Umed Hushkadamov told journalists.
"Come on, don't be stingy, buy a painting".
On the first day of the entry into force of the Language Act, it was revealed that not only can immigrant registration documents be fake.
Several "false" language-knowledge certificates have already been seized by Federal Immigration Service officials.
False documents had been printed on standard color printers.
Of course, they came into the hands of the new owners not as a gift: each immigrant, wanting to facilitate the task of passing the tests, paid seven thousand roubles for the document.
That's two-and-a-half-half-half more expensive than the official exam procedure, which will cost the immigrant worker 3,000 roubles.
The main task in the near future is to protect the anti-Corruption system so "diploms" cannot be bought, government officials and rights advocates converge.
So far, the authorities can promise immigrant workers who would not have been able to successfully pass the test the first time to study the basic language course.
In addition, immigrants who do not speak Russian will be offered jobs that don't require active communication with people.
The Ministry of the Interior does not take drastic measures against the illegal arms trade
The percentage of crimes committed with registered weapons is quite low
The Ministry of the Interior proposes to apply the law more rigidly in relation to owners of civilian weapons.
This is the reaction of the government to the recent incidents: Squeeze shots during wedding celebrations in which fortunately there were no victims, and the massacre initiated by Moscow lawyer Dimitri Vinográdov, in which Squeezing seven people lost their lives.
The police want to ban the use and possession of traumatic weapons in public places and to raise the minimum age for obtaining a license to wear weapons, from 18 years to 21 years.
This idea was supported by the head of the Duma committee on security and anti-Corruption, Irina Yarovaya, who vowed that reforms to the weapons possession law will be taken to the State Duma in the near future.
Not everyone is happy with the fact that the Russian authorities are trying to counter this problem by applying "bad-hand politics."
An open letter has appeared on the network, whose authors, representatives of various shooter's social organizations, demand that such "non-sense rigid" measures be desisted.
"The percentage of vandalism with the use of registered weapons is minimal," criminal lawyer Vasiliy Lésnikov told the BBC news service.
According to statistics from the Ministry of the Ministry of the Interior, for six months of 2012 142 crimes with the use of firearms were committed registered in internal affairs bodies, compared with a total of 1168 crimes recorded for the same period.
The authors of the open letter are confident that stricter legislation in the civilian arms sector will not be an inconvenience for the offender to go to the black market.
Now, according to his words, in Moscow you can find any low-priced weapons.
Meanwhile, the Ministry of the Interior states that the situation of the proliferation of illegal weapons is under control.
Suppliers: from factories to officers
The black arms market is supplied through various channels.
The main channels are years, says Retired Colonel Victor Baranets, who for ten years worked in the Ministry of Education and the High General Staff.
Screenshooting of the website that accepts weapons orders
The first, "restige of the army or military," i.e. the weapons that were stolen during military actions in the Caucasus.
"Arms were stealed by the Russian officers and the same Caucasians," says Baranets.
The following type of "black weapon," those stolen by criminals and representatives of the security forces.
Mr Baranets explains that this includes the stolen weapons from police warehouses and those stolen directly by law enforcement officials.
Illegal weapons go for sale from military arsenals.
In many military arsenals there were often blashes.
"There's proven versions that some fires were premeditated to cover the foul," says a former soldier.
According to Baranets, the gun manufacturers also put their grain of sand.
"Private weapons factories have now proliferated that are uncompetitive on the international market and distribute arms under ropes to the black market, including that of Moscow," the expert says.
One more source on the black market is contraband.
A large number of pistols and machine guns come from poor countries such as Kyrgyzstan.
"There they have their own production, sometimes artisanal, and a mafia has been formed that has organized the flow of weapons," explains the former soldier.
Where'd the guns come from?
