Employees of fast-food restaurants and supermarkets in the U.S. are on strike.
Up to a quarter of U.S. teens find their first jobs as tellers on "McDonald's"
Over the past few days in the U.S., a wave of low-wage-watched protests has begun in Walmart's supermarkets and restaurants of popular fast-food chains such as "McDonald's," "Burger King," "Taco Bell," "Wendy's" and "Kentucky Fried Chicken."
At the moment no one dares to say whether or not this nine-point wave will arrive, or if it is destined to lose strength and dawn in its early stages.
The protest actions are supported by the trade unions and a series of left-wing NGOs.
In addition to the increase of the modest wages received by Walmart workers and those of the fast-food system, the objective of the organizers of the protests is the creation of workers' unions in these companies.
At the moment, this sector of the economy has practically not been occupied by the trade union movement.
Forty-six di cents a year?
The actions began last week, after Thanksgiving, the so-called "Great Friday." On that day, in the United States the orgies of rebates that captured millions of inhabitants, and that was sometimes accompanied by clashes.
On that day, some workers of the Walmart corporation, which employs 2.2 million people in the world, abandoned their jobs and formed protest picketites alongside trade unions and left-leaning activists in the corporation's stores, whose products are aimed at modest and moderate-income people.
Walmart sells everything from nappies, fighter rifles and batteries for cars, to vacuum cleaners, eggs and milk.
The products in their stores are on an average of between 8% and 27% cheaper than those of larger supermarkets.
That's why many low-paid Walmart workers shop only at work.
Availability and assortment of products have made Walmart one of America's largest corporations.
According to two critics, Walmart can afford to sell very low-priced products, in part because it pays little to its workers.
The latter also complain of poor working conditions, such as the lack of forklifts or hand scanners.
"Great Friday," the show's participants demanded an increase in wages and complained that the cost of health insurance provided by the corporation went from $30 to $100 a month.
For a typical Walmart worker who gets $9.5 an hour, this expense is excessive, they said.
Scientists at the University of California at Berkeley argue that if Walmart increases the average wage to $12 an hour, this will cost the corporation $3.2-billion.
That's about 1.1% more than you spend on wages now.
If Walmart puts the wage increase on consumers' shoulders, every visit to the store will be only 46 cents more expensive.
In a year they'll spend the ridiculous amount of $12.39 more than they spend now.
Supporters of Walmart comment that the protest took place only in nine states and did not cause any loss to the corporation.
"Great Friday" lasted in its stores from 8 p.m. on Thursday until midnight the next day and, during that time, Walmart sold about 5000 items per second.
In total, its cash registers carried out "Great Friday" almost 100 million transactions.
Company representative Dan Fogelman said in an interview with the left-leaning Huffington Post website that they quit "fewer than five" Walmart's employees and that the protest had only been "another PR stunt," organized by the workers' union.
"Get free".
Demonstrations continued this week in New York, in which the goals weren't precisely the Walmart department store (which in this progressive city doesn't appreciate them, and that's why there's none at the moment), but the "McDonald's" and other cheap restaurants.
McDonald's says he sells billions of portions, and then they don't even give you sick leave and they don't pay you for your honest work.
New York City Council Member Jumaane Williams
At present, according to federal and New NY law, the minimum wage is $7.25 an hour.
Fast-wheeled restaurants are increasing it over time, but only slightly, and on average, Ordinary workers receive $8.90 an hour in New York.
Nobody earns less than them in this town.
How it's possible to survive in New York with that money, for me it's an incomprehensible thing.
There was a time when nearly a quarter of U.S. teens passed through the McDonald's and worked there, behind counters, part-of-time after-school, although they were still living with their parents.
Few people saw this as the main source of income or planned to stay there for a long period of time.
Now, I constantly stumbling in some interviews with McDonald's workers who complain that they have to live off this wage, and sometimes even feed their children.
On the other hand, in a Wall Street Journal forum there is a comment whose author points out that it is irresponsible to have children if you don't know what to feed them.
Participants in the protests, which began at 6:30 a.m. on Thursday at the McDonald's on 40th Street and Madison Avenue, demanded that cashiers and fast-food cooks begin to be paid at least $15 an hour, i.e. their current minimum wage.
They also demanded that workers' unions be created in the fast-food sector.
U.S. law prohibits the administration from impeding this right and punishing trade union movement activists with trickeries and layoffs.
On the other hand, administration rarely facilitates their lives.
But for objective reasons it's easy for trade unions to cover the fast-paced eating sector.
One of the main ones is the excessive turnover of the staff.
Discordants
Roarful protests took place that same day at other cheap Manhattan restaurants.
The culmination of the action took place midway through the McDonald &apos; shoes near Times Square, in which a number of local Democratic politicians participated, including City City Council member Jumaane Williams, who said: "McDonald's says it's selling billions of portions, and then you don't even give you a hospital or pay for your honest job."
Demonstrators received the support of other prominent New York Democrats, such as city mayoral nominee Bill de Blasio, who said they "must support workers in the fast-food sector to receive fair wages and the economic prosperity each New Yorker deserves."
As the New York Times put it, this has been the largest manifestation of its kind in American fast-food history.
But it involved only a few hundred people, including not all of whom were fast-food employees, in which tens of thousands of New York people worked.
It's not yet clear whether this will squeeze to provoke a massive movement.
"For now, deceiving the brain isn't that easy".
The issue of augmented reality, so popular among modern typism enthusiasts, lately is perceived normally mainly through the prism of special goggles.
At the beginning of the summer, at its annual conference Google demonstrated a moving model and then, in November, it was learned that Microsoft had also submitted an application to claim the corresponding patent.
However, from a conversation with the head of the 3D interactive tech group in Cambridge's Microsoft lab, Izadi Sharamom, it emerged that for the company's scientists, the goggles already belonged at a later stage.
They are attracted the prospect of manipulating virtual objects in the air with naked hands and the creation of open virtual spaces.
-Please, in general terms, what's the object of your research group?
"We dedicate ourselves to person-computer interaction, but we are also trying to extend the limits of this interaction.
For now humanity in its great mass has been trapped in the working stage with the pixels of the flat-screen, sometimes playing it with the finger.
"But we want to look 5-10 years forward and guess the cardinal changes in this interaction.
For example, the Xbox console and Kinect Sensors represent a step forward and today no Xbox is sold without Kinect, as everyone is interested in gesture control.
- What else does the future hold for us?
- Although Kinect has taken the interaction to the physical level, much of it still occurs on a flat screen, sometimes in 3D.
The introduction of information has improved (the system receives more data), and the output is still not very good.
We are trying to change this, we are working on truly three-dimensional visualization systems, based on different technologies, including design systems.
You must free the world from computer science to ours, the physic physics, make it more tangible.
For this, however, it is necessary to recognize not only the user, but also the space that surrounds him.
Then we will be able to complete the real world with virtual objects in a more comfortable way.
First of all, fuck those silly virtual-recreated helmets. . . . . . . . . . . . . 
- What do you think about voice control?
A very popular detail, but don't you think they overestimate it?
Of course you can't be considered a panacea, you've got to solve the question of privacy, since you don't always want others to find out about our actions and intentions.
In fact, all types of computer interaction are good, but each in its own field.
For example, for the control of devices in public places, we had a project in which we thought of gestures, but not in loose movements, but shorter.
In addition, the gestures were captured not by the camera of the device, but by a bracelet that recorded the movement of bones and muscles.
At the moment it's very big, but in theory it can be reduced to the size of a wristwatch.
But, in general, the future is for a combined type of grudgerability, for example gesture and voice.
- What's that?
- For example, how would you ask me to pass this soda can?
You're gonna talk and you'll show it at the same time.
Usually I'm just talking.
- That's gonna be very hard to recognize.
I mean, do you want to force users to adapt to what the machine can or can't do right now?
It's not necessarily, but it's a mutual rapprochement.
I believe that in the near future we are going to have to work mainly on the design of new types of sensors that can accurately determine a person's reactions.
These can be, for example, laser sensors, as they provide good in-depth resolution, which is very important.
- If we're talking about your work with Xbox Kinect Sensors, what complaints do you have about modern cameras?
Do they lack resolution, depth or anything else?
- In principle, the current generation is what may be the beginning of our work with three-dimensional recognition.
Of course it'd be nice to get 8 megapixels at the speed of 1000 fps.
But the question lies not in the megapixels, but the quality of the wreckage and in the depth.
From the latter point of view, all current technologies aren't good enough for us; this gives algorithm designers more work.
In other words, we have to recall not only the resolution on the X and Y axes, but also in Z.
Speed is also important, the number of frames per second.
The human movements are quite dynamic and the current of 30 fps are truly scarce, especially for the gestures.
Steven Bathiche of our lab in Redmond made an adjustable one-to-100-foot-the-match contact-consumption and it's worth mentioning that modern serial-produced sensors are closer to the second value (60-100).
Not everyone understands the extent to which this affects man-machine interaction.
It would be very useful for my work an equivalent of such a device, but I don't require contact so that the frames per second are more.
- Isn't it necessary to increase the number of cameras?
- The Kinect now has three "cameras," one of which is in fact the infrared emitter and the second is the receiver of the reflected signal.
But the third camera is actually a simple visible fingerboard sensor.
This one is not used to determine the depth of the object.
Potentially, as many of the cameras could solve the problem...
On the other hand, increase it and increase the required number of calculations.
It would be nice to create a flexible Kinect analog, to play with the location curvature of the cameras and to see how this would help to determine the three-dimensional position.
- As far as I remember, Microsoft, unlike Google, hasn't presented its glasses to the public.
Don't you think that, from the point of view of the daily use of augmented-reality technologies, glasses are one of the most visible platforms?
"Of course, hanging all the time holding up a smartphone in your hands isn't very comfortable, but I'm thinking: the best option would be an augmented "transmutable" reality when, based on a cloud platform, you could go from glasses to the smartphone,
Glasses are very personal devices, that's your strength (private things only you'll be able to see them), but also your weakness: an augmented fingry-educated reality won't allow you to work on virtual objects along with other people.
- Let's enimagine for a moment the manipulation of virtual holographic stalgics in the air is available not only to Tony Stark of the film "Iron Man," but also for any mere mortal.
There's a problem in this idea that critics often point out: there's no tactile response.
Your hands don't feel at all.
What answers does your group prepare for this challenge?
- In my lectures I often say that augmented reality is the seventh eruption in the interaction between man and machine.
I think the octave might well be the addition of tactile sensations.
In the meantime, one of the most interesting tricks is the use of the second-hand as support for the image.
Grab the tact perfectly.
But there are also technologies that are genuinely oriented to bring tangibility to these "images in the air." For example, the interference of several ultrasonic rays aimed at a specific point where the finger is located gives a similar feeling to the one you feel if someone was blowing your fingertips, but that sensation is still very weak.
There are also wrist bracelets that influence nerve finger endings, this also a promising direction.
- You've been trying to deceive your brain?
To make him think that he feels what it seems he should feel at the moment he sees something?
-That's a good idea, we haven't tried yet.
There's another challenge that we'll have to struggle with for a long time, how to get a person physically located in a very small space to believe that it goes to an open, almost endless space. We are also working on the idea of running tapes (not like gyms), mobile platforms, as well as giant balloons.
At the moment, deceiving the brain isn't that easy, you still have to work for many years.
That's what makes work with virtual reality attractive to researchers, that a lot of things are in a state of total conception.
