Giving birth in space
Earth's in jeopardy.
Global warm-up or clash with a killer asteroid.
Caravans of spacecraft with on-board humans depart in search of a spare planet.
To consecrate humanity the main question arises: How to continue the species in weightless conditions or on the same spare planet?
I don't think there's little to choose from.
In fact, only two are true planets that can be colonized, at least hypothetically.
"These are Venus and Mars," says State Astronomical Institute Chief Researcher Sternberg P.K. (IAES), Vladimir Surdin.
But if the conditions on Mars are better for life, on the surface of Venus the temperature is 500 degrees.
You can only live at high altitudes or in Venus's orbit... in space.
Reproductive problems in the space began to be addressed with the flora.
Half a century, plant experiments have been carried out for half a century.
Four generations of peas cultivated in orbit did not differ in anything from terrestrial varieties.
Then came the breeding of bugs in the orbit, small fruit flies, drosophiles.
In 1979, quail eggs were shipped into the space to see how the embryo develops in weightless conditions.
We've got a normal chick as a result.
But then the problems start.
"The problem is that this chick has to find support, it must stay on its own feet and begin to move," explains Vladimir Sychev, the laboratory director of the Institute of Biomedical Problems (IPBM) of the Russian Academy of Sciences.
Upon no support, the chicks gave random flip-flops.
And after 10 hours in the newborn there was a total atrophy of the instincts.
Chickens didn't react to light or sound.
The disgrace was that after four days they were just dying.
"And twice we got the chicks born, but then we left it because it's impossible to work there with them," Vladimir Sychev says about the failure of the experiment with chicks in space.
The last time a biological "miniarch" flew into orbit was 16 years ago.
Experiments will continue in the spring of 2013.
However, only same-sex species will travel in the biosatellite "Bion."
There's been an experiment with rats, who were already sent into space with fetuses.
In principle, nothing supernatural was found.
"That's precisely in biosatellites, but this was a unique experiment, and studies like this should be carried out," says Vladimir Sychev.
Upon landing, the space rat had offspring.
However, it has not yet been possible to address the problem of replication directly in the space.
That's a tough challenge.
The animals, by losing their usual habitat, simply can't realize their sexual instinct.
Man, however, unlike the small animals, in principle, he can.
Homo sapiens has abstract reasoning and is able to create a proper emotional background.
Similar experiments don't take place for ethical reasons.
But women have been traveling into space for 50 years.
Tereshkova's the greatest risk.
And the most precious thing about humanity is the women's organism.
Our gull flies, and no one on Earth could say: if it's after a flight into space.
You may or may not have a baby after this flight.
"Affter all, to this question no one has answered," says aerospace veteran Vakhtang Vachnadze.
In June of 1964, only after a year of flight into space, the first female cosmonaut, Valentina Tereshkova, gave birth to a daughter.
The girl &apos; s father, Andrian Nikolayev, was also a cosmonaut.
In 1988, Svetlana Savitskaya's second-worst female cosmonaut, who was twice in orbit and even worked in open space, had a son.
However, the risk persists.
We're very few, the astronauts are numbered that after long flights everything has turned out to be okay, and a healthy generation is growing.
"And if it can be said more, it is dangerous even on orbital flights," summarizes the cosmonaut pilot, hero of the Soviet Union, Russian hero Valery Polyakov.
However, humanity must seek some new avenues in biotechnology, in radiation protection, in the creation of artificial gravity.
The hydrolaboration of the Cosmonautas Preparing Center is a mandatory stage in preparation for the flight.
In zero buoyancy conditions, cosmonauts practice their work skills in space.
Water simulates the weightlessness.
If for an adult the water is a comfy environment, although still hostile, for a newborn child it's their native element.
The little anhibious men seem to confirm that life on earth emerged from the ocean.
There's a connection between the fact that the baby's about 9-month-old inside his or her mother in the amniotic fluid, as it's going to make it easier to get used to the water later.
In principle, this is logical, because from the time of the birth until the time of the first swimming swimming bath they spend only 2 weeks.
"That's too little to forget about something," says Marina Aksenova, swimming monitor of newborns.
In a nutshedliance, if weightlessness is a more natural state for a newborn, gravitation is necessary for women the force of gravity.
In the weightlessness the abdominal muscles and pelvis decrease very quickly, the ejection capacity of the fetus is diminished.
Well, we skip that, relying on the action of childbirth stimulants.
You can, of course, give birth in a special camera, it will push it.
"And then what?" Valeri Polyakov asks this original question.
On the other hand, the child also needs artificial gravity.
Without the sensation of gravity, the organism doesn't generate the skeleton or the muscle system.
It's impossible to wear a special workout suit, such as adults.
He'll just be stripped of what's vital to him.
"And this experiment that we are going to do together, by allowing the birth of a child in a weird environment, will cause us to bring to Earth a disabled person, a totally unadapted person," predicts the head of the bioethics committee of the Institute of Biomedical Problems of the Russian Academy of Sciences, Igor Pestov.
At the moment the birth of children in the space is a theoretical issue.
However, over time this will become a reality, when humans in their ships turn to a distant planet that will be the home for their children born in space.
