"The law will never be perfect," but its application must be correct. "This is, in my opinion, what we are lacking," the current situation describes.
The joint controls of the Czech and German inspectors also reach similar conclusions.
As an example of an incorrect practice, it mentions Petr Neas &apos; attitude towards the system of self-autonomous only on paper, which is known as the "Evarc" system.
The Prime Minister recently said the SDG (Civic Democratic Democratic Party) wouldn't bother the entrepreneurs with inspectors. Then it's prohibited or allowed?
"The law must be regulated in any way and if it bans something, in that case, neither the president of the government can prevent the actions of the office that has an obligation to carry out such controls or to demand obligations," Kala says.
At the meeting of the control committee in the Congress of Deputies, you talked about the common project between the Czech-German team, within the framework of which the legislation relating to public adjudications in both countries was compared.
What exactly's that?
It's a parallel control that we started carrying out about two years ago.
Simply said: European legislation regularizes how public awards should be organised, followed by the legislation in force in each country and then praxis itself.
We've put it all together and, although control hasn't been completed yet, we've achieved very interesting differences. Generally, the legislation of our country is "stricter and more complete," however, the concrete practice in some parameters is better in Germany.
It is therefore confirmed that it is not enough to incorporate increasingly strict rules, but it is necessary to concentrate on the implementation of the law itself.
What can this project actually contribute? What do you think are the conclusions?
Precisely a common control of this kind could help us to stop investing efforts in the realization of laws, the reduction and the improvement of the limits, even if this does not have such a positive impact.
The economy of savings means getting an appropriate thing at a reasonable price (which doesn't always mean that the price is the lowest) to avoid enrichment and, in the long run, criminal actions.
But not by lowering the adjudication limit means we're gonna get it.
Maybe because the system is saturated with so much "mope," that those who want to look for failures in the system will be able to do it more easily than if the boundaries remained as high as they are now.
You are talking about the problems of our country with the transfer of legislation that regulates the practice of public adjudications.
How does the control system work in Germany?
Was it an office similar to our NK or is it organized in a different way?
As for the offices, such as the NK, the Bundesrechnungshof (Federal Court of Auditors) operates there, with an organization similar to ours, with a college as well, the only thing is that in its case it is structured in a somewhat different way, but in principle both offices evolve in parallel.
. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .
The Federal Court of Auditors (BRH) can only control the federal money, which here we call state funds.
The public funds, which are managed by the regions and the municipalities there in Germany, are controlled in the Federal Court of Auditors.
When it comes to local legislation, is it simpler than ours?
Usually, I wouldn't want to make a comparison without specific data. However, Germany is still a model in some respects, but it is not possible to specify exactly what is best.
Could it be better, for example, in terms of the quality of the inspectors?
I certainly don't, but, I repeat, I don't like making these kinds of comparisons.
It is necessary to say that even those countries we take as a model must face a large number of problems.
If it weren't, it wouldn't take them any advantage to collaborate with our office, would it?
With regard to the legislation of our country, what did the amendment of the law on public awards mean for your Office? Is its impact in any way apparent?
Bearing in mind that the period of validity of this amendment is relatively short, it has not yet manifested itself in the control proceedings.
Since we carry out ex-post checks, there needs to be a certain delay.
For the time being, it is observed in the process of the preparation of future controls: we have started a new "non-economic risk detection" tool, through which almost 14,000 public awards have passed, which have been analyzed. The changes will be perfectly reflected here, since the limits are modified, the conditions of some types of the selection process are rectified.
Do you consider that the adoption of this law is beneficial or that, rather, it represents a mess for the bureaucratic system?
I think this law is surely a step in the right direction and I hope so.
The problem that may arise is that the regulation "is tightened" and that it is impossible to comply.
Already with the previous regulations, controlled persons were linked to the contractor (e.g., in the case of the regional operational programmes of the regional council), and each law unrespected for public awards meant a violation of budgetary discipline.
Does it then make sense to harden the law in this way?
I don't think so.
The system must exclude those who want to attack it or take advantage of it and do not punish those who make a formal failure, which has no impact on decision-making.
Such a system will only manage to create greater bureaucratic pressure.
And how do you get out of this?
We'll see where we'll get this path started.
The Prime Minister has recently stated that the SDG would not bother entrepreneurs with controls of the so-called Evarc system, with reference to being autonomous only on paper. What does this mean?
Is the varc system prohibited or permitted?
The law must be regulated in any way and, if it bans anything, then even the Prime Minister of the government cannot prevent the action of the Office that has an obligation to carry out such controls and demand obligations.
You can say: "We change the law and allow it," but you can't say we're gonna act like nothing.
According to the law on public adjudications, the regulations are quite strict as to the manner in which formal action must be, and this is how it should be to protect public competition.
On the other hand, on the one hand, it is tragic that the candidate is excluded with the best offer for not complying with the formal requirements.
The law will never be perfect, but its application must be correct. That is, in my opinion, what we lack.
The roads have ice plates, although they are mostly passable
In a number of parts of the Czech Republic the roads have ice and snow plaques.
Although the majority of the tracks are passable, in a number of precautions should be exercised.
Karlovy Vary Region
Today in the morning the roads in the Karlovy Vary region were passable, but had ice and snow plaques.
Temperature decreases and will range from five to ten degrees below zero. Thermometers will rise slightly during the day.
It has stopped swimming snow in the region and in the lower areas the snow layer is thirsty.
By contrast, on the ridges of the Metalic Mountains the snow reaches almost 30 centimeters.
Visibility in some places was still poor because of the fog, according to information from the region &apos; s road operations control centre.
Fast-track R6 and first-class road in the region are now unrestricted.
However, it is recommended to extreme caution, especially on some bridges whose surface can be icy and slippery.
In addition, all second- and third-class roads, including mountain roads, are also roverable.
However, in some of them there are still swaths of icing and splendid shoes.
There is a need to increase attention on the road, especially in the highest areas.
Region of Pardubice and Hradec Králové
