"In the highest areas of umper, snow remains are left.
Drivers in the port of Eervenohorské will find half-dispose snow in the direction of Jeseník," confirmed today an operator of the road administration from umper to TK.
On the ground and at night their colleagues also went to Jesenická and, after the chemical treatment, the clues of the higher areas are now clear and wet.
On the Olomouc road the tracks are unrestricted passable, in Eternberská drivers should drive with precaution on the forest stretches, where the roads are still moist.
Estí Nad Labem Region and Liberec Region
Operators on roads in the north of Bohemia have reported complications on different stretches early in the morning.
In addition to a number of snowfall or ice ice cream, according to the police database the mountain road between Telnice and Knínice in the Estí nad Labem region is closed.
Temperatures remain below zero, even in the lower areas, so the ice and snow obviously remain on the road. On the other hand, in the lower areas, especially in the south-south of the Czech central massif, there are no problems and the roads are usually dry.
There have been no service complications due to the mmento.
Road operators have noticed the presence of ice in the surroundings of .
According to the meteorologists, the conditions have been ideal during the night. During the day rain and snow is expected to melt and at night the sky will be clear and there will be ice.
Unfavorable conditions have been advertised from the general roads on road I/13, on the border between the Estí nad Labem region and Liberec.
The road between Telnice and Knínice had to be smashed by the branches of the trees that had arched on the road with the weight of the snow.
Eimon Ornest: In our concerts we want to accumulate positive energy
It is said that in a little less than a month the end of the world is going to arrive, what do you think about it?
It's just one of so many other alarmist news that we like to grasp.
Music group The Tap Tap well like to make jokes about this and we like to say that we are the world's only music group that manages to gather enough positive energy to postpone the end of the world or to avoid it.
In addition, in December you organize a truly original project: three concerts against the end of the world.
You can explain it to our readers?
It's a state charity event we've been planning for two years.
We decided to take advantage of the marketing potential that has the end of the Mayan calendar set for December 21 at 11:10 a.m.
The day before, on December 20 at 9 p.m., we will organize 3 simultaneous concerts in Prague, Brno and Ostrava.
They'll end at about the same time the Mayan calendar ends on Kiribati Island, in the Pacific Ocean, 12 hours ahead of our time zone.
Who did you come up with this idea?
Initially it was my idea, then we were forging the project all together with our designer Honza Augusta.
In addition to wanting to gather enough positive energy and avoid the end of the world, we also want to reflect, together with society, on the state in which we will leave the world to our children.
At the end of the Mayan calendar we have also created a unique collection of items: boots, T-shirts, bags and original keychains against the end of the world that can be purchased at www.e-tap.cz, another way to support us.
The group The Tap Tap, along with other performers, also sang the so-called anthe anthem against the End-of-the-World, titled "End of the World, Candidate."
It's currently very popular on YouTube, will you be able to be heard at charity concerts?
Yeah, and it'll be just the last, okay, as long as it's all okay and doesn't end the world...
They will shoot all the performers at the same time in the three concerts.
The anthems will link to each other simultaneously thanks to the special live broadcast on Czech Television.
Hanák wrote the lyrics of this song and plays the leading role of Jesus in the video clip, where he also shoots Xindl X...
How did this collaboration arise?
We also work together with other personalities of the Czech cultural scene, apart from organizing many charity events and concerts...
We make every effort to participate in this project.
It has been shown that most of them are interested and amused to work with us.
To what will the money raised be directed at concerts against the end of the world?
Equipment for the Studeo barrier-free educational center, which we have completed in its sixth consecutive year with the civil association Tap en Jedliak.v ústav.
The students of the school of Jedli-k. ústav are regularly visited by teachers with whom they carry out fun and interesting activities.
Students don't have sufficient financial means to pay teachers and we make an effort to make it possible.
Within the framework of the completion of the works in Jedliak-v ústav, there will be an independent building to which we will move with this project.
Various groups and performers work in each concert.
What's the selection criteria?
We are trying to develop a program that is attractive for all generations, including children.
For example, in Prague they will perform Chinaski, Support Lesbiens, Illustratosphere with Dan Bárta, The Tap Tap, Marián Bango and Jisí Such.
For more detailed information, please visit www.kpks.cz.
Do you have any other similar "spectacular action" planned in the future?
In May we will perform for the first time at the Praské jaro festival, so here we will surely prepare an attractive program with interesting guests.
Next next year we'd also like to play at the Bohemian National Hall in New York and me, personally, taking advantage of the fact that we're in the U.S., I'd like to complete it with performances in Washington and in Chicago.
Your plans abroad aren't exactly minimalists, until now you've performed for example in Madrid, Brussels, London or Moscow.
The Tap Tap, however, is a group made up of people with disabilities.
What's the logistics and the organization of these trips like?
It isn't as difficult as it may seem at first glance.
There's five members in an electric-wheelchair, the chairs must travel in the luggage compartment and we also carry a lot of weight, the boxes with the instruments...
However, so far we've managed very well. SA and British Airways were perfectly prepared, which doesn't cease to amaze me.
Even in Moscow, where we've recently come from, it all came out without problems.
Thanks to departures abroad you can compare society's different attitudes towards the disabled and other aspects.
What have you been in so far?
In Madrid, Luxembourg, London and other cities, everything works better than here; in Russia we have witnessed that, in the east, there is a long way to go.
Contrary to Prague, Moscow is full of barriers, it's still uncommon for a person to move alone around the city centre in an electric wheelchair.
Something as common as it is, for example, giving preference to someone in a wheelchair in an elevator, there it's not.
Fortunately, a civil partnership has been set up to address the problems of persons with disabilities.
In what aspects should we take an example of more advanced countries?
There's plenty of things we're going from behind...
But it is important to mention that the improvement and improvement of the situation always depends on the people it directly affects.
It's very normal for people with a severe disability to travel on their own in public in London and in Madrid to go to the service, to the museum, wherever...
But it is not very common for a large number of people with disabilities to participate actively in social life, in this regard, with The Tap Tap Tap we're a little ahead.
A respectful or barrier-free society is one thing, but when the time comes when eminent athletes, artists, actors, politicians or lawyers emerge, it will really begin to change everything.
Until now, they are isolated cases of people who have a great willpower.
The group The Tap Tap is currently very popular, but if we look a few years ago, what led you to form the group in 1998?
I came in as an educator in Jedliak-v ústav, where I had a lot of young people around me who wanted to dedicate themselves to something.
"And since I'm a musician, I play, among others, the saxophone," I founded an amateur group with a co-worker.
And in time, as our moderator Láa Angelovic says, we got a little out of our hands (laughs).
However, popularity hasn't come until these last few years, or am I wrong?
It is true that it was quite helped by connecting with celebrity singers and also working actively to promote the group.
We come to the conclusion that the work that isn't seen is as if it doesn't exist.
Thanks to European Union support, we can also be able to afford teachers of quality, equipment, etc.
Were you meant to go this far with The Tap Tap?
From the very beginning I saw the potential that existed, that you can do something in another way.
The spectacle world is full of things that resemble each other.
It is logical, in its own way, everything new is welcomed cautiously and it takes a long time to be accepted.
There's a few extraordinary things, but I dare say The Tap Tap is one of them.
The impression of someone who sees us for the first time is clearly the compassion, it's a natural reaction...
But pcompassion is totally superfluous because people with disabilities are not abandoned beings who suffer and from whom we must have compassion.
There are people who are able to live a full life and develop, on the condition, of course, that they have the necessary means.
I always say that if something works well for people with disabilities, then it's not just a breakthrough for them, but for society as a whole.
Did you help the fact that you're a discerning boss, as many people describe to you?
If we want to do a quality job, we have to be rigorous in many respects and demand some discipline.
I think it's utterly normal.
Some people arrive with a romantic vision and with their heads in the clouds and when they find out they have to go two days to rehearse, go to the preparatory test and spend a lot of time traveling and in concerts, the initial euphoria abandons them quickly.
But that's how it works everywhere, in every group you want to work and get something.
The Tap Tap group currently has 20 members.
How many of them were already at the beginning of 1998?
Only one: Laa Angelovic.
We are an open group, people come in and out, you don't avoid it.
Whoever has the will and eager will always find the doors open
It'll be the day before the world's long-awaited Engagement on Thursday and 9 p.m.
It will take place at the Incheba Arena in Prague, in Fléda de Brno and in Plynojem in Ostrava and will involve 12 groups and other musicians from the Czech Republic
The three cities will connect at the end of the evening via videoconference to clinch the group song The Tap Tap "Fin of the World," cancelled
The objective of the concert is to gather the resources necessary to finance multifunctional equipment of the STUDEO barrier-free educational centre in Jedli-k?v ústav in Prague, which costs 25 million Czech kroner.
Entrance to the concert costs 400 CZK and children up to the 12-year-and-year-olds have a free entry on the pre-sale option in Bohemiaticket
Poland and the cosmos.
At the meeting of ministers of the countries of the European Space Agency held last week Poland was accepted as the 12th member of ESA. It is the second ESA member country of the former Eastern European bloc, after the Czech Republic, which became a full member on 12 November 2008.
Poland initiated intensive collaboration with ESA in 1994 and during the following years participated in the agency &apos; s project committee.
Poland's path to cosmos, however, began much earlier.
Polish enthusiasts were already engaged in flights into space prior to World War II, although their work was not always understood.
I remember, for example, the A. Eternfeld conference at the astronomical observatory in Warsaw where, on December 6, 1933, he presented his ideas on the pioneering study "Introduction to cosmonautics."
The ideas of the young engineer (born 1905) left attendees indifferent and, years later, Eternfeld recalled that Dr. Jan Gadomski had been the only one who had shown an interest in his work.
In the year 1934 Eternfeld won Robert Esnault-Pelterie and André Louis Hirsch Award in France for their "Introduction to Cosmonautics."
Doctor Jan Gadomski (1899 - 1966), mentioned above, later became a great promoter of astronomy and cosmonautics.
He published hundreds of articles in Polish journals and wrote a series of books dedicated to this scientific discipline.
Gadomski became a promoter of cosmonautics, was known all over the world and his contribution to science was rewarded when his name was given to one of the craters on the craters of the moon's hidden face.
Already in 1925 a dresine was built in Poland that was going to be equipped with a rocket engine.
Unfortunately, it is not known who was the author of the proposal and there is also no knowledge of the details of the project.
It is not clear whether the rocket was to be used to boot the dresine or for braking.
The information about this dresine is limited to that collected in the press of the time.
Starting in 1933 the Polish artillery began to engage in the missiles.
The research was funded by Komórka Techniki Uzbrojenia in collaboration with Prof. Mieczyslawem Wolfkem and prof. Gustawem Mokrzyckiegim.
Based on the preserved material, it becomes clear that the investigation reached the stage of practical testing.
However, with the arrival of the German troops, the investigation was halted.
In the year 1937 appeared in the specialized press the prototype of a photo-changing fighter missile designed by the engineer Rohoziski and the following year was published the study "Rakieta - torpea powietrzna and rakietobomba lonicza," by the author Leliwa-Krzywoblocki.
Both projects were aimed at the military use of missile engines.
Shortly before the war, the Interim Scientific Advisory Committee (Tymccesowy Komitet Doradczo-Naukowy) was responsible for all missile technical projects for military use and coordinated all the work.
The committee was founded in 1937, but after two years of activity, it was interrupted by the beginning of the war.
Other studies devoted to cosmonautics appeared in the post-war Polish press thanks to the work of the Polish Astronautics Society (Polskie Towarzystwo Astronautyczne).
In the November 1954 issue of the magazine Problemy refers for the first time to the society with four very extensive articles dedicated to astronautics.
In one of them, Prof. M. Subotowicze proposed the creation of a society that would dedicate itself to cosmonautics.
Artificial satellite projects had already been launched at that time and it was obvious that cosmic research was a discipline with prospects for the future.
Since the beginning of 1956 the Polish Astronautics Society (PTA) aspired to join the International Federation of Astronautics (founded in 1951) and in the autumn of that same year the PTA became a full member.
The following year the first president of the PTA, Kazimierz Zarankiewicz (1902 - 1959), became vice president of the International Federation of Astronautics.
He held this charge until his death in 1959.
Starting in 1956, the PTA participated significantly in the development of the RM probe rocket (Rakieta Meteorologiczna), which was a success and became the first Polish rocket in which scientific research was carried out.
The RM-1 category was finalised in 1957 and was first released on October 10, 1958.
The rocket was 1,800 meters tall, about 80 cm long and did not weigh at 5 kg.
Later, the RM-1A version was completed and in the summer of 1959 the aerial tests of the two-stage RM-2 RM-2 rockets began in the Desert of Bodów.
Footage streak measured 1.4 meters and weighed about 11.5 kilograms.
Another type of developing rocket was to be the subject of real scientific study. The RM-34 rocket was going to have a heyday of 14.5 km and its mission would be to control the high-rise wind.
However, work was adjourned in 1962.
The successor of the first RM-type rockets was the Meteor-1 rocket, which was built during 1962 and 1965.
It was designed as a two-stage rocket, with a total length of 510 cm and an initial weight of 32.5 kg.
Three types (called Meteor-1A, -1B and -1C) were developed that differed from each other because of the space available for scientific devices.
In the Meteor-1A rocket the space available for the volume was 0.4 liters, in the Meteor-1B of 0.34-litre and in the Meteor-1C of 0.62-litre Meteor1.
The peak in all three types was 37 km.
The Meteor-2 rocket was developed at the Air Institute between 1965 and 1968, whose first aerial tests began in October 1970.
The Meteor-2 rocket had an initial weight of 380 kg and was able to carry a useful weight of 10 kg up to 60 km in height.
Later the Meteor-2H and Meteor-3 version was built.
It is also necessary to mention Poland &apos; s entry into the COSPAR (Committee for Space Research) in the year 1960 and the formation of the COSPAR national committee two years later.
As part of the Interkosmos programme, Poland joined the research of cosmic space on Soviet artificial satellites and, in 1978, Polish pilot Miroslaw Hermaszewski became the second intercosmonaut after Vladimír Remek.
Suspension of the law on social work is not the solution.
Last last week the constitutional court adjourned the law.
The resolution aroused a heated discussion in society.
Of course it will be interesting to look at the existing problem from a broader point of view.
The liberal economic systems in the EU, as in the globalised world, are based on the principle of greater economic and less regulatory competition.
As a result, this means that the various economic forms and national economic systems are in permanent conflict with each other.
The cause lies in the principle of free trade and unresponsive and unlimited movement of private capital, coupled with unbridled financial speculators.
The effect of the large differences in labour costs (of wages) produces economic tensions.
Under this concept it is necessary to understand the situation in which the manufacturer, with the import of cheap goods, tries to shorten distances and to this end "causes a decrease" in the price of the competitor with the aim of seizing most of the market and thus increasing its profit margin.
On a large scale it means, for the majority of entrepreneurs, to move production abroad, to buy cheaper there or to disappear. The result is high unemployment in those countries where the price of work, compared to other economies, is higher.
Because private capital does not have any social responsibilities and, with it, it is also not responsible for the unemployment that this phenomenon generates, the social expenditure of the state necessarily grows.
All of this situation fosters the total disinterest of entrepreneurs in paying taxes and compensating for the economic and social damage they have caused in their eagerness to obtain profits.
The situation is universally so well-prediable that there is no need to provide specific statistical data.
Unscrupulous practices of private capital create situations, in certain economies, where the Governments of those countries are obliged to compete with the aim of artificially reducing the social level of the population itself and thus attracting foreign capital to the country.
In other words, governments put private capital at the disposal of their own people regardless of social decline.
This is particularly evident in the changes in existing laws.
The objective is to force the population to accept the dictate of private capital, especially with regard to wages.
On the one hand, this takes place, on the one hand, through the system of economic obligation in the case of long-lasting unemployment; on the other, through the restricted right of employees in terms of labour rights.
The result is growing poverty coupled with the widening differences between the poor and the rich.
For a number of years now, there have been food delivery points in Germany for poor people who can't keep their own work.
The number of people who receive this type of aid already reaches a million.
In the context of the increase in the competitiveness of the German economy, there is a fairly frequent case: people employed with such a low salary that the state must pay the rest in order to reach the essential minimum to survive.
Such a scandal related to the situation of auxiliary employees of the German federal parliament became public.
The savings measures of virtually all the states of the south of the EU undoubtedly lead to the same situation.People, pressured by an abysmal decline of their social level, are forced to emigrate as in the 19th-century century or to bulge with a lousy salary at the limit of society, in the hope that one day it will reach the private investment capital country.
You need to wonder where it's coming from?
In the case of other EU countries, then poverty passes from one country to another or it never arrives, since Chinese, Indians, Brazilians, Turks, Moroccans, Egyptians or Africans continue to work for a tiny part of a European wage.
This also affects the whole of Latin America.
Liberal theotheries and the media claim to satiety that the state shouldn't contribute capital to the economy, that directing the economy leads to its failure.
Privacy defends nail-and-to-to-to-throw the thesis that the state should not intervene in the economy by meddling in control.
Below it is necessary to wonder whether private capital does not influence its selfishness in the political march and, therefore, throughout the state.
In this case the answer is clearly yes.
Proof is the existence of lobbies, in very powerful and ubiquitous practice in every state.
The result is a desperate situation that manifests itself in the area of anti-bloody, in mutual favors, in legislation that is practically reprehensible everywhere but is not punishable.
In Germany the situation is as follows: State ministries, in the absence of financial means, entrust the drafting of bills to private legal firms, which are often linked to industry.
These kinds of bills are then approved in the Bundestag, the German parliament.
In this way, all the power does not emanate from the people, as announced in the constitutions of Western democracies, but from the strong financial groups that represent their own interests.
It is obvious that democracies with a liberal orientation are quickly found in a situation such as that described by Appiános in his work The Crisis of the Roman Republic in Caesar and Pompeii: "The state had been disintegrating for a long period of time and the administrations dealt forcefully."
In exchange for bribes, illegitimately obtained favors and help to finance stones or swords.
Bribery and corruption spread unimpeded and the people went to the polls with the vows in advance..." "there were no people with character who aspired to a position in the administration, so once, because of this disorganization, the state spent eight months without consuls..." "He began to talk seriously that the only remedy for such a serious situation was autocracy and that it was necessary to choose an energetic man..." Although Appiános thought of Pompeii, Caesar rose to power and transformed democracy forever into autocracy.
The conclusion, in the same way as in antiquity, is that today's society relies on inconsiderate preferences of individual benefit without taking into account common interests.
Privacy is not able, in its essence, to grasp and reclaim the profits of society as a whole.
The result is the same as that at the time, an unprecedented decline of the elite, without an effort being made to reform it in depth.
The causes of the rise of fascist and commun-communist regimes must therefore be sought in the unbridled liberalization of economic systems in the 19th and 20th centuries.
With respect to the current situation, it is possible to consider the disappearance of these systems for the benefit of a liberal democracy, as a definite pause, waiting for the next blow.
The really demolishing fact is that the current elite completely ignores the loss of hundreds of thousands in lives, social and humanitarian catastrophes to which we have witnessed, as well as crimes against humanity, as we know them in ancient and most recent history.
Suspension of the law on public adjudications isn't the solution - at least not in the long term.
Under the pressure of economic competition, both at international level and within the EU level, the government of the Czech Republic will be forced to continue looking for other avenues to reduce the social level of the population.
This trend is therefore systemic.
The solution involves concrete socio-political reforms, which reinforce both the participation of state capital and the influence of the citizens in the government and weaken the monopoly position of private capital in society for the benefit of the state.
