Argo Movie: If it gets rough, call Hollywood
In November 1979 a group of the Islamist student demonstration occupied the American embassy in Tehran and took 52 graduates as hostages.
His release would be in exchange for the dethroned shah Mohamed Réza Páhlevi, who after the revolution fled to the U.S., the country that had sponsored his regime for decades.
For the U.S. administration the situation did not offer any positive perspective: to throw shah overboard it could not be allowed, since, if it did, it endangered the trust of its allies in other countries.
But the liberation of the hostages in Iran, where the revolution had led to the constitution of the theocratic regime, was not within its reach.
It was a blow to the prestige of the United States, which was subsequently further intensified with the failed attempt to forcibly release the hostages.
Jailed diplomats were liberated 444 days after the Algerian government's negotiating intervention.
In the U.S., his fate caused a wave of solidarity and animation against Iran.
The failure in Iran contributed decisively to Jimmy Carter's defeat against Ronald Reagan in the 1980 presidential election.
The film "Argo" by director and actor Ben Affleck theaters an episode of this story, which reaped a little success for America.
Shortly before occupying the embassy, six employees escaped from the building.
After a tricky journey, they ended up at the Canadian embassy &apos; s residence.
The CIA, in collaboration with the Canadian authorities, managed to get them out of Iran, for this he helped an undercover tale an extravagant point: they came out with Canadian passports, such as members of the film team that examined the location in the country for the shoot of a sci-fi super-production.
Combination of genres
The plan outlined by "ex-progression" expert Toni Mendez required the collaboration of Hollywood.
To make the lying seem truthful, it was written about the cinematographic project in specialized magazines, press conferences were organized, the fictitious production society even had a real office.
Excertent details of the operation were kept secret for a long time, the film has been made from the memories of Tony Mendez.
Affleck's film is a unique combination of various genres.
On the one hand, a realistic political thriller; on the other, a so-called "caper movement," the story of an episode that went well, a deception (an example can be the Ocean's Eleven series).
The film combine moods. On the one hand we find well-documented sequences of Tehran on the one hand. The credit sequence shows iconic photographs of the time of the events, which were reflected the same way in the film; there are not many differences in this regard.
On the other hand, typical Hollywood scenes somewhat lighter, marked by irony and a slight exaggeration.
And in addition, scenes from the  CIA headquarters and other institutions: men dressed that analyze the situation at the negotiating table, in the corridors of the office, on the phone...
Ben Affleck has managed to reboot his career in an excellent way.
The actor, who had been mocked, has become a respectable director and his way of acting has ceased to be the target of ironic comments.
Argo is his third feature film, after "Goodbye, Goodbye" (2007) and the thriller "The City of thieves" (2010).
At the same time, it's Affleck's first film in which the action takes place in the city of Boston, where he was raised.
Likewise, the sensibilities of grasping the atmosphere of the place was a feature that placed the directors of old movies above Hollywood standards.
And Affleck demonstrates it in Argo, where Tehran "wreeks" in Canada.
The best scenes of the film take place in the streets, in the reconstructive events. The introductory sequence of the embassy takeover is impressive for its synoptic capacity that, at the same time, conveys the emotion of bewilderment and the lack of foresight that arises when, somewhere, history takes a step forward.
Affleck and his collaborators achieve a similar effect on the fictitious scenes (the impostor team in the Tehran bazaar).
Too much action in too many places
The director had to accept the fact that an explanatory story doesn't offer much in the cinematic sense of the most intriguing scenes.
And he manages quite well. Sometimes he adds something to the reality: it doesn't all come out so elegantly here (the scene in which he lurks the crisis at Tehran's airport, the diversion of a call to America and the unbridled quest behind his clue become crazy).
Argo's weakness is the lack of control that arises from the need to show too much action in too many places.
Alan Arkin and John Goodman have a very hilarious role as Hollywood collaborators, although their characters deserved more space and are by no means the only ones in this film.
For these reasons, Affleck's film loses a little dramatic stroke, but it is a film that can be followed with a moderate interest, appreciating its realization and the retro style of a thriller that evokes the 1970s.
But if it's collapsing, it's going to be difficult.
As an evocation of a real story and as proof of how exaggerated the notions of omnipresence and omniscience of the secret services may be, it is enough.
Balloon, banana and circus blogging rules
The server www.bankovnipoplatky.com, which every year presents a survey of the most absurd bank rates, has now decided to announce the contest on "the most absurd regulations or ideas of the EU workshop."
"It occurred to us after the last event, the EU plans to approve a 40 percent share of women in leadership positions in big European companies," explained Právo Patrik Nacher, the organizer of the survey.
Nominating absurdities include, for example, the European court's recent decision to unify insurance policies between men and women.
Until now, women are ahead of better prices in life insurance because they objectively pose a lower risk.
"Other incredible ideas from the EU workshop can be proposed by people until the end of this year."
The election will take place at the end of February 2013," Nacher said.
Controversial EU regulations include, for example, the mandatory addition of biocomponents to fuel which, as a result, damages the environment; another ban is lifelong thermometres of mercury thermometers only because they contain a small amount of a dangerous substance or the tire cage-sletter guideline, which has contributed to a considerable increase in the price of eggs this year.
It has been criticised for the factual ban on using the term "butter locker" or removing the classic bulb from the shops.
A quality banana must be 14 centimeters long.
The apparatus of the Union makes such decisions often under the pressure of one or the other commercial or industrial lobby, whose requests in Brussels are normally defended by some state or group of states (the Czech Republic, even with the threat of a veto, vindicates the demands of its banks).
The interests of a lobbyist were behind the request, for example, that higher-than-equity bananas should have a length of at least 14 centimeters in the EU and could not present "abnormal curves."
The European Commission defended itself by saying that this only seeks to harmonise non-homogenised national rules to date, which only complicate their marketing.
The rules referring to fruit and vegetables in the EU, despite the opposition of some states, have softened on the basis that the existing directive leads to a waste of groceries.
The nominee to winning the poll could be last year's U.S. prescribing that inflatable balloons being marketed it is mandatory to note that children under the age of eight are not allowed to inflate without the supervision of an adult.
The U.S. here was referring to an American study according to which, when talking about toys, balloons are the leading cause of infant death by suffocation.
There is also a similar restriction applicable to the use of matasuegrins by children as young as 14 years of age.
Curious events arise even in our country
Equally unpleasant is the regulation concerning European civil servants: anyone who has an official function in the EU cannot use the denomination of Macedonia. In order not to hurt the sensitivities of Greece, we must talk about FYROM (Former Yugoslav Republic of Macedonia).
Server Bankovnípoplatky.com, in collaboration with the Association of Liberal Economists Laissez Faire, nominated in the survey, among a few pre-appropriate proposals mentioned above, the Union's pre-prescription on the volume of food supplies in the territory of a Member State.
The EU determined the maximum volumes of food supplies that can be found on the territory of the Czech Republic on the same day of our entry into the EU.
The Czech Republic subsequently surpassed, for example, the volume established for canned mushrooms, posing a threat of a substantial fine.
The organizers of the survey were also interested in the occurrence of paying those countries that do not have access to the sea and the idea of granting a subsidy for the application for grants.
These kinds of ideas did not arise in Brussels but in Prague.
"We're at a disadvantage because we don't have a sea.
We are asking the European Union for compensation," the then agriculture minister Jaroslav Palas (SSD) said in autumn 2004.
He argued with the fact that in our country there was plenty of cereal and, as a result of the so-called intervention purchases, the state had full warehouses and it had to be exported.
According to Palas, since the Czech Republic is away from seaports, the EU should pay us hundreds of millions of euros.
Ultimately, the European Commission expressed its readiness to assist the Czech Republic and organized a public competition for the purchase of cereal from countries that do not have access to the sea.
The grant to receive a grant was proposed to the municipalities by the ministry for local development headed by Minister Pavel Nomec (US-DEU). Specifically, it is intended for the drafting of Brussels grant applications.
EU: Rare Predations Are an Excerpt
The guidelines of the Member States are often the target of criticism, the EU's efforts on regulations and also a more effective operation and development throughout the Union deserve, according to various experts, our recognition.
According to the experts, the most serious problem is, for example, the collection of EU subsidies for projects that have nothing to do with the deepening of European integration, but that Member States have achieved in budget negotiations.
The Czechs also have the sensitive fifiber, in the same way as to other countries of the Union, the struggle for the right to the designation of origin of their traditional products, even if our country has not always emerged victorious from these struggles.
The Czechs, after six years of controversies with Germans and Austrians, successfully defended Olomouc's requisition name, but they had to change the name of national rum, whose tradition in our country dates back to the 19th-century, and was renamed "tuzemák."
Only the products made from distilling sugar cane may be called rum, but not those made from sugar beet.
In the official list of proprietary designations of origin of the EU, together with feta and gorgonzola cheeses known around the world, the marzipan of the German city Lébeck or the ham of Parma, you can already find for example the cookies of Karlovy Vary, the tent of Pohoelice and of Tiebo and, even, the variety of hop lyps.
As proprietary maras of the EU, you will be able to be proud of Pardubice ginger bread and the barquillos of Hosice v Podkrkono-í.
"People want me to save the republic, but I'm an amateur," says Okamura
Mr. senator, how did you ever think of running for election for president?
It's not about being a senator or president.
If in our country everything was okay, then I wouldn't present my candidacy.
But I can no longer bear to see it steal in the last twenty years in our country, how thieves go unpunished, taxes and retirement age are raised.
I didn't have ambitions to be a politician.
"When I see something I don't like, I'm trying to come up with a solution to change it.
And since I'm already forty years old and my candidacy is independent, the only possibility I have to change things is to introduce myself as a senator or president.
He's already gotten to the Senate, but shortly afterwards he's on his way to the presidency.
Aren't you knock your back on those people who voted for it?
Always said that my candidacy for the presidency depended on results in the senate elections.
Later I specified that if I was elected a senator, I would present my candidacy for the presidency.
But my goal isn't the function; the function is the tool with which I can realize my own ideas.
That's why I need as much influence as possible and the strongest possible mandate.
The problem isn't just that we're a nation we criticize at the bar or in front of the TV, we trample on anyone who wants to initiate a change.
Added to this is the media, which disapcredit and confuse freedom of expression with the freedom to lying.
It's said I've bribed journalists or that I've been an advisor to Jisi Paroubek.
Let's talk about his ideas.
He presents himself to the presidential fight with financial thesis and criminal responsibilities of politicians and the declaration of the property above twenty million.
To do this you have to change the law.
As a president he doesn't have this competence and only the senator as a whole can propose laws.
How do you wanna figure it out?
As a citizen, I have been part of a pressure group so that the profession of guide can be recognized as self-serving, and we get it.
The problem lies in politics: if someone arrives with a good proposal, whether it's the right-wing or from the left, it is rejected on purpose and this affects the citizens.
As an independent nominee, I have the greatest potential to garner support from all parliamentary groups.
I have the ability to choose something from every game that is better for our country and implement it.
Do you rather consider yourself a man of the right or from the left?
From the Czech point of view, I have the impression that people consider me more on the left.
It doesn't matter to me, whether it's a bit of a left or a bit of right.
What I don't care about is let's get through.
I don't care if somebody's on the right or left, I wanna unite people.
Always openly support any good solution, regardless of whether the proposal comes from the KS-M or the SDG and, in the same way, I'll be against bad ideas.
Joking when you're said about you who's a populist.
But isn't he proving it with what he's saying?
When you create a business plan for the company, you also have certain objectives and ideals.
And you're trying to get closer to them.
There will be those who call it populism, but all the proposals I'm talking about already work in the world or well-known pundits talk about them.
But without the support of the parliament he only has slogans left.
And with that he cannot survive in long-term politics.
Or do you think that if you go to the people and talk to them, you'll get, for example, criminal and financial accountability?
I don't have a choice.
Convincing politicians, reporters and civil society and trying to put them on my side to achieve this.
If he were elected president, there would be no problem in organizing a live broadcast on television in which he would ask the leaders of parliamentary parties to legitimize criminal and financial responsibility for politicians, officials, judges and prosecutors of the state.
Eventually they'd have to communicate why they don't want it.
"If there's a tough character who points to the disputes, you can put pressure on the political scene.
Let us take the direct election of the president, which has been an achievement of the public pressure, on a case-by-case basis.
At the the same time I say I'm just an amateur, I'm neither a genius nor an intellectual.
For my opinions and ideals I try to seek allies.
I have just started in politics and I try to get as much support as possible for my program.
I'm trying to get things ahead of myself, but in case it doesn't go well, I'll quit in six years and go back to the private sector.
It sounds a bit like Okamura is going to save the Czech Republic.
I don't be a saviour.
I don't know I'm doing anything with a person, that's why I've visited a number of well-known personalities, in case they might be encouraged to run for the senate.
I went to see Radim Janáura, but he refused because he had a lot of work.
On the other hand, at least I have supported investigative journalist Jana Lorencová, who brought to light helm oil stokes.
I presented myself on the basis that people are really dissatisfied, now I'm on the balance.
Sixteen per cent of the population did not go to the polls and, those who did, voted for the representatives of the establishment.
In the senator we're two from the independent sector.
"People chose the senate, so it's going to be tricky to achieve change.
However, I will defend my own ideas, such as the direct election of mayors or governors.
Are you considering forming your own party?
I haven't already considered it because I don't have time for it, to check the blameless past of each of the potential members of the party, nor do I have any money.
I don't even have any money for the campaign for the presidency, there's only 20,000 kronor in my transparent account.
You don't have any money?
You're talking about property statements, what's yours like?
I esteem my private property at about $60 million.
In Prague I have a land valued at 25 million, a flat valued ten million, another apartment valued at eight million, a collection of works of art with a value of ten million, an Aston Martin of about 3.5 million and an koda Superb valued at one million, and in the account I have a couple of millions.
By the way, I have an Aston Martin because it was my dream of youth. I've always liked James Bond, who was driving this car, was gallant with women and, at the same time, struggled with evils and injustices.
You're driving an Aston Martin, your property is valued at $60 million and you don't have money for the campaign?
You say that you want to change the republic but you don't want to put too much money on your part.
It's not very convincing.
I don't have $15 million for the campaign.
Shall I borrow?
I've already kicked $2.5 million in the campaign.
The fact I don't have any sponsors shows that there's not too much of an interest in my program.
I don't have an obligation to repay the campaign with money out of my pocket.
The expenses of the campaign essentially cover what I will receive as a senator's salary.
But I wouldn't be able to live off it. For example, I wouldn't be able to give my son the English school, which costs 30,000 kronor a month.
If I just wanted to make money, then I wouldn't stand as a candidate.
So you're gonna get on with your entrepreneurship so you can live?
Didn't you say before that you were gonna quit?
This has nothing to do with a higher salary.
As I've promised, I've partially quit my activities.
For example, the representative of my travel agency will become my replacement in the spring.
People would want him to be the Samaritan to save the republic.
But I also need to live from something.
How used to earning monthly as an entrepreneur?
Between 200,000 and $400,000 that I keep winning.
"If you were president, then I'd quit your business.
You can consult the entire interview in Saturday's edition of Práva.
Architecture firm MVRDV shows that the true adventures are not only in their heads - in the example of the Spijkenisse library and the recently completed "buyer mountain" - 2 photos
"I don't think the building's fun, it looks futuristic, and there's finally something interesting to see," says Lisette Verhaig, a passer-by who passes through the street.
Stefan Spermon, an IT technician at a large company located close to the library, points out: "In addition, the building is nice."
But I'm wondering why we need a library these days.
We've all got the Internet, tablets and e-books.
No one enters those old-fashioned past libraries, doesn't it?
Spijkenisse, a bedroom town located at the gates of Rotterdam who barely deserves a fleeting visit, bears the dubious honour of having risen with a record to forget.
The population of 80,000 has the lowest literacy rate in the Netherlands.
In order to counteract this embarrassing situation, a few years ago it was decided to contribute in some way to the general culture and to copy the seven fictitious bridges represented in euro banknotes in the form of decorated reinforced concrete miniatures.
The success of the educational offensive was limited.
That's how the policy makers decided that there was only one possibility to improve the stats: we had to build a library.
Winy Maas, of the Dutch architecture studio MVRDV, a teacher in the art of bar diagrams and responsible for fun buildings at the same time cynical, took the theme with his usual serenity and appeared at the presentation of the contest in 2003 with five books under his arm and a smile on his face.
And as the jury looked perplexed around and shrugged his shoulders, Maas proceeded to stack the books by size forming a pyramid and concluded his brief speech with the following words: "Dear fellow citizens:
Here you have it, my proposition for Spijkenisse's mountain of books, the so-called "Boekenberg."
Nine years later, the 30-billion-euro mount rises on the planned site.
It is part of the revitalization project, which is completed with an underground car park, a supermarket, a post office and a pair of housing and townhouse buildings.
At the beginning of November, the Lies Mountain was honored with the second "Best Library of NL 2012." award.
In addition, the project is also nominee for "Dutch National Wood Award 2012."
In this way, the unhistoric small city, which to date had nothing more to offer than a post-modern pedestrian area and an incredibly ugly town hall after whose white facade one would intuit rather a dairy plant, now has a work of contemporary architecture.
But above all, Spijkenisse now has the first public cultural building in its history.
The Long Way to the Book
The first impression is that it's a paradise of books under a quesera.
It is also true that there is an elevator that transports us right to the center of this mountain massif, but the true lovers of space and reading do not use it, but prefer to merge with their topography every step of their climb to the top.
The fully glazed interior is brilliant and user-pleasing with the view, the brick's stitch and the elegant lanterns unequivocally support the public condition of the library.
The urban mood is perfect.
By raising the view you can visualize the park's benches, dogs and boys and girls playing soccer.
And everywhere books, books and more books.
"Normally the shelves of books are arranged along the facade and in the center opens a huge dark room that is usually somewhat uncomfortable and impersonal," says Winy Maas.
What we've been doing is turning the classic spatial configuration upside down and taking the reading area from the inside to the outside.
The indoor of the mountain is very well exploited: in the middle there are the offices, the Internet library, the chess club, the environmental education center and the maintenance room.
Special Mention deserves the black shelves that assume the functions of mural cladding, parapet and railing at the same time.
The sight, touch and smell are bizarre.
Even architects and civilian engineers all the day shook their heads when they hear about this unknown material.
"We wanted to work with recycled materials," explains Joop Trouborst, project manager in the city of Spijkenisse.
And that's the way one day, on a frisia farm, we ran into the right by-product from agriculture.
For many years now, in greenhouses and in the fields of the Netherlands a plastic fabric of a few millimeters of thickness has been used as a base to hold the roots of the crops.
It's an inexpensive and efficient material.
This thin tissue lasts two seasons and then seizes.
For the library this fabric was presected, for the first time in such quantities, to obtain plates of four centimeters thick.
With the heat and the pressure, the so-called agricultural plastic (KLP) changes color and becomes a dark, homogeneous and resistant material that smells like a mixture of new car and sports shoes.
Behind 105 steps you reach the top of the mountain.
At the end of this continuous route of almost 500 meters around the mountain the reward awaits us: from the cafeteria you not only have a fantastic view of the city, but in it you can also enjoy tasty Dutch croquettes and its ficus planted in huge pots.
These create a pleasant environment while maintaining the appropriate atmospheric humidity for this mountain of literature.
Donations for a new soul
"Although it's difficult to believe given its large glazed surfaces, this building is an exemplary project when it comes to ecology," Trouborst says.
Necessary heating and cooling are obtained from geothermal energy.
Even in the sunshine sunshine for a few hours in its interior. It is located under a huge pyramid of glass, even on the sun's sunshine only shines for a few hours inside.
The enormous wooden fences located in a transverse direction to the glass facade serve to shade and absorb most rays from the sun.
This creates a comfortable indoor climate.
The rest is a matter of automatic shutters.
Stefan Spermon, the skeptic worker of the Information Technology sector, has already dared to set foot in the new library.
And Lisette Verhaig has also been there.
Chinese traditional medicine professor Cynthia Bogarde has also visited the library. She describes the "Boekenberg" as Spijkenisse's "lapse soul."
The reason is that, on the occasion of the inauguration that took place a couple of weeks ago, all the inhabitants of the city were invited to donate a book from their private collection.
In this way it is to fill, for the time being, the optical holes of a library that does not yet have its entire archive; it currently has 70,000 copies.
Consequence has gone deep.
The shelves are packed.
"There's nothing worse than a half-empty-eating library," says architect Winy Maas.
"I believe that thanks to our initiative now all the inhabitants have a certain link to this building.
Everybody knows that his book is part of the library.
He's just there to adorn himself.
In this way, the MVRDV study has achieved every success in this reigning discipline that in specialized slang is known as "the creation of an identity."
The city of Spijkenisse has written a chunk of the history of Literature.
Regardless of how young and uneducated it can be.
At last a starting point to shape your identity.
Szabo: "The Germans must play a more leading role"
Germany has abstained in the vote on Palestine's admission.
According to Stephen Szabo, an expert on U.S.-Europe relations, this puts Berlin in a fairly tricky diplomatic position.
Deutsche Welle: Earlier this week, Germany hinted that it would vote against a proposal to grant the Palestinian territories observer State status of the United Nations.
Eventually, however, Berlin abstained.
- Why?
Stephen Szabo: Germany doesn't support what the Israelis have done in Gaza.
However, because of the country's special relationship with Israel, Germany must be careful.
But at the same time I don't think he supports the American position either.
Germany wanted to demonstrate its independence without being too critical of Israel.
During the uprising that took place in Libya in March 2011, Germany also abstained when it used to establish a no-flight zone.
It has finally been declared by NATO.
Is it difficult for Germany to take a clear position when important international issues are at stake?
Yeah, that's right.
That's because it's currently reorienting its foreign policy, moving away from a policy led, so to speak, by the United States and focusing on a German foreign policy.
And what makes things even more difficult is that Europeans do not have a coherent and uniform policy.
Therefore, the Germans are at a crossroads.
They are expected to play a more independent role, but are not accustomed to doing so.
I think they're still bragging what their role will be, but they've already embarked on the road to a "normal" foreign policy.
A foreign policy similar to countries such as France or Great Britain.
What does a "normal" foreign policy from the German point of view actually mean?
It is a policy that, when it comes to national interests, is ready to defend positions other than those of the United States or its European partners.
I believe that German foreign policy faces a strong motivation in economic policy, i.e. on exports and its relations with certain regions, such as Russia, China or the Middle East.
Germany's economic interests are somewhat different from those of other great powers and that is why Germany must protect its interests.
Have these economic interests influenced the position taken with regard to the conflict in the Middle East and the UN vote?
On the one hand, Germany boasts large markets in the Middle East, especially in the Gulf.
For this reason it must be careful and not alienate from Arab public opinion or the Arab elites.
It's important, of course.
But I wouldn't give it so much importance because it's not a totally one-sided relationship.
However, they do play a fundamental role in German considerations.
Has Germany undermined its relationship with the U.S. by refraining from voting in important decisions such as the vote on Palestine?
I believe that in Europe, and even in the United States, the German position enjoys an enormous degree of comprehension.
For this reason, I don't think there's such a drastic rupture as in the case of Libya.
Maybe with that Germany has managed to gain some respect.
Ultimately, all of this has only shown that it is a country they take seriously as an international player and whose interests need to be taken into account.
There are differing views on the Palestinian initiative in Europe.
On the other hand, the U.S. has been clearly in favor of a veto.
Is there a disparity of opinion between the U.S. and many European nations?
There have always been such disparities due to US domestic policy.
I think the Obama administration secretly shows a great understanding towards the European position.
But, because of the political situation here, it is clear that the government cannot publicly defend that position.
I don't think the true disparity of opinion really has the scale attributed to it.
If we look at the relations between Obama and Israeli Prime Minister Netanyahu, we see that Obama isn't excited at all about the policy he develops.
Is it difficult for Germany to reconcile its close relations with Israel and the United States, on the one hand, with the position of its main partners in the EU, on the other?
I think that's precisely what makes it so difficult for the Germans.
Of course it would be a little easier for the Germans if there were a coherent and homogeneous European policy, but this is not the case.
Therefore, they do not have the possibility to be part of a superior entity, but they must drive things forward from their own position.
That's precisely what they're now doing with the euro.
I believe that in the future Germany will assume a leading role in forcing Europe to adopt a European position.
Of course this isn't easy for Germany, either simply because of its relationship with Israel.
This has always been a tricky topic.
But I don't think the Germans are clear that they must play an independent role.
Does Germany see itself as an important international actor? Does Germany really want to assume a leading role?
Or maybe you're still finding it hard to accept that leadership?
Germany still hasn't gotten accustomed, it's always uncomfortable and, for obvious reasons, it's still struggling to play a bigger role.
If we look at the crisis in the euro, for example, we see that every time Germany is positioned as a lead actor, certain anti-German feelings are unleashed.
This isn't easy for the Germans.
It's an old problem and it's that nobody wants to be surrounded by enemy countries.
Seen in this way, Germany is in a much tougher position than the U.S.
It must show a receptive attitude towards the different neighbors and opinions and that is not easy.
U.S. influence on European policy is diminishing increasingly, but at the moment the EU doesn't cover that vacuum, who's left then?
The Germans will have no choice but to assume a more leading role.
Even if they don't like it, if it's unpleasant or if it turns them into an even more unpopular town, c'est la vie.
Stephen Szabo is Executive Director of the Transatlantic Academy in Washington, an institution in which academics and policy experts from Europe and North America jointly discuss the challenges facing the transatlantic community.
Szabo is also a member of the German Marshall Fund, where he specializes in German policy, U.S. foreign policy and transatlantic relations.
'Camp Brand Protection' in China: when Puma and Armani suddenly become Chinese brands
Armani is a world-renowned brand and Polo Ralph Lauren too.
- What's Armani Polo?
Behind this name hides a denomination officially registered in China but which has nothing to do with the original companies.
However, it enjoys protection as long as the real eponyms do not file a lawsuit.
And even then it's not clear that they're going to be given the reason.
"It's more and more difficult for foreigners to protect their brands in China," says Thomas Pattloch, a lawyer at law firm Taylor Wessing specializing in protection rights violations in the Far East.
A new case arrives at my desk every week.
Pamphes imitators simply add a couple of letters so you can register your marks.
In this way, Gucci becomes Lu-Gucci and instead of Prada registers Prada-Kny.
The German companies are also legally copied in the same way, as has been the case with sports goods maker Puma.
Pattloch unveils an archivist in which he keeps seats from Beijing's trademark office.
On September 14, 2010, a Chinese company requested to register the Zegna DF Puma brand, an artificial name that was also used from the Ermenegildo Zegna fashion house.
That the Chinese are world champions in copying and infringing intellectual property rights is plentifully known.
In the big cities there are department stores of various plants dedicated exclusively to the sale of forgeries.
Pattloch &apos; s cases, however. On behalf of his clients, he struggles against the fact that the trademark office officially grants Chinese companies the right to take advantage of a denomination that is already registered elsewhere.
The Spaniards call it "Bang Ming Pai" or an accompanying brand.
The word has its origin in the Chinese term "Bang Dakuan,"
that refers to women who have "swept" a wealthy man.
Chinese authorities don't feel culprits
"It's detrimental to the business, you have to fight against it," Pattloch demands.
"The brand makes waters, the monopolistic position disappears and the damage that the image suffers is enormous."
The financial losses and the procedural costs of the sectors concerned are million-dollar, especially for luxury iconic products.
According to data from market analytics company CLSA, with a volume of 15 billion euros a year China is, in size, the third luxur luxury goods market that is growing at the fastest pace.
However, achieving the annulment of inscriptions of dubious legality in the registration of trademarks is complicated and also involves a lot of money.
Processes can last up to nine years and the result is uncertain.
Pattloch speaks of cases in which the court dismissed a lawsuit because it had taken so long that the name subject to the lawsuit had already turned into a "challenge."
"If the plaintiff is unlucky, he may even have to pay the plagiver money because the process has damaged his brand in China," Pattloch says.
Occasionally this is jungle law.
Some of the most well-known cases relate to graphic elements.
In 2009, Daimler-Benz lost a lawsuit against construction machinery maker Sany, the company that has recently been sprung up by the German concrete-bomb maker Putzmeister.
That's why today the Chinese can continue to use an emblem very similar to the Mercedes star.
Volvo's Buyer Geely initially wore a blurwhe-and-blue logo relinquishing BMW; the matter settled in court and Geely had to amend it.
Clothing maker Lacoste lost in China against imitators from Hong Kong and Singapore whose logo depicted a crocodile looking in the opposite direction to the original.
Chinese authorities don't feel culspect.
The CTMO trademark office in Beijing recognizes that in 2010 they have experienced difficulties due to a shortage of equipment and staffing.
Last year, however, "the occupational emergency situation returned to normal."
As a result, unprocessed claim processes has been reduced by 22 per cent.
Almost 57,000 of these cases were resolved, 75 per cent higher than the previous year.
However, 81,500 claims are still outstanding.
Struggling this costs a lot of money.
As is often common in China, the figures are surprising.
Last year CTMO registered more than 1.4 million applications for brand protection, almost a third more than in 2010.
It would be a new record and it means that China leads the list of new enrollments around the world for the 10th consecutive year, according to the trademark office.
It seems that this also happens with the number of valid logos, amounting to 5.5 million in total.
By 2011, 1.8 billion yuan would have been bagged for rights.
In other words, this means that on average each enrollment costs 1,280 yuan or 160 euros.
Struggling this costs a great deal of money, as evidenced by the case of the German family company Freudenberg.
For more than seven years the group has been battling a Chinese impersonator in court.
Although the Germans have managed to stop the illegal manufacture of immechantuary car parts,
The impersonator has succeeded insuring China's Freudenberg-branded rights.
"We ourselves have provoked this situation because in Germany the surnames are unprotected," says Hanno Wentzler, President and CEO of Freudenberg Chemical Specialities in Munich.
The CTMO trademark office also rejected the appeal of the muniques.
Freudenberg has been right in the next two instances, but the opposing side is still fighting today.
You have to be very careful.
Now the case has reached the gates of the High Court of Justice, where it is still pending resolution.
Wentzler is confident he will be able to resolve the issue favorably and praising the professionalism of the courts.
However, he also states that "the procedure is extremely expensive, both in terms of money and time."
It is difficult to estimate the internal costs; the business archive has had to review files up to a hundred years old in order to be able to provide evidence.
Five years ago Freudenberg offered the opposing side a "ssum of six figures in euros" with the aim of reaching a friendly agreement, but it did not succeed.
"That shows just how much we care about it," Wentzler says.
Dances stalking in the Far East even threaten to affect Europe
Especially if imitators are secured the rights over unprotected trademarks there.
Thus, a Chinese manufacturer tried to register the name Freudenberg for shoes and leather products in Germany.
For a long time the group abandoned this commercial sphere, however it was able to prevent registration.
"You have to be very careful," says Wentzler.
Both he and lawyer Pattloch recommend that German companies be extremely cautious when it comes to doing business with China.
It is not enough to rely on the international legislation on trademark rights, but what needs to be done is to "register also in China everything that can be susceptible to protection," as Wentzler points out.
On the contrary, the topic can be much more expensive than a simple record.
In fact, should Freudenberg lose the last assault of his particular battle, he would then have to repay the anti-reliant licence fees by his own name, as Wentzler explains.
That or in the segment in question we would be kicked out of the market.
World AIDS-fighting Day: Dancing, Singing, Help
In Heidelberg rehears the choir Imbongi and Sufiland the AIDS orphans rejoice
The tale of a link capable of surpassing well over 6,733 kilometers away.
First you start with dancing: cowboy boots, basketball shoes, shoes of heel and moccasins look for the compass on the parquet floor, and find it quickly.
One, two, three, four.
Only then the voices of the singers (contraly, bass, tenor and soprano) begin to rise slowly, swirling, hindering and following each other.
And Fiete Hopf, the 29-year-year-old headteacher, threatens to get almost out of his boxes as he tries to order with his energetic and gentle gestures.
It's Monday in the U.S. Monday in the music room of the Heidelberg Institute of Medical Psychology the Imbongi choir rehearses a new song.
There have also arrived the fifty singers, aged between 23 and 69, from the human geneticist to the master of the house.
"Om'Obani" isn't an easty piece, every choir's voice has a different text and also in a barely-knowledged foreign language: Zulu, a language spoken by eleven million people in South Africa, Botswana, Lesotho, Malawi, Mozambique and in some areas of Swaziland.
Self-help help
The United Nations programme for the fight against AIDS is estimated to exist around 34 million HIV-infected people worldwide.
Twenty-eth 23.5 million live in southern Africa.
There are 245,000 AIDS orphanages in Swaziland.
More than 40 per cent of the population is already HIV-positive.
The Association Voices for Africa has found godfathers in Germany for the 180 AIDS orphans of the village of Esitjeni.
Fifteen of them go to high school.
For 15 or 20 euros a month you can sponsor a kid.
This contribution will cover tuition of the school tuition, the uniform and will guarantee you a hot meal a day at the Gogo Center.
Imbongi "story narrator" or "panegirist" in Zulu.
In this living room no one knows how to speak fluent this Bantu language, but sing they do know.
For ten years this choir rehears songs in this gentle foreign language that sometimes manages to transport them to their place of origin: the south of Africa.
8,733 kilometers away in a straight line from Heidelberg in the north-east of the kingdom of Swaziland, there is a village called Esitjeni that depends on the voices of this German choir.
Forty per cent is infected.
Approximately 2,000 people live there, some in simple mud huts and straw, a large part of them are children.
More than 300 don't already have parents because they've succumbed to AIDS.
Esitjeni is a small example of what affects Swaziland: the highest AIDS infection rate and the lowest life expectancy in the world, according to Unicef.
Circumcision is unreasoned by the population, a practice that has been shown to halve the risk of contagion.
More than 40 per cent of Suziland's inhabitants are carriers of the immunodeficiency virus, and dying at just over thirty-something has become common.
During a joint trip to Africa in the spring of 2005, the choir discovered this remote population, but what they saw above all were many children on the streets who lacked not only the protection of their parents, but of the main: food, clothing and training.
Without a school degree, the odds in a poor country are more than sloppy.
Initially it was only the particular commitment of a few that made it possible for a child to go out to school and enjoy a hot meal a day.
However, a year later the choir founded the "Voices for Africa" association that has since professionally taken care of AIDS orphans in Esitjeni.
Facts about sexually transmitted infections
What's the main sexually-transmitting diseases?
STDs of bacterial origin include syphilis, chlamydia and gonorrhea.
The most common Viral-origin STDs are AIDS, human papilloma virus, genital herpes or hepatitis.
Thirks and scabies are among STDs transmitted by parasites.
What are the main groups of those affected?
Syphilis and gonorrhea occur more often in men who have sexual intercourse with other men.
The Robert Koch Institute assumes that at least four out-of-five syphilis cases recorded in Germany originate from sexual contact between men.
In the case of heterosexual adults the most common sexually transmitted diseases are chlamydia infections, triarchies, the human papilloma virus, candidiasis (fhongo) and gonorrhea.
The spread of HIV among heterosexual adults in the country is relatively rare, but approximately 20 per 20 per cent of new HIV infections are recorded in this group.
Among young people, chlamydia infections are much more common than in other population groups.
According to European surveys, three quarters of all infections affect young people between the ages of 15 and 25.
In our country, the human papilloma virus is also added to the most common diseases among young people.
How has the number of infections evolved?
Not all sexually-extremized diseases are subject to compulsory declarations.
According to the Robert Koch Institute, the number of syphilis infections has doubled from 1,697 cases in 2001 to 3,698 cases in 2011.
The number of new HIV infections has maintained a downward trend since 2007.
Approximately 2,700 cases were registered in 2011.
It's one-tenth less than in the previous year.
What are the symptoms of a venereal disease?
Infectious diseases can cause soccers in the genital area, discomfort when urinating, flow, abdominal pain, as well as blisters or warts.
They often do not cause pain or cause other symptoms and therefore are not detected.
How can one be protected?
Condoms can reduce the risk of contagion but do not offer 100 per cent protection.
Some sexually transmitted pathogenic agents can also be transmitted through dirty and physical contact infections.
For this reason, the experts recommend to undergo regular medical examinations, especially if it is frequently changed from a sexual partner.
If diagnosed early, most STDs can be cured and it is possible to avoid any late consequences.
Through collaborations, donations and, last but not less important, the fees charged by the choir for its performances throughout Germany come to raise quite a lot of money.
"In total we have already shipped about 200,000 euros to Esitjeni," says Annette Lennartz, president of the association.
In Zodwa Dlamini's (self-conscious and endowed woman with leadership) manages the money sent from Germany.
It cares that orphans find a good accommodation, for example with one of the grandmothers.
"Ggogos," as the older women are called in Zulu, are the pillars of the village.
Some of them have up to 14 orphans in their care, offer them a roof where to live and ensure that the children come to the school every day promptly and dressed in their uniforms.
Those who don't have anyone are placed under the protection of Khanyisile, an unmarried woman who, like the two cookers who cook every day for more than 200 hung-hung hungry children, receives a salary from the association.
In addition, "Voices of Africa" has launched a sewing school, built two hens and, in collaboration with the U.S. health organization PSI, has managed to get many of the people's inhabitants tested for HIV.
This isn't an obvious thing, since, regardless of how clearly this disease is across the country, it's not usually openly talking about it.
A king with 14 wives
"AIDS is an absolute taboo," says Annette Lennartz, "because it has to do with sexuality."
Something bizarre in a country where the king officially has 14 wives.
The Last Absolute Monarch of Black Africa, King Mswati III, is known for his opulent lifestyle and filled with excesses.
Polygamy rather than democracy.
This unaccepted form of life is largely responsible for the rapid spread that the HIV virus has experienced in recent decades.
But it is also due to the countless timers who tour the country from one side to the other and carry it wherever they go.
According to Annette Lennartz, "in every corner you can get condoms for free, but these are barely used.
It's another culture, here they only accept skin-to-skin contact."
To promote cultural exchange, the Imbongi choir travels every two or three years through southern Africa singing songs that speak of melancholy, combative spirit, hope and confidence in the black race, songs that many at the southernmost end of the African continent still recall from the apartheid era.
A bus full of whites singing songs in a black language; so much recognition not only brings with it an excellent atmosphere and a lot of joy, but has also managed to tear off a couple of tears from some rabid border soldier.
The journey always brings them to Esitjeni, where the singers visit sponsored children.
Even though this tiny population barely appears on the maps, in the Ezulweni River valley it is well-known.
"Get to Esitjeni, there's the glimpse of the light," says local people.
And after traveling the 7,733 kilometers in a straight line back to Heidelberg to the rehearsal halls of Bergheimer Street full of singers marking the pace, one realizes that it is also possible to see its light shine from there.
Messenger: NASA unveils ice on Mercury
Probe has found evidence of icing on Mercurio.
Probably the ice sheet is up to 20 meters.
NASA, the U.S. space agency, has provided evidence of ice on Mercury.
Despite being the planet closest to the sun, there's iced water there, as can be seen from three studies published on Thursday in the specialized journal "Science."
The Messenger probe reportedly found evidence that an ice sheet has been formed in the area of the planet permanently in the shade.
It looks like it's at least 30 centimeters and probably even up to 20 meters thick.
The water presumably comes from comets or, with a high probability, of asteroids that once collided with Mercury.
"No one relates the ice finding to the possibility that life exists on the planet, says Messenger probe chief investigator Sean Solomon.
Temperatures on Mercury can reach up to 426 degrees Celsius.
The findings, however, could help clarify how water and other components of life have reached other areas of the solar system.
