Drink butter every day and live 168 years
To the south of Azerbaijan many people manage to reach biblical ages.
There's even a museum dedicated to longevity.
Footage of footprints in a country in which 97-year-olds are still considered young.
To the south of Azerbaijan many people manage to reach biblical ages.
There's even a museum dedicated to longevity.
Footage of footprints in a country in which 97-year-olds are still considered young.
The tour of Talysh's mountain range could be described as savage and romantic.
The minibus advances with a great splash through the winding road that passes next to wooded hills, high-flying rivers and the simple houses of local farmers.
It's all green and juicy, you might even think it's in the Black Jungle.
However, we are in the southernmost area of Azerbaijan; from here the border with Iran is just a few kilometers away.
Here live the "talyshi," a village of which you don't know a lot, other than they perfectly dominate the Persian and the Azeri who are very long.
Our ultimate destination is Lerik.
This tiny town boasts of perhaps too massive architecture designed in the Soviet era that doesn't seem to fully fit into such a picturesque mountain landscape.
European tourists barely reach this place, as the road from Azerbaijan's capital Baku is too heavy.
To travel the 323 kilometers that separate both urban centers, it takes eight hours because the road is of only one direction.
The astonishing wealth that the country enjoys thanks to oil from the Caspian Sea has not yet reached this remote region.
However, Pilata Fatulayeva (48) is convinced that Lerik has what needs to be done to become a tourist attraction.
"Baku became famous in May on the occasion of the celebration of the Eurovision Festival and next year we will hold the Festival of the oldest people in the world," says Fatulayeva.
This is the director of the Longevity Museum, perhaps the only one that exists in the world.
Here the lives of eight dozen local talyshis who have reached the age of 100 is documented. Fatulayeva points to a black-and-wheel-white image.
This is my grandfather, who lived 120 years.
His son has died at the age of 136.
The undisputed star of the museum, however, is the shepherd of shepherd Eirali Moss, who seems to have lived 168 years.
However, there's no birth certificate that certifies it.
And if we take into account that the old-fashioned man has lived 122 years, it seems that the age of Msl.mov is quite suspicious.
"He was born in the region in 1805 and he died in 1973," Fatulayeva explains.
The man was wedded on three occasions and had 23 sons and reportedly still had time to conceive one more daughter at the age of 136.
Was there a mistake, then, Erali M?l?mov at his age in a couple of decades?
But Rembrandt Scholz, a gerontologist at the Max Planck Institute in Rostock, has also heard of the advanced age of this Central Asian inhabitant.
"In some regions of China, Japan or the Hunza valley in Pakistan, very old people also live," Scholz says, "even in Sardinia there are a lot of older men."
Due to the lack of documentation, it is impossible to provide scientific evidence about the age; especially where there is no civil registration either.
Every day cups filled with melted butter
It is a fact that, surprisingly frequently, the inhabitants of the region around Lerik often reach a biblical age.
There are currently 20 people over the age of 100.
Why do so many elderly people live here in the south?
Azeri tour guide Farid Mugimzadeh explains that these are the special genes of talyshis.
The director of the museum, Ms. Fatulayeva, instead believes that the cause lies in food.
However, from a nutritional point of view, it doesn't seem logical that the calorie-little diet of the talyshi, which adore meat, bread and especially dairy products and many of which, moreover, drink a glass of melted butter every day, is so healthy.
Or maybe it's the natural way of life that keeps the young people? In Cengemiran, a small settlement not far from the city of Lerik Rubaba Mirzayeva lives Rubaba Mirzayeva.
At the age of 97 he's relatively young for what's stylized by these latitudes.
Mirzayeva, who claims to have 143 descendant descendants, lives in a simple wooden house typical of the entire Caucasus region.
Sitting on the ground, it tirelessly moves a huge tub of butter.
Eight people live under a roof, including one of Mirzayeva's sons and daughter, both of whom became grandfathers a long time ago.
Two young children also leapt back and forth.
In the kitchen they prepare tea for the guests, a tea that is served in some typical bulging-shaped glasses.
Mirzayeva's teeth are white and perfectly lined and under the handkerchief he hides two long braids of dark blonde that his son proudly unsures us.
I've always washed my hair with milk, so I've never dropped it and it's maintained its color.
"I've never worn a shampoo either," says Mirzayeva.
The pension is enough to live.
He's always ate only what he's produced on his own farm: tomatoes, potatoes and peas.
I've never been shopping in my entire life. I've never gone shopping at a supermarket.
Then she's telling us about her husband, who's in the army.
The worst was the post-World War II era.
However, it all returned to its channel when the "dear father," Heydar Aliyew, took control of the situation.
This kind of propaganda sounds weird in an older lady.
However, in Azerbaijan the cult professed to the paternal figure of the nation, who leads his country as a dictator, knows no limits.
He stayed in power until 2003, at which time he ceded the witness to his son Ilham.
At the very least the elders of Azerbaijan don't know what poverty is.
Mirzayeva charges 230 manat (approximately the same amount in euros) as a monthly pension, an amount with which you can live well taking into account the situation here.
And perhaps the gracious son of Mirzayeva is right: "in our culture the elders are highly respected."
They live in a large family, are loved, they receive all the necessary care and they are happy.
As if this wasn't reason enough to stay alive for many years...
"Human rights" are lacking in the Constitution
The revolution has returned to Cairo.
Demonstrations rotating against each other in Cairo highlight the tremendous division in which the country is plunged.
The future constitution, with Shara sharia as a basis, is highly controversial.
Egypt's president spared hype in knitting.
"We must overcome the transition.
And for that to happen is my responsibility, before the people and before God," he said on public television.
His speech was addressed to the whole of the people, especially the Coptic Christians, the liberals, enlightened Muslims and the secular.
All of them, repreheatedly immersed so far in confusing opposition, are scary.
Fear of a theocracy shore of the Nile for the grace of the mighty Muslim community.
Just as Mohammed Mursi explains, almost apologizing, has had to temporarily annul the competences of the Constitutional Court and expand its powers "to be able to save the revolution."
However, neither the Egyptians nor the whole of the world know very well what this 61-year-old engineer with a doctoral degree awarded by the University of Southern California really wants to save.
Do you intend to deprive the judiciary of its power?
In fact, the 234 articles, the approval of which has been enforced by a constituent assembly made up of 100 predominantly Islamist members at a 15-hour nightly meeting, are truly worrying.
As in previous constitutions, the draft also says that jurisprudence is based on "principles of Islamic law."
However, what's "principles" means?
This was and is still open to interpretation and it seems that Islamists are going to take advantage of this vague formulation, as well as the room for legal maneuver it entails, to impose a stricter interpretation of Shariah.
This is at the very least what a new added article suggests: in all Shar sharia issues it is mandatory to consult Al-Ashar University, the most important Islamic institution in the country with great power throughout Sunni Islamism.
That doesn't have to be the case, but this may mean clergy would control the legislation, something that - in fact - would result in incapacitation of the judiciary.
A large chunk of the draft constitution is open to interpretation
What's also problematic is that it's trying to maintain military jurisdiction.
During Mubarak &apos; s dictatorship these courts served to exert a tough crackdown on the opposition.
As many as 11,000 civilians were detained in military prisons after the dictator.
As set out in the draft, the state must protect "the true essence of the Egyptian family" and "promote its morality and values."
It's such an imprecise formulation in legal terms that with the help of this article the state institutions could even control the contents of cinematography and literature.
Speaking clearly, it's nothing but censorship.
Certainly, no article explicitly establishes equality between men and women.
On the other hand he forbids offending or slandering the Prophet Mohammed and his legacies.
But it's not clear what an offense is and how it should be sanctioned.
Equally dubious is the formulation that bans "offense to people"
Was a caricature of the president or a joke about a lawyer enough?
A question of interpretation, like many other things in the draft that has been presented to Mursi for his signature and that, according to his words, "very soon" he will be presented to the Egyptians for a referendum.
"Resumption has come back".
The Opposition has been guzzing for weeks against the superiority of the Islamists.
Ten thousand people gathered in an unussonable cleavage on Friday afternoon in Cary's Tahrir Square and vowed to fail the Act before it can come into effect.
"Revolution has come back and we will end up fierce," says Hamdin Sabbahi, who ranks third on the presidential election lists.
The Nobel Peace Laureate and former Director of the International Atomic Energy Agency, Mohamed El-Baradei, stated that the draft constitution should be "in the garbage bucket in history."
Through Twitter he accused Mursi's followers of wanting to lead a "collective coup against democracy."
"When he convenes the referendum we will go to his palace and overthrow it," says opponent Jasser Said.
"We're stilln't tired, the blood of our brothers still hasn't been expiated," opposition politician Chaled Ali was quoted in the Egyptian media.
And several judges announced that they did not want to supervise the referendum, so it would not be considered valid.
"The Koran is our constitution"
For its part, the well-organized Muslim community called for a counter-demonstigration; in case not in Tahrir Square, but after a prayer on the other side of the Nile, to the University of Cairo.
Many women covered with a veil and supporters of the Salafists took part in the cry of: "People demand the application of the divine law."
They demanded Mursi "clean up the country," while proclaiming: "The Koran is our constitution."
A struggle for the sovereignty of the symbolic Tahrir Square, where it all began, would have provoked a situation similar to that of a civil war.
It's clear that Mursi's supporters didn't want to risk something like this happening.
The Muslim siblings claimed that both those who oppose the draft of the constitution and its supporters would have expressed their opinions loud and clear.
Now it would have been the time to let the people decide in the polls what direction the country should follow.
In this regard, it seems that the draft Islamists is guaranteed the majority.
"The expression 'Human Rights' doesn't even appear"
Hafez Abu Saeda is angry with this forced constitutional process that should actually have lasted until February and included all social forces.
This 48-year-old lawyer specializing in human rights and president of the Egyptian Organization for Human Rights (EOHR) defended the Muslim siblings when they were being held in prison or had to face a trial during Mubarak's dictatorship.
Not because he shared his ideology, but because human rights are indivisible for him.
For this reason he was beaten, convicted and jailed.
"And now the expression "Human Rights" doesn't even appear in the new constitution," he laments in a conversation with the newspaper "Welt am Sonntag."
The extension of Mursi &apos; s powers to all three branches of the state caused this lawyer to re-sign.
The measures taken are flagrant violations of the rules of the democratic game and will lead Egypt to a new dictatorship.
"Instead of strengthening civil society, what the president has done is abolish it," Saeda laments.
Democracy, however, cannot function without civil society organizations.
Saeda feels abandoned, also by the international community, which sees the ideological struggle taking place on the banks of the Nile with a mixture of curiosity and tension.
This could turn against him.
A protester in Tahrir Square warns: "you're conceiving a monster you won't be able to control."
The Norwegian rakfisk: the most smelly fish in the world?
Norway &apos; s five million people enjoy one of the highest standards of living, not only of Europe but in the world.
Is it possible that the success of the country has any relationship with its greed for extremely smelly fish?
Take a series of over-heated cheeses.
Place them in the middle of a mountain of dirty, wet soccer clothes.
Leave it there for a week.
You can then smell the paralyzing rakfisk aroma, one of Norway's delicacies.
I'm in the tiny town of Fagernes, about three hours from Oslo.
Smooth. The scenery is spectacular and that smell, ever-present, floats in the air.
The rakfisk is salted trout that is left to ferment in water during, depending on how smelly you prefer it, up to a year.
"As it gets dark and it gets cooler, the Norwegians flock to a festival that's being celebrated here in Fagernes, dedicated to this delicacy of the most, so to speak, captivating.
"You eate raw and then you drink a glass of aquavit from a drink," says Havard Halvarsen, a city firefighter who is also known as the "Rakfisk General," who is in charge of organizing the festival.
Everywhere we see people eating this fish that is stored in small buckets and sibbling from a drink important amounts of the drink.
"Some people like aquavit better than rakfisk," Havard says.
Drink can kill the smell.
I'm tasting a few pieces.
If you can avoid passing it near the nose, it's not bad; it's not very different from a slice of sushi that you've traveled a few kilometers by bus.
The rakfisk is a product that comes from a very different epoch, in which poverty lasddled Norway and where, before cooling there was cooling, the fish was stored during the autumn submerged in water and salt in hermetic barrels.
Later, in the middle of the winter, when it's already well fermented, it's pulled out and, obviously with the inconsant senses because of alcohol, you eat.
Only a generation ago, thousands of Norwegians were forced to leave their country in search of work, and migrated mainly to the United States.
At present, the population is rising rapidly; more than 13 per cent are immigrants, attracted by abundance of jobs, lofty wages and a complete system of social services.
A sprawling of Sweden's inhabitants, the old rival and until not too much richer than Norway, arrives in this country to work.
The rakfisk is seen as something important, an essential but rather smelly part, of Norway's past.
It is among the most expensive dishes that can be bought.
But, in fact, it's all expensive.
Norway doesn't usually appear on the agenda for international news, and most Norwegians like it to.
People here are still unwilling to utter the name Anders Breivik, the far-left ractitioner who shootered and murdered 77 people, including men, women and children.
Instead, they refer to the shooter as "July 22."
It's very difficult for the Norwegians to believe that in their peaceful country one of their own was able to commit such cruelty and manslaughter.
Behind much of Norway &apos; s current wealth is the growth, since the early 1970s, of one of the world's largest oil and gas industries.
"But oil isn't the only reason we're doing so well," says Anna, our waitress, as rakfisk trays passes and that, with her long blonde mane and amazing blue eyes, she represents the image of Norwegian well-being.
"Let's, as you say, prudent people".
His English, like that of most people in this country is impeccable.
"We don't like bragging, we don't like ostentation".
Norway has managed its oil wealth with a lot of caution; everything but a small percentage of the money from this industry is invested in a special fund for the benefit of future generations.
While everyone squandered money they didn't have during the years preceding the financial crisis around the world, Norway kept its wallet closed.
"As long as we can ski in the winter and hike in the summer, we're happy," Anna says.
"And Eat rakfisk," he adds with an unwrapped laugh.
I'm in snow queuing to eat something; I've already had enough rakfisk.
Now a moose burger is certainly something different and rather succulent to savour.
But in the evening, that smelly fish comes back.
The hotel in which I stay is one of the many venues where a rakfisk dinner is held where dinners vote for the best fish, or perhaps the one that tests the nostrils most.
A television has connected live with a presenter who wears bird and is surrounded by rakfisk dishes.
Looks like the Eurovision contest.
"what's your stitches for the best fish up there in the mountains, Thor-Juergen?"
"Here's our punctuation, Havard".
There's laughter and applause.
A man falls out of the saddle, perhaps beaten by the aquavit.
Either maybe it's the vapours of all that fish.
Mexico: Enrique Peña Nieto Faces a tough start
While Mexico's incoming President Enrique Peña Nieto is preparing to take office, the BBC correspondent Will Grant analyzes the challenges that the new president will face and the different expectations of his population.
Traffic in Mexico City is particularly bad at the moment.
In a city that, under the best circumstances, is already congested, a steel fence has been installed since Monday that cuts several of the main roads that leads to the city and is causing enormous chaos on the roads.
The aim, however, was not to prevent workers from getting to their place of work, but to prevent protesters from reaching parliament.
On Saturday, the new president of Mexico, Enrique Peña Nieto, will receive the presidential band and will assume the management of the nation.
He's facing a tricky task.
Mexico has been achieving good economic results during Felipe Calderón's outgoing government, but the country is immersed in a war against narchocracy that has already claimed about 60,000 lives in six years.
"My government has a great commitment with the Mexican people to reduce the violence," Mr. Peña Nieto told U.S. President Barack Obama earlier this week at the Oval Office.
"I am going to propose a new security strategy that will enable us to achieve that goal."
Prior to meeting with the president of the U.S., Mr. Peña &apos; s previous political experience was as governor of his home state of Mexico.
A very populous and fast-growth state that surrounds the capital; Opinions on the new leader are divided in this territory where he used to move.
A candid man
In the bucoholic city of Valle del Bravo, for example, he is remembered with affection.
Its residents attribute the merit of the growth of on-the-site tourism and the construction of infrastructure.
To get to the city you can drive on one of Mr. Peña Nieto &apos; s new roads, which is a huge improvement compared to the cracked and bamboo-filled roads that were rehabilitated.
You can also see plaques with his name at the entrance of a modern sports centre and an stunning interactive museum on climate change.
"We are confident that he will achieve a real and lasting change," says his friend and political ally Gabriel Olvera Hernandez, a member of Mr. Peña Nieto's party, the PRI.
"Particularly when it comes to security and the economy, we look forward to the interesting and real change that our country so badly needs."
After an uninterrupted period of 81 years in power, the PRI was unleashed by Vicente Fox in 2000.
Representative Olvera admits that, after 12 years outside the presidential palace of Los Pinos, within the party there are many expectations regarding Enrique Peña Nieto.
It also rejects the opposition's qualification of the new president's lack of essence.
"She's a very sincere man, very committed and with an excellent view of the country.
He's a fantastic man of state and, above all, he's a person who knows how to listen.'
On the other side of the state, however, it'sn't the impression that a lot of people have about its former governor.
In Nezahualcoyotl, also known as Ciudad Neza, the contrast with the cobbled streets of Valle del Bravo couldn't be greater.
Hidden beneath elevated stretches of motorway it is, in many ways, a suburb of Mexico City itself.
And the problems of the municipality are also tough and urban.
Earlier this year it was called for the intervention of the military to assist in the fight against drug gangs operating in neighbourhoods and violence against women is particularly serious.
The bodies of do dozens of murdered women have been left abandoned in just the last two years, dozens of murdered women have been left abandoned in just the last two years.
More than 1,000 women were murdered in the state of Mexico while Mr. Peña Nieto was the governor, a much higher rate than that of Ciudad Juárez, unhappily known for his violence and a place that is synonymous with the murder of innocent women.
Critics of Mr. Peña Nieto say, at best, he couldn't address the problem of femicide while in office.
More critics accuse his administration of turning a blind eye.
In a concrete house, typical of the damaged neighborhood, Irinea Buendía has to make efforts to hold back tears as she shows me photographs of her deceased daughter, Mariana Luna.
According to the official version of the events, Mariana committed suicide in 2010.
However, his family believes his partner murdered her.
"When I got to his house, it looked as if his body had been wiped out," Ms. Buendía recalls.
"There were any signs that she had been beaten and the rigor mortis had already appeared."
As his mother recounts the story, a photograph of Mariana watches from the walls, next to a cross reading a single word: Justice.
However, that's exactly what the family say he's been restrained.
"The authorities of the state have treated me as if I were an old gossip, a fuss, a whistle.
What they want is for one to simply accept what they say and shut up."
"But that cannot be the case when there have been so many irregularities and so much negligence," he says.
President Peña Nieto will receive on Saturday will be accompanied by an enormous responsibility.
Tens of thousands of families have been affected by violent crimes in Mexico for the past six years and the new president has vowed to give them priority during his tenure.
"I hope he'll be the same kind of president he was when he was governor," says PRI MP Olvera in Valle del Bravo.
However, that is precisely what the families of the victims of Ciudad Neza most fear.
Bradley Manning didn't report mistreatment, prosecutors say
Prosecutives try to deal with Bradley Manning's abuse allegations during his stay in prison
The trial centers on Manning's stint at Quantico Military Jail in Virginia
Defendants to dismiss the case because Manning's incarceration was stubborn
The Army soldier is accused of stealing thousands of confidential documents.
Prosecutors tried to prove on Friday that Private Bradley Manning, accused of leaking confidential information in U.S. history, misstated multiple opportunities to denounce the ill-treatment he now alleges he suffered while in military detention.
During cross-examination by the prosecution of Manning at a pre-judgment hearing in Ft. Meade, Maryland, prosecutor Commander Ashden Fein stated that records of weekly visits by officers from the unit that Manning received during nine months of detention in Quantico, Virginia, do not show any complaints about the treatment he was receiving.
Interrogation, which was carried out during a hearing following the defence &apos; s request that the case be dismissed since his incarceration had been tough and had already meant sufficient punishment, came a day after Manning had testified that he had planned to commit suicide while in custody.
The army's intelligence analyst, arrested in June 2010, is accused of stealing thousands of confidential documents while on duty in Iraq.
Later, WikiLeaks published the material on the Internet.
WikiLeaks has never confirmed that Manning was the source of information.
At the hearing on Friday, Fein reviewed with Manning the forms that the officers filled out after visits to Manning during his arrest at the Quantico military prison, where he has been imprisoned for maximum detention from July 2010 to April 2011.
The officers reportedly asked Manning's questions and quoting his answers.
When Fein on Friday asked about the forms, Manning conceded that he had rated the treatment he was given wardens as "excellent" and the treatment received at the facilities in general as "very professional."
Forms do not show any allegations of ill-treatment, even though the officers asked Manning directly about the treatment he was receiving, Fein said.
Mr. Manning replied that he had expressed concern about some issues and that the officers visiting him had talked about those problems and had informed him that they would be addressed, but that they did not record those issues in the register.
"They wrote 'no incidents' (because I've talked about the problems) but this doesn't necessarily mean I wouldn't have mentioned any problems," Manning said.
U.S. Army Col. Denise Lind also asked Manning why he hadn't denounced the treatment he was receiving during the January 2011 meeting with a council tasked with examining the suicide intentions he had expressed on a form months earlier.
Manning replied that his intention during that meeting was to obtain the reduction of the "injury prevention" regime under which he was located.
The U.S. military said it had put him under that restrictive regime, which is a degree below surveillance for possible suicide, to ensure its protection and the safety of other people.
"I wanted the staff to know that I was okay and (I wanted to) stop being under injury prevention ... to be able to enjoy what, from my point of view, it's a better quality of life," Manning said.
Manning testified on Thursday of his arrest in Iraq and his transfer to Kuwait, where he was held for almost two months before being transferred to a military prison located at the Marine Base in Quantico, Virginia, in July 2010.
He said he had considered committing suicide in Kuwait and on one occasion lost consciousness because of the heat.
He said the fact that he wasn't allowed to know what's happening to him or what's going on in the outside world was very distressing.
"My world was reduced only to Camp Arafjon to that cage," Manning explained Thursday.
"I didn't think I was gonna die in that cage".
Manning stated that, once transferred to Quantico, he would spend most days in a small cell, at least for at least 21 hours and often for 23 hours, without company.
Manning said he was only allowed to have a mattress, a blanket, slugs, some clothes and his glasses.
He explained that he was trying to move all the time, as it was forbidden to sleep or even lying down during the day.
Manning said he always had a light coming from outside pointing his eyes as he slept.
He said that if wardens couldn't see his face when he turned around at the night, they would wake him to turn around again.
Manning &apos; s attorney in January 2011 filed a formal complaint about Manning &apos; s treatment.
Manning was transferred to Fort Leavenworth Military Prison in Kansas in April 2011.
On Friday the judge asked Manning about a plea he had made in Thursday's statement that, after being forced one night to sleep naked in his Quantico cell, he was forced during a morning rebuck to remain naked standing in front of warlords and other inmates.
Manning had testified that he had never been given the chance to grapple with the blanket during recount.
During the judge's cross-examination on Friday, Manning said he deduced by an order given to him by the zeaker who should drop the blanket with which he could have been covered, but acknowledged that no one had ordered him to drop it.
Manning testified on Thursday that he had been forced to sleep naked the night before because of an attempt to prove to an officer that he didn't pose a danger to himself.
Manning said he had told the officer that he might have woven the elastic of his underwear or flip flops to self-harm but hadn't.
Manning testified that that night his underwear, flip flops and glasses had been removed from his cell.
His lawyers are hopeful that the judge will at least take into account the experiences he went through during his stay in prison and drastically reduce his sentence in case he was convicted in the court martial, which is scheduled to begin early next year.
The defense said it plans to have Manning pleaded guilty to misdemeanor and defending other charges deemed too extreme.
The hearing is expected to resume later this weekend and prosecutors are expected to argue that the conditions of the detention were justified.
The Pentagon has maintained that Manning was retained in accordance with the rules of application to any detainee who is in maximum custody in Quantico.
Charges against Manning include co-existence with the foe, illegally provoking the publication of information on the Internet, leaking national defense information and the theft of ownership or public records.
If he is found guilty of all charges, he could face life in prison.
My Mexican-American Identity Crisis
He claims that many were forced to leave Mexico due to a lack of opportunities
The Mexicans tend to criticize those who left; he says they remember the Mexicans of those tough times
Navarrette says Mexican-Americans are stuck between two worlds.
On a recent trip to Mexico City, he had barely gone down the esplanade and arrived in the immigration processing area and was already completely confused.
Archives indicated the road to two queues: one for "Mexicans" and one for "Extranjeros."
I stayed there for a few seconds without being sure where I had to go.
Having grown up in Central California, all my life I've been called "Mexican."
It's an ethnic typology, just as my friends in Boston refer to themselves as "Irishians" or my friends in New York call themselves "Italians."
Later I made up my "Mexican-American."
But this was Mexico.
In my grandfather's homeland, typography or scripts weren't necessary.
He was just American.
I'm speaking well enough to handle myself during an interview on this language.
But I don't have the vocabulary level of a native and I can't get rid of my American accent.
So I took my American passport and put myself in the queue of strangers.
I thought at that time this week when Mexico's president-in-chief, Enrique Peña Nieto, visited the White House to meet with President Obama.
On the agenda of the day, as always when the leaders of these two countries meet, it consisted: immigration, drugs and trade.
Peña Nieto was also eager to talk about the growth of the Mexican economy, one of the reasons why at the moment it is as likely that a Mexican will stay in Mexico as he ventures to go to the United States.
He wants to partner with the U.S. and Canada and create a European Union-style trade bloc in North America.
In addition, Peña Nieto vowed to continue fighting drug cartels in Mexico, although he did not give details.
For Mexico, the relationship with the U.S. is tricky and full of grudges.
Probably the majority of Americans never remember that in 1848 the United States invaded Mexico and forced its leaders to cede half their territory at gunpoint.
But for the Mexicans, who think of centuries, not in minutes, the elements that remind them are everywhere.
So at a time when a U.S. official says anything that leads to the slightest criticism of Mexico, you begin to hear complaints, both in the press and among the Mexican elite, about how Americans are usurping the sovereignty of their neighbors.
And the sons of Moctezuma are set on a war footing.
And even so, for Mexico, the relationship that really represents a whole challenge lies with the more than 35 million Mexican-Americans living in the United States.
Shall we scramble about grudge?
There's plenty.
In Mexico there are winners and losers, people for whom the country provides opportunities and others for whom this is not the case.
The only reason why there are so many people of Mexican descent living in cities such as Los Angeles, Las Vegas, Phoenix, Denver or San Antonio is that, at some point in our genealogical tree, there was a person, perhaps the father or mother or one of the grandfathers, to whom the opportunity door was closed in Mexico and had to march to the north.
And most of the time that person lacked a certain profile: daring skin, a low level of studies, coming from a poor people, etc.
We're their descenders and we're loyal to them.
Mexico doesn't.
And, even though we may now be living the American Dream, having gone to good schools and having had good jobs, we can never lose sight of the fact that what we're living is that, the American Dream, and not the Mexican.
It's possible that sometimes our identity isn't clear, but our allegiance is.
It's for the United States.
In addition, we are aware that many Mexicans belonging to the elite of the ruling class do not like us.
Feeling is mutual.
They see us as a reminder of a humiliating defeat and look over our shoulders as if we're from a lower category that isn't Mexican enough.
Our Spaniard will never be good enough and our ties to Mexico will never be strong enough.
In his view, our existence is synonymous with failure.
If our families hadn't failed in Mexico, they wouldn't have left.
And now we wouldn't be trapped behind this silk curtain, living well in the U.S. but, still, like lonely souls.
My wife, who was born in Guadalajara and legally came to the United States when she was a kid, reminds me that there is this friction between the Mexicans and the Mexican-Americans because the former know with more solidity who they are and the latter annoys this.
Although she is a U.S. citizen, she sees herself as part of the two countries.
In the meantime, many Mexican-U.S. I've met feel they're not part of either.
We love listening to the Mexican group Los Tigres del Norte, but also Bruce Springsteen.
You've got the best in every country, but you'ren't ingratinated to any of them.
In Mexico they see us as U.S.
And in the United States we are considered Mexican.
Now, to further complicate things, so I've found out during my trip, some Mexican leaders and some intellectuals want to reconnect with the diaspora.
They Want Mexican-U.S. Americans to work as impromptu "ambatresses," representing their interests in the U.S.
We'd tell our fellow Americans how fantastic it is to visit that country and we'd pressure the political leaders to strengthen their ties with Mexico.
- Yeah, of course.
That won't happen.
There's too much grudge.
And, with wage inequality, galloping corruption and drug violence, many of us aren't so sure it's a fantastic country.
I'm afraid you're gonna be left alone with this idea, folks.
That's fair.
If some Mexicans are not yet prepared to pardon the United States for the way they treated Mexico a century-and-a-a-half years ago, then they have to accept the fact that some Mexican-Americans are still resentful about how their relatives were treated much less long ago.
- Hmmm.
Maybe we'll be more "Mexicans" than I thought.
Violent Battles, New Middle East
Ceasefire between Israel-Hamas may well be an unlikely basis for peace
Will there one day be a lasting peace between Arabs and Jews in the Middle East?
