In a constantly connected world, it'sn't so bad to get bored
During this year's Thanksgiving festivities, unexpectedly I had to spend five hours at an airport because the plane we were flying on suffered a mechanical problem and we had to wait for another plane to arrive.
So I had plenty of plenty to think about boredom.
I'm not gonna lying to you.
Spending half a day in an airport waiting for a flight to arrive is quite boring, even if you have the means to be entertained, such as books, magazines or iPhones, without forgetting the duty-free stores.
But more and more children's development academics and experts praising the goodness of boring.
According to them, it's okay that both us and our children we get bored every now and then.
Bossing forces the brain to look for interesting options and may encourage creativity.
Since, at present, most of us are connected to this or that screen, we don't experience the boring's benefits.
So, should we accept boredom in good spirits?
- Yeah, yeah.
And no.
But we'll talk about it any later.
In the first place, like a lot of people, he assumed that, now that we have more time for leisure, boredom would be a relatively recent phenomenon.
But, as Peter Toohey, Professor of Greek and Roman History at the University of Calgary in Canada, and author of the book "Boredom: A Lively History," puts it.
"In fact, the history of boring is very extensive," he says.
On the walls of Pompeii there are graffiti in Latin about boredom dating back to the 1st century AD.
We must also consider how we define boredom.
"The problem is that it has already been defined and discussed in very many different ways," says John D. Eastwood, professor of psychology at the University of York in Ontario, Canada.
After examining the published research and posing the idea to a 100-strong discussion group, Professor Eastwood and his colleagues defined boredom as the experience of "wretching, but not being able, participating in an activity that fills you."
He says the difference between boredom and apathy is that the person doesn't do anything but he wants to do it.
He points out that with a apathy there is no desire to do anything.
The main experience of boredom, he says, is "the alteration of the attention process, associated with a low mood and a sense that time passes slowly."
Boredom may sound terribly like depression.
However, Professor Eastwood says that although they may be related, people who are boring tend to see the problem as an issue of the environment or the world, while people who are depressed believe the problem has them.
Sometimes we think we're boring when what's really happening to us is that we have trouble concentrating.
In his study "The Unengaged Mind: Defining Boredom in Terms of Attention," published in the September edition of the journal Perspectives on Psychological Science, Professor Eastwood and his colleagues referred to an earlier experiment in which the participants listened to a tape of a person reading the article in a journal.
Some groups listened in the adjoining room to a full-volume television show that had no relationship, others listened to the low volume, so it was barely perceptible, while the third group did not hear the audio at any time.
The group that listened to the program with the low volume showed more boring than the other two groups; they had a hard time concentrating, but weren't sure why and attributed it to boring.
"When you're trying to focus on a difficult or interesting task, impaired attention can lead to boredom," said Mark J. Fenske, professor of Neuroscience at the University of Guelph in Ontario, and one of the authors of the study.
"On the other hand, when you're doing something boring, such as detecting defective pieces on a manufacturing line, music that distracts you can help you don't get bored."
"In fact, we currently know that moving or scribbling, actions that are often interpreted as a sign of boredom, can actually help to combat it, as they cause that person to be more physically alert."
"Resembrance shows that those children who are allowed to move learn more and retain more information than those who are forced to stand," says Professor Fenske.
We all know what it's like to get boring at some point: the delay of an airplane, a monotonous speaker, a particularly boring film.
But some people have a more tendency to get bored than others.
To help measure it, some researchers in the 1980s developed an "burning-trend scale."
The ladder includes issues like "many of the things I've got to do are repetitive and monotonous" and "there's so many things I'm interested that I don't have time to do everything."
Stephen Vodanovich, professor of psychology at the University of West Florida, says that, using such scales, researchers have discovered that boys have a more tendency to get bored more often than girls, and they specifically need more external and more varied stimulation.
But, in general, teens are a kind of bunch that gets bored quite easily.
In 1991, Reed Larson, professor of human and community development at the University of Illinois, conducted an experiment in which he contacted 400 teens and their parents between seven and eight times a day through a search.
It found 32 per cent of teenagers said they got bored while they were at school or doing homework, while 23 per cent said they got bored when he wasn't at school.
On the other hand, 3 per cent of the parents said they got bored.
Professor Larson said he didn't know whether current boredom ratios, 21 years later, would be higher or lower.
But he said what he did know was that "adolescence is a period in which boredom reaches its highest levels," largely because both children and teens don't be given a lot of control over what they want to do.
So, going back to my question at the beginning: is it nice to get bored?
Sometimes it'sn't, it's because, taken to the extreme, it can cause us to expose ourselves to absurd physical risks, such as gambling or falling into the consumption of substances as a method to combat it, as the research shows.
On the other hand, many philosophers and writers talk about the connection between boredom and creativity, according to Professor Vodanovich, who has been studying the issue for more than two decades.
"Boring is the brain's way of telling you that you should be doing something else," says Gary Marcus, professor of psychology at the University of New York.
But the brain doesn't always know what's most appropriate.
If you're boring and you're using that energy to play the guitar or cook, that'll satisfy you.
"But if you look at the television, you may be satisfied in the short term, but not in the long term.
"So, if your kid is boring and you give him an iPad, you may no longer get bored, but he won't have learned how to entertain for himself or two," says Professor Fenske.
And "That ability to dosage applies to other situations," he says.
Don't you only learn to entertain on yourselves on your own, but learn to better control yourself in other areas.
- I don't think we really want to praising boredom.
But we shouldn't be too critical of him either.
Rather, our goal should be to feel comfortable apart from the constant rumbling of activities and technologies.
Professor Eastwood okay.
"By watching, we need to get bored more, but boredom is too distressing and unsettling a desire to relate it to something worthwhile," he says.
He points out that what people are really looking for is a way to disconnect and enjoy a dead time.
"In an environment where we're constantly overstretched," he says, "it's hard to find ways to focus our attention when noise stops."
