This exhibition is the culmination of a story whose sole protagonist is Garavani, but which couldn't have been written without his distinguished clients.
Valentino has always been fascinated by the weird, distant world of nobility.
In the first room of this exhibition, open until March 3, private letters and photos abound with the signature of the most pomegranate of the aristocracy, from Princess Salimah Aga Khan to Lord Snowdon, through Princess Marie-Chantal of Greece and Margarita of England.
Valentino teaches these personal memories as trophies of his social ascent: from humble dress in the city of Voghera, in northern Italy, an idol of the international "jet-set."
To be in love with royalty there's nothing wrong.
"At least they don't turn off the buttocks of their cigarettes on your magnificent rug like some beautiful pop music celebrities do," says Baroness Fiona Thyssen-Bornemisza.
In the 1960s and 1970s, we both lived in the Alps and we were good friends.
Valentino is a spectacular host and entertain with generosity and elegance.
"We all loved being invited to their chalet in Gstaad," explains ex-wife of "Heini" Thyssen, intimate with forgotten beauties such as Marella Agnelli or Eugenie Niarchos.
Valentino has always preferred elegance to notoriety.
He's still a star.
Valeria Mazza, wearing a Valentino
Argentinian model Valeria Mazza also doesn't forget the charisma of hysteria.
Many many years ago, after a parade in Piazza di Spagna in Rome, we went for dinner at his apartment.
There were twenty people, including Sharon Stone and John Kennedy Jr.
In every detail of the apartment, the decoration, the food, the music, in everything you could see and felt its spirit.
"To each of the guests he made us feel important and loved," recalls the "top-model," which began working with him during Paris Haute-Sewing Week in 1995.
"It doesn't ever happen in fashion because its designs are works of art," he finishes.
Alcurnia Parade
Garavani's life isn't a history of obsessions, it's about well reciprocated loves.
He loves people with good education and better degrees, and they love him.
One of the galleries of Somerset House has been transformed into a glamorous sixty-metre-long catwalk that proposes a change of roles: the visitor seizes the models and must parade through the catwalk to contemplate an "audience" of dream that looks like Valentino's masterpiece, such as the dress Jackie Kennedy chose for his wedding to Aristotle Onassis, the wardrobe that Monica Vitti wore in "La Notte," or a coat of wool and skin that belonged to Emperatriz Farah Diba.
Names such as Sible of Luxembourg, Gloria von Thurn und Taxis, Mette-Marit of Norway, Rosary of Bulgaria or Sofia of Habsburg shine in this audience of mannequins.
Naty Abascal and dressmaker in 2006
A lot of those clients say the first Valentino is like the first love, "impossible to forget."
I'm perfectly remembrazed.
It was a pants, a shirt, a "gillette" vest and a jacket from the autumn-winter 1971-1972 collection.
"It was a gift he made to me," says Naty Abascal, one of the designer's muses.
"I'd prefer him over others because of his femininity, because of his great love for women, because it enhances our beauty," adds the former Duchess of Fair.
I very much like the colors he wears, they have great light and are "donors" to his face.
Feasuring proportions are perfect.
Princess and fashion prescriber Patricia della Giovampaola d'Arenberga noco can forget the first time she dressed a Valentino.
When he was a teenager and lived in Italy, he dreamed of becoming old and the opportunity to be able to wear one of his costumes at night...
The time finally came in the late 1990s.
I bought my first Valentino dress to wear at a float at the castle of my cousin, Prince Edouard de Ligne.
It was a red outfit with a fly skirt skirt, "corsage" dragged and cleavage word of honor.
"It was a dream come true," Rodrigo d'Arenberg's widow says.
"Valentino is indifferent to fashion, his obsession is timeless," explains this Italian aristocrat who lives between Paris, New York and Buenos Aires.
Princess D'Arenberg keeps her glimpse grove dress with "maximum cleavage ... because a dress isn't just a dress, it's also the memoir-grabbing ensemble."
Fashion "king"
Somerset House's exhibition grand final is Marie-Chantal Miller's wedding dress at their wedding to Pablo de Grecia in 1995.
It took us four months of work and 25 "ragazze" (as the dressmaker calls its seamstresses) to make that ivory silk costume with pearl inlays, twelve different lace types and a queue of four-and-a-half meters.
According to the journalist Suzy Menkes, the highest authority of the specialized press, this dress represents a milestone of haute couture at the end of the 20th century, "the return of the clients of high society."
Obfuscated for years with the "savoir-Etre" of the elite, Valentino is now his best exemplary.
Cavaliere di Gran Croce (the highest ranking distinction in Italy), Cavaliere del Lavoro, Commandeur de L'Ordre des Arts et des Lettres, decorated with the Legion of Honour, Garavani accumulates so many honors and any of the spouses of her clients.
"I've always been struck by its refinement, its cool, its neat and perfect appearance," says D'Arenberg.
The last time I've seen him was a month ago at a gala dinner at the Orsay Museum.
He'd be at Countess Jacqueline de Ribes table, a great friend of mine.
"He was impeccable, the weather doesn't happen for him".
If a Princess says so...
The toughest office in the world: Kawah Ijen human mules
For four euros, porters of the Indonesian volcano risk their lives and health by carrying 70 kilos of sulfur through steep stone trails
There's people for whom his work is hell and another who literally works in hell.
It is the case of Anto Wijaya, one of the 400 miners who make a living by removing sulfur from the Kawah Ijen volcano, east of the Indonesian island of Java.
To do this, it has to go down every day to the bottom of its crater, where the sulfur gas emanating from the Earth's bowels solidifies by coming into contact with the air.
After tearing large sulfur rocks, which in a total weigh in about 70 kilos, he carries them in two bamboo baskets he carries on his shoulders through steep stone trails.
It is only 250 meters to the top of the volcano, which rises to 2,386 meters of altitude, but the porters take more than 40 minutes to ascend at turtle's step, keeping the balance and tempting its steps so as not to slipping and falling down the cliff.
You know that any stumbling stumbling block could cost them their lives, as happened to a French tourist who ploughed years ago through the riscos of Kawah Ijen.
Couriers of Kawah Ijen earn 5 euro cents for every pound of sulfur they extract
Once upstairs, they make their way among the tourists who photograph them like circus monkeys and, groxidizing the heavy baskets, walk three kilometers to the scale that a mining company has placed a little lower, 1,850 meters high.
It is PT Candi Ngrimbi, a company that has been exploiting the volcano since the 1960s and, never better said, its workers, who are paid 662 Indonesian rupees (5 cents euros) for every kilo of sulfur.
Then it sells it for 10,000 rupees (83 euro cents) to the petrochemical industry, as this mineral is widespread in everyday life and is used to manufacture matches, fireworks, cosmetics, dynamite and even to bleach the sugar.
"How it's normal to carry 70 kilos, we take out about 46,000 rupees (3,8 euros) on every trip," explains Anto, who usually carries out three daily carries.
It lasts for three hours in each and it ends gripped, but they allow you to collect 138,000 rupees (11.5 euros) at the end of the day.
Although it seems a misery to such an inhuman effort, it's three times what I'd win on the pitch.
"Meeters' wages are very high here, where coffee harvesting is paid at 15,000 rupees (1.2 euros) per day and the average monthly wage is two million rupees (167 euros) ," clarifies the carrier, who previously worked as a bachelor on the tourist island of Bali.
There, his salary was 75,000 rupees (6,2 euros) a day and the cut wasn't that hard, but Anto has returned with his family to Banyuwangi, a town near the volcano, for a compelling reason that, in Indonesia, it's as blunt as sulfur: "I married a young woman from Bali, where they're Hinduists, and I've brought her to Java to convert to Islam."
Anto has asthma, breathes with difficulty, coughs constantly and his eyes are irritated by toxic gases
At the age of 27, Anto wears her asleep in the Kawah Ijen, whose sulfur has already begun to pass bill despite covering his face with a mask and special glasses.
He has asthma, breathes with difficulty, coughs constantly and his eyes are irritated by the toxic fuss that the volcano fire.
It's the price you have to pay to achieve your dream.
"I will work two more years because I want to open a store or study Spanish or French," he promises in more than acceptable English.
Chased by life, this sympathetic and intelligent young man could be a tour guide, waitress or a hotel receptionist, but instead he does a mule's work.
Sharing an filthy wooden hut with other porters, it gets up every day at two o'clock in the morning because the sulfur doesn't stop running at night, when its characteristic yellow color turns blue and shines in the midst of darkness.
Defying the shades, Anto goes down to the crater clutching with a small lantern attached to his helmet, which he himself has bought with his money.
About 400 porters carry lynutic baskets on their shoulders from the crater bottom
In spite of its penguin profits, the mining company has not macanized the extraction of sulfur in order to save costs or provide any equipment to the carriers, who work on its own and both the weight.
In fact, they don't even see some of the 30,000 rupees (2.5 euros) surcharge per camera that, next to the entrance of 15,000 rupees (1.2 euros), the guards of this natural park charge tourists who come to photograph the volcano and its human mules.
"This job is for beasts and not for people," protests Madrusin, a 42-year-old porter who's been in Kawah Ijen for three decades since leaving school.
Capable of lifting up to 110 kilos, he assures that he will endure working "everything he can" because he needs the money to educate his three children, aged between 18 and 10.
I won't rejoice, I'm gonna die here 'cause the volcano's been my whole life
Although sulfur squeezes its throat and squeezes its eyes when the wind changes suddenly and traps the miners in the thick columns coming out of the volcano, they are so tough that no one complains of suffering serious diseases... beyond, of course, of their usual respiratory problems, arthrosis, pains in the knees and sores in the shoulders, which have malformed by the weight of the baskets.
Swatching the basket on her back, Unainik can only carry 50 pounds at the age of 53.
Every day, he and his comrades plump 15 tons of sulfur into the volcano, which three lorries transfer to the Tamansari warehouse, 18 kilometers away by a path of goats between the weed.
"I won't retire, I will die here because the volcano has been my entire life," proclaims Unainik opening his mouth well, where he lacks several teeth.
Of his five children, the eldest, aged in his 30s, he also works carrying sulfur.
Time passes, but misery perpetrates one of the toughest trades in the world from generation to generation: the human mules of Kawah Ijen.
Singapore Searches Babies to Save the Economy
Singaporeans are blasting the career, stress and the cost of properties and education for not having children.
"The people of Singapore need to grow".
I'm a patrician husband, you're my patriotic brilliant wife, we fulfill our civic duty and create life.
It may seem implausible that these verses will be part of a mint pill advertisement, but despite that - or perhaps because of that - the video went viral on YouTube in Singapore earlier this year.
Phrases are part of a rap that uses local references such as "Let's put a bao (bowl) in the oven" to laugh about Singapore's birth rate.
The advertising company that made the video, BBH, has the hope that with the announcement they will be able to draw attention to the problem in an amusing way.
Its creative director, Douglas Hamilton, says he wanted to use the power of music for people to fulfill "your national duty."
It's a purely internet thing, so we had to make it fun and fun.
It's the biggest problem in this country.
We're the worst in the world to replicate ourselves, so we felt that this was an issue we had to address.
We knew that the government had tried so many things, such as throwing perfumes with pheromones or organizing quick dating evenings (speed-dating).
A lot of those ideas may have been creative, but they didn't necessarily work.
"So we think, why don't we do as creative as we can to solve this problem, which is to compose a rap?
1.2 children
The one who doesn't take it so lightly is the government of Singapore.
He spends $1,300-a-a-great-a-year policies to encourage citizens to have more children.
A government package for marriages and parents grants up to $15,000 per child, extends maternity leave and distributes tax benefits.
But it's all had little effect.
Singapore is a wealthy, high-tech city in south-eastern Asia, also known for the conservatism of its leaders and its strict social controls.
Singapore &apos; s birth rate, according to its national population division, currently stands at 1.2 children per woman.
The last time he was above 2 - known as a replacement rate - was in 1976.
So why don't the Singaporeans have any more children?
Tan Wei Ming, Director of Marriage and Family Policy at the National Population Division, says it's because of "greater education" and "a wider range of racing opportunities."
"That has allowed people to have a wider range of choices in terms of life goals and priorities, beyond getting married and forming a family," he explains.
These changes in social norms have contributed to an increase in bachelorettes, and delaying marriage and births, resulting in a decrease in the birth rate in Singapore.
In the meantime, an immigration policy aimed at drastically boosting immigration to cope with the population decline has created resentment among the local population.
In Singapore there are websites where xenophobia barely disguised against many new immigrants, in particular the Chinese, who are criticised for maintaining low wages and not integrating.
The increase in immigration is also seen as one of the Reasons why Singapore's governing party last year experienced its worst election result since independence.
Since the election there has been an attempt to correct the problem, with the highest quotas and levies for foreign workers.
Unexpected consequences
While the fall in the birth rate has a known effect on a nation's economic growth, tax revenues, health costs and immigration policies, Singapore's example is also having some unfinished consequences.
The government is trying not to build so many small houses.
For example, it has begun to affect the real estate sector.
The urban development authority has gone on to control the number of small apartments, known as "shoe collection," that can be built in certain areas of the city.
These apartments have 46-square-foot surfaces and have had a lot of sales success.
However, there's a concern that they can promote a bachelor's lifestyle and discourage developers who want to build big, family houses.
But Lim Yew Soon, general manager of real estate company EL Developers, says its "shoe boxes" are sold much faster than the larger units.
They are more popular in the sense that the units run out days, even weeks, faster than the larger units.
This makes it so much better for our cash flow.
However, he admits that the new rules provide clearer guidance for developers, who were previously hindered if they wanted to offer too many small units on a project.
It's too stressed.
Singapore's a state city.
While these new rules may be a step toward raising the national birth rate, talking to Singaporeans working in the downtown financial district doesn't look like they're going to have much effect.
"People are very stressed, the houses are expensive and so is education, so many people are postponing it," says a young executive.
Other people may have children.
"But it is important for me to have my own money and time," says another 20-year-old.
Men and women mention their careers, stress and the cost of property and education as the motives that prevent them from having children.
So as much as the government tries to push its citizens to have children, when it's about making babies, Singaporeans have the last word.
Deprived offline is private on-line
Privacy.
According to the Dictionary of the Royal Spanish Academy it is the quality of private or "the sphere of private life that you have the right to protect from any intrusion."
What's private for a child under the age of 16?
How do you apply this definition in your daily life and social networks?
Do you understand the dangers you're exposed to by ventilating on the Internet situations you probably wouldn't share offline?
the newspaper interviewed five boys between the ages of ten and 15, frequent users of the network.
In four of the cases, they linked the term to "something very mine," on the personal level, and "in the key and user name," when they applied it to social networks.
"My deepest secrets wouldn't bring them up to a post," says Jorge, ten, as he tries to explain the meaning of private on sites such as Facebook, Twitter, Hotmail and Windows Live Messenger, where he has had accounts for two years.
"It's very secret secrets, my mom can get to know them, but not everyone," she says.
In FB I raise nice pictures or games of games.
I'm also entertained with my acquaintances.
"I wouldn't share a photo that's not mine, or of someone who's ridiculous," she says.
The toddler admits it is wrong to post obscene photographs of naked people, of crimes or writing humiliating or violent comments.
Jorge assures to know the 35 friends he has in FB and his nine followers on Twitter.
Most of them are relatives.
The mom's included, she's got the key to one of the accounts.
I snapped Twitter to express myself and write interesting tweets.
"I don't know if they're answering me, I'll just upload them," he adds.
"Social networks are entertaining, I can talk to distant relatives or my friends quickly," he says.
There's no hesitation in answering that I'd never accept a request from an unknown person.
I couldn't hesitate someone to recommend a stranger.
The 14-year-and-a-year-old Joseph's case is different.
The teenager owns open accounts in Hotmail, Facebook, My Space and Ask, the latter admits not to know 20 of the people added to his list of friends.
"It doesn't scare me, 'because we've got something in common, music for example," he says.
According to the boy, none of them have hinted at or asked for the address of his house or his phone number.
"Whether they pressured me or demanded I just take them out of my account," he admits.
Joseph became a follower of Ask, after reading a recommendation on Twitter.
Experiences of what is known as cyber bullying are no strangers to the kid.
An acquaintance from a friend was extorted on a social network.
They threatened him and demanded money from him.
"I never knew who he was," he says.
The victim, according to Joseph, didn't close the bill.
"Just gave him privacy".
It then explains a series of steps to safely configuring the account.
Unlike Jorge, this kid would take pictures of acquaintances in uncomfortable or embarrassing situations.
Yeah, if I'd like it or I feel like it.
"Although I don't know that is cyberbullying," he says.
Key Questions
Marielos Porras, an English teacher and graduate in Education and Learn, believes that in order to guide children and teens it should be understood that the purpose of social networks is to inform.
"Internet emerged as a means to search for information, but with the appearance of these sites they changed the rules of the game," he says.
Porras points out that the academic Marc Prensky, a Master's Degree in Pedagogy from the University of Yale and the author of the work Nativos y Immigrantes Digital, coined these terms to explain the phenomenon.
The digital natives are the children and young people who were born with the tech.
"It's the digital immigrants that we have to teach them when we're still learning," he says.
He explains that the issue is complex, "because we're asking them to have a definite criterion about what's whether it's appropriate to disseminate, publish or say at an age that maturity doesn't give for that."
"They also be selective when what they care the most about is being popular, to have thousands of friends without thinking about the consequences," he adds.
According to the specialist, the most effective way to teach privacy to children and teens is through questions that make them reflect.
"Telling them don't do it doesn't work," he adds.
Porras then lists a few options: There are things you wouldn't tell a stranger, why are you doing it on the Internet?
Or, you'd like them to post a picture of you like the one you posted of a friend?
Do you know what the others are posting about you?
When you've tattooed party photos, did you ask other people's permission to tag them?
And one more, do everyone need to know what you're doing every moment?
It's another point to make them see that they have to act on the line like they do offline.
It's the same rules.
"One outside the Internet acts with respect, morality and other principles, it must still be on social networks," he says.
Monitoring
Estuardo Guardia, a university professor, primary school teacher and educational consultant, says it is essential for parents to consciously read the policies of social networks.
Upon comprehending each of the subparagraphs they have solid foundations to talk with their children about the implications of opening an account on the Internet.
"For example, the age and what is allowed to share or publish," he says.
According to the Guard, it's key to remind the children of "don't talk with strangers."
Summit of Unasur ends without publicizing the Lima Declaration
The 6th Presidential Summit of the South American Union of Nations (Unasur) concluded today in Peru without the publication of the Lima Declaration, announced previously and theoretically signed by the seven attending leaders.
Efe repeatedly tried to access the document signed at the 6th Ordinary Meeting of Heads of State and Government of Unasur, but sources of Foreign Affairs and the Presidency initially pointed out that they were going to deliver it after the closure, but then stated that it will be published at some point on the Peruvian government's website.
When they were consulted by the text, they pointed out that the content had been presented by the President of Peru, Ollanta Humala, during brief statements to the press.
Journalists &apos; access to information from the Summit was restricted at all times.
During the day it was only transmitted, in the press room, the soundless video of the presidential assembly with the message "deprived session, restricted audio."
The little information circulating between the reporters was delivered by the press spokespersons of some of the Unassur governments attending the meeting, although not the Peruvian.
The only document published during the day was the list of attending presidents, which caused the discomfort of hundreds of journalists from various national and international media who asked for more data.
Later the Presidency of Peru sent an e-mail to the media with the "complete declaration" of the summit, but it is the pronouncement of Humala, and not the official document that closed the summit.
Last October, Peru hosted the Third Summit of the South-Arab America (ASPA), and on that occasion, although strongly requested by the press, the so-called Lima Declaration that they had previously announced was also not disseminated.
It can be corroborated on ASPA's official website that the document was published last Tuesday.
In both international citations, the Peruvian authorities made an effort to ensure free transmission systems for all reporters, but limited the collection of information to the maximum.
The summit also culminated with the joint commitment of Chile and Peru to accept a judgment of the Court of The Hague governing a border dispute between the two countries.
The Presidents of Peru, Ollanta Humala, and of Chile, Sebastián Piñera, met during the regional meeting and confirmed that they will respect the (ICJ) decision, which begins on Monday in The Hague to hear the allegations of both parties, in the process Lima presented against Santiago.
"We will comply and execute the sentence that defines the differences we are bringing to this international court today," said Humala, along with his Chilean pair.
"Chile has been, is and will continue to be a country respectful of international law, the peaceful resolution of disputes, treaties and international tribunals," Piñera added, and greeted Humala with a handshake, next to the flags of the two countries.
The confirmation of both presidents that they will be submitted to the ICJ occurred after Colombia denounced this week the Pact of Bogota by which it had agreed to submit to the judgments of that international court, following a decision on maritime limits with Nicaragua that it considered seriously wrong.
The summit was carried out with the non-at-at-at-at-at-at-attendants of the Presidents of Brazil, Dilma Rouseff; Venezuela, Hugo Chavez; Bolivia; Evo Morales; and Argentina, Cristina Kirchner.
Paraguay, suspended from Unasur since 2011 following the impeachment of its former president Fernando Lugo, did not participate in the meeting.
The host president, Ollanta Humala, was in charge of opening the deliberations in the morning and closing the summit, shortly after no-day Lima.
The president read the final document in which he reported that 16 agreements were adopted and the lines of action were set for 31 projects between the South American countries for a total of $17 billion in investments.
Among the agreements adopted he mentioned that the countries of UNASUR will take "important steps towards the goal of a South American citizenship for which we are expanding the residency agreements."
He reported that actions are being promoted to improve "cooperation in the fight against citizen insecurity and transnational organized crime, actions to make medicines more accessible, low-priced Internet access in all corners of South America, and respond jointly and efficiently the risks of natural disasters."
With a Europe in crisis, "the economic consolidation (of Latin America) must not have a triumphalist matrix but must serve to expand our productive matrix and glimpse a better future for our peoples," Humala added.
"We have decided to privilege a group of 31 iconic projects that will improve the connection of the spaces of South America, especially in rural and border areas ... linking our countries and generating new economic circuits," the Peruvian president said in a message read.
Among these projects, he mentioned that five correspond to Peru, and they are located in the transversal axes of its territory, from the coast to Brazil, and two point to a greater connection with Ecuador, although it did not provide further details.
The final document also mentioned the political situation in Paraguay.
"We hope that the electoral process in that country will serve to rejoin the Union of South American Nations," of which it is currently excluded.
Latin America &apos; s need to maintain itself as a region of prosperity and peace, integrated and of good relations between neighbors was another of the issues highlighted by the summit.
In this sense, the president of Colombia, Juan Manuel Santos, said before starting his participation in the regional meeting that he hoped to meet with his Nicaraguan partner, Daniel Ortega, on Saturday in Mexico to deal in a respectful way the maritime dispute following a failure of the ICJ, questioned by Bogotá.
"Tomorning (Saturday) you may have a meeting with President Daniel Ortega," Santos said.
"We are going to review all of these paths, they are not exclusive, even the treaty with Nicaragua is going to require a conversation with Nicaragua," he stressed.
"With President Ortega I hope I will be able to tell him to handle this in the most civilized and respectful way possible," Santos added.
Santos and Ortega would meet on Saturday in Mexico where they are scheduled to attend the inauguration of the new president of that country, Enrique Peña Nieto.
Also, as part of the summit, the defense ministers of the bloc met in advance to approve the 2013 Plan of Action, which seeks to strengthen dialogue and consensus on defense in the region.
Argentina, Bolivia, Colombia, Ecuador, Peru, Brazil, Uruguay, Venezuela, Chile, Guyana, Suriname and Paraguay join UNASUR, although the last country is suspended.
Peru has the pro temporan presidency of the regional bloc.
"South-America Must Learn from Europe to Integrate a Citizenship": Rafael Correa
The president of Ecuador, Rafael Correa, said today that the creation of a common citizenry is a goal in which "South-America, in this case, must learn from Europe."
Correa, who participated in the 6th Presidential Summit of the Union of South American Nations (Unasur), held in Lima, told the state TV Peru that Europeans were "shocked in the Second World War" and in other conflicts, "But now they are practically a homeland."
In this regard, he defended the project to establish the South American citizenry encouraged by Unasur member countries.
"It is necessary to achieve the free mobility of citizens and workers by any South American country, as is already the case with the members of the Andean Community, but there are still reactionary sectors that want to return us to the past," he warned.
The Ecuadorian president was also in favor of the recomposition of the Organization of American States (OAS) on the premise of reducing the influence of the Anglo-Saxon states and taking into account those who have signed San José's pact on human rights.
Those who give professors never commit themselves to anything and, on the other hand, we South Americans sign it all.
"It is incomprehensible that the Inter-American Commission on Human Rights is in Washington under U.S. funding," he said in an allude to political asylum given by Ecuador to WikiLeaks founder Julian Assange.
Correa said he is not remorseful of this decision because with it he has not betrayed his principles, but has respected his "deep democratic and human rights values."
He added that, at the time, "there were well-founded suspicions that Assange was extradited to a third country and that his process would not be respected."
He also criticised the Swedish justice system for requiring him to be subjected in his territory to the interrogation for an alleged sex offence when "Swedish legislation itself makes it possible to be carried out by videoconference, which could be done from Ecuador's embassy in London."
Correa said there's a risk that Assange's physical and mental health will deteriorate.
"I haven't talked to him since he's in our embassy, but the ambassador informed me that I had a small lung problem, nothing serious," the Ecuadorian president said.
What there is is is the danger that his physical and mental health will deteriorate by being locked in a small space without being able to do outdoor exercises.
"That complicates anyone's health," he added.
Correa pointed out that the solution to the asylum granted to Assange since June at the Ecuadorian embassy in London, through the granting of a savior that allows him to travel to Ecuador, is in the hands of Britain, Sweden and the European legal authorities and stressed that there have been talks with London in search of a solution to the lockdown of the founder of WikiLeaks.
We don't negotiate with human rights, we don't use that word in this case, but there's been ongoing talks.
"The resolution of this problem is in the hands of Great Britain, Sweden and the European legal authorities because Assange &apos; s lawyer Baltazar Garzón is doing a series of procedures at different European levels," he said.
And he said that "whether Brisbane gives the safe passage tomorrow this ends."
And if Sweden, as its legislation perfectly permits and as it has done in other cases, interrogates Mr Assange at Ecuador's embassy in London or interrogates him via Skype tomorrow this problem ends.
Correa took the opportunity to reassert himself as a defender of press freedom and nuanced that what he does not tolerate is " mediocrity, bad faith and lying, which distort freedom of expression."
"The eldest enemies of press freedom aren't wicked and wicked politicians but bad journalists based on profit, blackmail and extortion," he said.
In this regard he was pleased that it is no longer these journalists "neither the bankers nor the hegemonic and bourgeois countries that dominate Ecuador," and he said that from being re-elected it "would deepen the revolution in order to continue the path and the right direction."
Correa also supported the decision to maintain the veto on Paraguay in Unasur at least until its next elections, arguing that the organization "should be firm and do not tolerate the opportunism and the coup coated in legality" because in reality it "shattered the legitimacy of Paraguayan democracy."
The Ecuadorian president also considered "perfectly relevant" the desire of his Colombian counterpart, Juan Manuel Santos, to now negotiate with Nicaragua the maritime limits between the two countries following the ruling of the International Court of Justice in The Hague, which benefited Nicaragua's maritime sovereignty.
At the moment, that ruling isn't in place.
It is a problem between a South American and a Central American country.
Controversies are inevitable, but they must be overcome by the will to walk together.
They must be processed in a wide way in order to overcome them and move on.
He also trusted in a good conclusion of the maritime boundary litigation they maintain in the same court Peru and Chile and said that "it is correct for Latin America to seek international instances if the two countries undertake to accept judgment however hard it may be."
Referring to the eventuality that he presented himself as a candidate in the upcoming presidential elections in Ecuador to seek a third consecutive mandate, he said that he sees this possibility "with a lot of optimism and joy, although sometimes it is quite hard."
