Silk-syst deaths today are cause for late detection
Fabrizio was a 21-year-old when he was confirmed the outcome of the ordeal: AIDS positive.
"It was as if a bomb dropped on me," he recesses in remembramorating the timing of the announcement that the doctor was trying to do "softer," without obvious success.
The kid hid it from his family.
He decided only to take care of his illness and he began to learn about it; with such an endeavor that he already celebrated his 43rd birthday.
Undoubtedly he is one of the oldest patients in the HIV Unit of the Guadalajara Civil Hospital (HCG), whom he arrived in 1994 after several battles.
Fabrizio has lived with the human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) for 22 years, somewhat difficult to imagine in the early 1990s, when there were many doubts, few treatment options and more stigma.
Then, even the director of an IMSS clinic he avoided saying goodbye "because he had a chops."
Then having AIDS was synonymous with death.
Now it is possible to survive this syndrome and do so with quality of life.
However, unaware of his illness, many people still arrive when the virus has already wreaked ravages, "exhausted" their immune system and are victims of opportunistic infections.
Thirty-six years after the appearance of AIDS in the world, at least of the first reported cases, "the great achievement means at this moment that the survival of a patient who initiates treatment in a timely manner and the survival of the general population is exactly the same," said the head of the HCG HIV Unit, Jaime Andrade Villanueva, after referring that this information was endorsed in April of this year in a prestigious scientific publication.
Infectologist and expert in HIV/AIDS, Andrade Villanueva commented that since 2008, scientists had concluded that AIDS was not a fatal sentence, but the years of survival and quality of life depend on the degree of affectation of the immune system presented by patients at the beginning of the diagnosis, with a better expectation for those who do not be drug users: up to 30 years with a count of 200 CD4 and of 50 years, when they report 500 CD4.
Simply speaking, this means that those who are diagnosed with HIV-positive at the age of 25, under these terms "as long as they are kept in control they will be able to live without problems until the age of 75," the interviewee explained.
It should be remembered that the life expectancy of Mexicans today is 76 years on average.
Although mortality has dropped significantly in recent years, in the case of Mexico, the number of people dying of AIDS fell from 6,678 cases in 2007 to 4,862 in 2011 (UNAIDS annual report), it is also true that 60 per cent of patients in the national database have died.
In Jalisco alone, 255 people were killed in 2011, and there are 187 deaths until May of this year; however it is assured that there has been universal access to antiretroviral drugs since 2005.
-Why's there still fatalities?
I don't think the problem's about access to treatment.
That's how I visualize it, that's how it's happened to us in our hospital.
At least in the last 12 years we haven't had a drug out of the problem is that patients arrive very advanced because they don't know their state of infection, that is, in late stages of the disease.
He gave a blunt fact: "Nine out of ten patients arrive when they already have an opportunistic infection what has to be done to have a greater impact on global mortality, is to make earlier diagnoses and, therefore, mass detection tests have to be offered to all people who require it."
Its proposal coincides specialists and officials of the State Council for the Prevention of AIDS in Jalisco (Coesida), also the patients themselves, such as Fabrizio, who came to take the test in a particular laboratory, only motivated because a friend had done so and, despite his young age, he was already in the stage of AIDS and he even suffered sarcoma from Kaposi, a cancerous tumor that is one of the common complications.
Everything changes when you know you have AIDS.
Some people think they're gonna die and don't wanna know anything.
"If I'm gonna die anymore, I'd better burst three times a week," they say don't in my case.
The change was to get better, I eat well, I exercise, I take the medications.
To date, her parents only know that she suffered from cancer.
I live a normal life, just like anybody else.
"I work, I have a lot of activities, I travel, I have an active sex life, but co-responsible, I take care of myself and the other person," said Fabrizio, who agreed to share his intimacy with MILENIO JALISCO to motivate with his testimony those who today, within the framework of World AIDS Day, are scared.
That they are tested, if they were at risk, that the sooner they know if they are HIV-positive, it is the better and if they already have the diagnosis, to learn that they can live like any person, being responsible.
That, your message, sums up the quest motto for AIDS this 2012.
Condoms behind the counter
Great are the holes that exist between health programs and the ordinary citizen, said Ricardo Salazar, a tapatío journalist who has assumed the cause about HIV.
And the biggest one is in prevention.
In the places dedicated to this task "the distribution of condoms has actually been increased, before they gave us one or two, now they give us packages of a hundred, and that is very okay but it turns out that there are those today, still, they do not have access to a condom," he said.
Teenagers are among those most vulnerable to new infections.
"Why do you want them?" is a common question, with sorrow and value judgments of social workers, school counsellors, pharmacy employees and health personnel, to which the adolescents do not want to expose themselves, the communicator said.
He proposed reorienting this ineffable distribution; that the condoms are not only behind the counters and those hundred are in public bathroom dispatchers from the sites frequented by young people.
It's not motivating promiscuity.
Neither is it a question of paying them the chelas and the motel, as Governor Emilio Gonzalez replied, when asked if there would be the distribution of condoms in his administration.
"That's not how it's sexuality, it's best to bring condoms closer to those already practicing sexual activity," he said.
Keys In Jalisco
There are 13,435 accumulated cases (12,158 of AIDS and 1,317 HIV).
The state is a 4th national place in new and accumulated cases of AIDS and 13 euros of HIV.
Ninety-nine per cent of infections correspond to the sexual route, 6 per cent to the blood and 2 per cent to the perinatal route.
It's estimated that 50,000 people may be living with HIV because in a registered case there are 4 to 5 people who don't.
Ratified by a U.S. appeals court a judgment that ignores the restructure of the Vitro Group's debt achieved through a commercial competition in Mexico, the scenario places a nefarious precedent for any national company with ramifications in the neighboring country that has solvency problems.
It would therefore be pointed out that the procedures in terms of the survival of the signatures that allow Mexican laws are not valid in the country of bars and stars, contrary to international conventions.
On the practical ground, the backlash to the sentence handed down last June 15 by Judge Harlin Hale of the Bancarrotas court for the Northern District of Texas, leaves Mexican firms defenseless in the face of the possibility of seizure of their properties beyond the Bravo.
However, the resolution opens the door to Mexico's main glass maker to the U.S. Supreme Court, citing three inconsistencies.
At the outset, while the judge of the case states that creditors must be governed by the U.S. Bankruptcy Act, the U.S. Court of Appeals for New Orleans-based Fifth U.S. Court says the main proceeding is the commercial competition processed in Mexico.
The first point would involve ignoring international procedural cooperation in cases of insolvency of companies with a transnational profile.
In fact, the United Nations Model Law on the Uniformity of International Trade Law was created towards that end, placing the American Law Institute as an arbiter.
Secondly, the judgment establishes that without the intercompany vote, recognized in the critical mass of the commercial competition the debts owed to its parent company the subsidiaries of Vitro, the majority required to approve the restructure would not have been achieved.
However, the possibility is recognized by Mexican laws.
In fact, Vitro hasn't been the first case in which the scheme is accepted.
There are half a dozen examples, among them Agremex and Comercial Mexicana, whose intercompany debts were endorsed by the Federal Institute of Commercial Contests.
The truth, moreover, is that by subtracting the votes of the subsidiaries, anyway Vitro &apos; s creditors who battled him in the U.S. Courts, i.e. "vulture" funds such as Aurelios Capital, Aurelios Convergence, Elliot International and Liverpool Limited, will not have achieved a majority.
Polls would have been 45 percent to 37.
The data is omitted from the Court of Appeal.
From another angle, he blames Vitro for the difficult situation he faced starting in 2008 from the serious economic crisis faced by the U.S., slashing the country.
For the time being, the signing of the Gonzalez Sada family will file an appeal for reconsideration with the appeals court itself so that the vote will reach the plenary of the appeals court, i.e. the five judges, since only three voted.
Failure to thrive will claim Cerciorare &apos; s appeal, which requires a review of the trial by a higher instance, in this case the Supreme Court of Justice of the United States.
The serious thing in the case is that the court ignored a document sent by the Government of Mexico under the figure of amicus curiae ("friend of the Court"), detailing the procedure followed by Vitro within the framework of the Commercial Competitions Act, pointing out that it defused in accordance with the agreements signed by the two countries in order to tie it with chapter 15 of the U.S. Bancarrots Act.
Moreover, it is noted that the country folded to the principles of the United Nations Commission for International Trade, i.e. the rules established for cross-border insolvency cases, which guarantee equity for debtors and creditors.
Carambola of two bands: they beat Vitro and beat the country.
