Héctor "Hetin" Reyes: "Basketball has been my life"
Basketball players Héctor "Hetin" Reyes was for more than 60 years linked to the sport of the tabloid and, thanks to him, traveled the entire world.
Few people in Puerto Rico have a mental collection of the history of local basketball as wide as Héctor "Hetin" Reyes has.
And it is that before a stroke confined him to a wheelchair in 2008, Reyes was immersed in the sport for more than 60 years, he was a player of minor categories, National Superior Basketball player, owner and leader in the BSN with the Bayamon Cowboys or president of the Basketball Federation.
"I wore a lot of hats in basketball throughout my life, including several at the same time, such as when he was president of the BSN, general manager of the National Team and federal president during the 1990s," Reyes recalled during a First Hour visit to his home in Bayamon, where he resides with Isabel, his faithful wife for more than 50 years.
"Basketball has been my life".
Reyes doesn't exaggerate in making that assertion.
The walls of his house, almost all decorated with photos and memorabilias that denote his wide trajectory, attest.
Bayamon in the Helm
Of all, those he treasure with the greatest excitement are remembracing his passage through the Cowboys from the mid-50s as a player until 1982, when he turned 15, serving as a co-captain or owner of the franchise.
"That was my best years, the ones I enjoyed the most because I had the opportunity to be part of eight Cowboys championships since 1967, whether it's proxy, co-star or leader.
There were many years of satisfactions, including the five championships run from 1971 to 1975.
And then I said goodbye with one in 1981, Jerome Mincy' debut year at the BSN.
From now on "Cuco" Ortiz took over, who was a great administrator," Reyes said.
"I remember Gene Bartow, who'd led here and was at the University of Alabama, (Birmingham), told me: "I've got you a very strong player of 6"7 tall.
Do you want it?"
And that was the beginning of Mincy, one of the best players Puerto Rico has ever had.
Bartow then recommended sniper Raymond Gausse, who was nativized and was one of our best shooters.
"I remember he said that if Mincy had given Bayamon a championship, Gausse would help bring in another.
The Cowboys championship with Gausse rejoiced it, but already in the distance, because by 1988 it was already of federative mandamas.
At that time he preferred to enjoy his performances and Mincy's in the National Team.
Remembrance when we first be beat the U.S. during the Pre-Olympic in Mexico in 1989.
"Then came the 1990 World Cup, where we came fourth and it must have been bronze if it wasn't for that Canadian referee who made us repeat the final game for the second time," Reyes said.
That 1990 World Cup group is the best national team you've ever seen?
He is among the best, just like the one who beat the Dream Team at the 2004 Olympics.
But my favorite was that of the Pan-Americans in Cuba in 1991, when we won gold and gave the U.S. team a peel, which was quite similar to the one who beat the bronze in the World Cup.
That team not only repeated Mincy, Gausse, Ramon Rivas, Fico Lopez and "Piculín" (Ortiz), but it also had (Javier) "Toñito" Columbus and James Carter juniors, the Lion (Francis and Edgar brothers) and Mario "Quijote" Morales, who had not been able to be in the 1990s due to a knee injury.
A team that maybe wasn't the best in terms of personnel, but it gave us a gold medal and a lot of joy was that of the 1995 Pre-Olympic in Neuquén, Argentina.
With role-to-teachers such as "Canito" Nieves, Pablo Alicea and a young Rolando Hourruitiner to replace the players suspended by Mar del Plata's Pan-American mess, we won gold against all odds.
What's been Puerto Rico's Best Player?
Undoubtedly, Piculín Ortiz.
Their numbers at international tournament level are impressive.
No one in Puerto Rico has dominated at that level like Piculín did.
And that doesn't count their trajectory in the different leagues he played.
Who's been the finest Puerto Rican leader?
That's very difficult.
We've had a very nice litter that includes Julio Toro, Flor Meléndez, Carlos Morales, Raymond Dalmau, Armandito Torres.
Of the young people, I really like Leo Arill's work.
What do you think of your greatest achievement in the federation?
Having been part of the National Team's most glorious era between 1988 and 1995 that in the early 1990s the BSN had up to 17 teams in a stint.
What's left for you to do?
There were things he would have wanted to implement, such as the regionalization of the smaller categories.
For example, Ponce's boys will only play in his area and only face teams from other corners of the island in the national play-offs.
Right now the children travel and play too unnecessarily.
At the very least I see the fruit of the compulsory certifications and courses for leaders, table officers and referees.
That pleases me.
What are you doing now?
The most thing I do is listening to music, to watch music videos of my time on YouTube, enjoy my grandchildren and from time to time go to basketball games.
And of course, to enjoy the company of my wife, Isabel, who has always been with me.
Actor Larry Hagman Dies
Larry Hagman, born September 21, 1931, in Forth Worth (Texas), became world renowned for his central role as John Ross Ewing, better known as "J.R.," in the television series "Dallas," in which he incarnated an unscrupulous, malicious and manipulative businessman.
Larry Hagman, whose role as predatory oil tyco player J.R. Ewing in the television series "Dallas" became a symbol of greed in the 1980s.
I'd be 81 years old.
Hagman, who returned this year as a J.R. in a new "Dallas" season, died Friday afternoon due to complications of cancer, according to a family statement provided to The Associated Press by the Warner Bros., the "Dallas" production house.
"Larry was back in his beloved hometown of Dallas, again representing the iconic role he liked most," the family said.
Larry's family and his closest friends were with him in Dallas for the Thanksgiving holiday.
Linda Gray, who played the role of her wife in the original series and in the sequel, was with Hagman when she passed away at a Dallas hospital, her publicist Jeffrey Lane said.
It brought joy to everyone I knew.
He was creative, generous, funny, affectionate, loving and talented, and I'm gonna miss him a lot.
"She was an original person and lived life to the fullest," Gray said in a statement.
Hagman was diagnosed with liver cirrhosis in 1992 and admitted he had drunk heavily for years.
In 1995 he was found with a malignant tumor in the liver and underwent a transplant.
Years Before "Dallas," Hagman became famous on television as a decent guy in the light comedy "I Dream of Jeannie," broadcast on NBC from 1965 to 1970.
In it he portrayed Captain Tony Nelson, an astronaut whose life is disturbed when he finds an attractive genius, played by Barbara Eden, and takes her to live with him at home.
He also starred in two comedies that were short on the air, "The Good Life" (NBC, 1971-72) and "Here We Go Again" (ABC (ABC, 1973).
His work in the film included well-received roles by critics in "The Group," "Harry and Tonto" and "Primary Colors."
But it was his masterful renouweath of the charming J.R. that drove Hagman to his ultimate stardom.
CBS' drama series about the Ewing clan and other characters in its orbit was on the air from April 1978 to May 1991.
The promotional phrase "Who Shot J.R.?," designed to generate expectation around an episode of ethic emotions in which Hagman's character is almost assassinated, spawned international speddles and millions of risky dollars in gambling houses.
He also helped give the series an audience record at the time.
When it was unveiled in a November 1980 chapter, an average of 41 million viewers tune it up and turned "Dallas" into the second most-watched entertainment show in history, behind the end of "MASH" in 1983, which had 50 million spectators.
It was J.R.'s sister-in-law, Kristin (pictured by Mary Crosby) who shooed him.
J.R. embarrassed her and then threatened to label her a prostitute unless she left town, but others also had reason to attack him.
Hagman portrayed Ewing as an insatiable smile: a cheating businesswoman and an unfaithful husband who tried to have his alcoholic wife, Sue Ellen (Linda Gray), interned.
"I know what I'd like on J.R."s tomb," Hagman said in 1988.
I should say: "Here lies the honest citizen J.R. Ewing."
That's the only businessman he lost.
Victoria Principal, co-star of the original series, recalled Hagman on Friday as "enormous, on and off-the-screen."
It is unforgettable and irremovable, for millions of admirers in the world, and in the hearts of each of us, who were lucky enough to know him and love him.
Fourteen episodes of the new edition of "Dallas" were broadcast a few months ago with great success for the TNT channel.
He had already finished the recording of five of the chapters for the second season and a sixth was in the process, the network reported.
Immediately there was no pronouncement from Warner or TNT about how Hagman's loss will handle the series.
Originally from Fort Worth, Texas, he was the son of actress-and-songwriter Mary Martin, who stealred classic works such as "South Pacific" and "Peter Pan."
Martin was still a teen when he had it in 1931 during his marriage to barrister Ben Hagman.
He tasted luck in the theatrical settings of New York in the early 1950s, and then served in the Air Force from 1952 to 1956 in England.
While there she met young Swedish designer Maj Axelsson and got married.
The couple had two children, Preston and Heidi, and lived for a long time in the Californian city of Malibu, where many celebrities reside.
In 2001 he titled his memoir "Hello Darlin': Tall (and Absolutely True) Tales About My Life."
"I didn't put anything in there that I thought I was going to hurt someone or affect them in some way," he told Associated Press at the time.
After the liver transplant he became a promoter of organ donation and worked as a volunteer in a hospital to assist fearful patients.
"I advise them, I give them courage, I meet with them when they come for their surgeries, and later," he said in 1996.
I'm trying to offer some consolation, such as "don't fear, it'll be a little uncomfortable for a short time, but they're going to be okay."
He was also an anti-smoking activist and participated in several campaigns.
Initiate course that explores "Fine of the World"
Every week, students scout apocalyptic topics such as nuclear warfare, zombies, viruses and germs, and global warming.
This semester, when professor of religion Stuart Charmé decided to take a course on the end of the world, he knew he had an irresistible hook: The End of the "Large Regressive" Calendar of the Mayan Calendar on December 21, to which many people have clinched as proof that the end of the world comes.
But Charmé had no idea what awaited him in the next couple of months: The cataclysmic Hurricane Sandy, a fiscal precipice that some have called "debt debt" and a growing conflict that involves Israel, the place where Christian theorists at the end of the times believe the Apocalypse will begin.
"I didn't realize that this was going to be the most apocalyptic semester there's ever been," Charmé told students at Rutgers-Camden-Camden University in New Jersey this week.
If you look at what's been going on in the world nowadays like we're 30 days away and counting, this has been a really good period.
Remember how bad it's good for apocalyptic-minded people.
And he isn't the only faculty who offers "end-of of the world" courses this semester, in theory the latest in history.
In Temple, Associate Professor Barry Vacker is teaching the "Medice, Culture and the End of the World" course.
Every week, students scout apocalyptic topics such as nuclear warfare, zombies, viruses and germs, and global warming.
"We analyze why these ideas proliferate over time," he said, and the way they offer hypothetical scenarios that guide some human behavior.
If the nuclear material falls into the hands of terrorists, for example, a war could erupt.
Earlier this month the students analyzed apocalyptic-themed films and explored how they compare to real-life examples.
"I've been trying to inform the students about what's possible, likely, credified and impossible," Vacker said.
On the main campus of Pennsylvania State University, Latin American history professor Matthew Restall and his colleague Amara Solari, an associate professor of Art History and Anthropology, have teamed up to teach a course, titled only "The End of the World."
"We don't add '2012' to always have the option of teaching the course again, in case the world doesn't end," Restall said.
Despite "imminent wreck," students must study, generate projects and take final exams.
At Penn State, the final exam will be taken on the eve of the Apocalypse, which leaves the students with no choice but to work "until the same night when the world is supposed to end," Restall said.
The courses became quite popular.
"Scattle in two hours," Restall said of his high-educated student course, which was filled with 35 students.
We received e-mail messages for weeks and weeks before semester kick-start from people who asked if there's still going to take place.
The students, for their part, claim that the course is one of the most interesting.
"I'm fascinated to see what people do to console themselves," said Bridgid Robinson, a 23-year-old career student in Religion and Sociology in Haddonfield, New Jersey at Rutgers-Camden.
The apocalyptic, secular or religious mentality is only a matter of consolation or the absence thereof.
Will Wekesa, a 25-year-old psychology and nursing career student, claimed to have watched all the apocalyptic films.
"I've never listened about a class that could teach that," he said.
I'm enjoying it.
But no student interviewed - let alone a teacher - said he believed in the expiry date of December 21.
"Our first project was about Mayan prophecy and in a certain way we discredit it," said Julie Zeglen, a 21-year-old student at Temple and from West Chester.
The Mayans have never predicted the end of the world; it's just a key point on the calendar, Restall said.
But he pointed out that there is an apocalyptic anxiety in Western culture, dating back several centuries, when people react to the changes around them by predicting the end of the world.
The Internet has caused a splash of these speculations.
"On the other sides people don't think about this," he said.
Especially in the English-speaking world.
Joseph Dougherty, a professor of religion at La Salle University who teaches courses in the Philippines this year, quickly responded to the question of whether he knew about any course on the "end of the world" there.
"The Philippines does not participate at the end of the world," he wrote, hinting at an exception to a higher authority.
We've got a pardon from the pope.
Restall noted that over the years there has been talk of many days of the final trial, and said that if nothing happens on December 21, "People will begin to think immediately about the next date" or philosophize that 21 December is the beginning of a seven-year period after which the world will end.
Students and faculties are taking the date lightly.
A number of people said they plan to go "end-of-the-world" parties.
"Maybe I'll call a few friends to laugh together," said Samira Ford, a 20-year-old communications student.
